</node>
<node id="Queue-Based Load Leveling">
  <data key="d0">Queue-Based Load Leveling</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A design pattern that introduces a buffer between consumers and requested resources to ensure consistent load levels through queued processing.&lt;SEP&gt;Queue-Based Load Leveling buffers incoming requests in a queue to provide resilience against demand spikes.&lt;SEP&gt;Queue-Based Load Leveling buffers incoming requests in a queue, allowing controlled processing pace independent of intake rate.&lt;SEP&gt;Queue-based load leveling is a pattern that partitions workload across multiple queues to distribute processing load for better scalability and parallel processing.&lt;SEP&gt;Queue-Based Load Leveling pattern buffers incoming requests in a queue to reduce the need to overprovision resources for peak load.&lt;SEP&gt;This pattern buffers incoming requests in a queue to smooth workload and reduce overprovisioning.&lt;SEP&gt;Queue-Based Load Leveling is a code optimization design pattern applied within Azure Databricks environments.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-36d66c785cc9b10b4cf377888b6bd59f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-686a674e18aea8177f756b4cd627bcd9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d81a147434c35f3d6af3a65d96c1c22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e4f212602314ae8aa68b2a4631872d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-55fa8a30517c9b1ec527c6f5d09a0fc9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-563595f22347b72716cb226e6b3627c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ca9e9ecafaa14f1c2e106f58a475e4c</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/mission-critical/mission-critical-application-design.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/reliability/design-patterns.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/performance-efficiency/design-patterns.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/performance-efficiency/scale-partition.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/cost-optimization/design-patterns.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/cost-optimization/optimize-scaling-costs.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-databricks.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777000959</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Circuit Breaker">
  <data key="d0">Circuit Breaker</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A design pattern used to handle non-transient failures and improve application resiliency.&lt;SEP&gt;A design pattern that provides stability by waiting for recovery or quickly rejecting requests rather than blocking while waiting for unavailable services.&lt;SEP&gt;A design pattern that prevents continuous requests to malfunctioning dependencies, avoiding excessive resource utilization.&lt;SEP&gt;Circuit Breaker is a pattern that allows a service to recover by stopping retry attempts after a threshold, preventing endless retries.&lt;SEP&gt;A pattern related to retry that monitors service availability and resumes normal operations when the service recovers, with fallback options during downtime.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-36d66c785cc9b10b4cf377888b6bd59f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ed4331e930dc66a90d716e75ccb847e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f353c01b035545d08e4ab51fe6daecad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06ce09d2bcc53388fe6e985bfc862f65&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af17a604206f8056ed34d52736aca636</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/mission-critical/mission-critical-application-design.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/performance-efficiency/design-patterns.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/design-guides/handle-transient-faults.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999211</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Bulkhead">
  <data key="d0">Bulkhead</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A design pattern that partitions service instances into groups to isolate failures and sustain service functionality.&lt;SEP&gt;A pattern introducing segmentation between components to isolate blast radius of malfunctions and contain security incidents.&lt;SEP&gt;A design pattern introducing segmentation between components to isolate malfunctions and enable individual scalability per bulkhead.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-36d66c785cc9b10b4cf377888b6bd59f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-90dd8914d5ed47ea7dd3fc98eec8c06a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f353c01b035545d08e4ab51fe6daecad</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/mission-critical/mission-critical-application-design.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/security/design-patterns.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/performance-efficiency/design-patterns.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776997053</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Saga">
  <data key="d0">Saga</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A design pattern that manages data consistency across microservices with independent datastores through defined event or message channels and compensating transactions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-36d66c785cc9b10b4cf377888b6bd59f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/mission-critical/mission-critical-application-design.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776979323</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Health Endpoint Monitoring">
  <data key="d0">Health Endpoint Monitoring</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A design pattern that implements functional checks accessible through exposed endpoints to inform application health.&lt;SEP&gt;Health Endpoint Monitoring monitors system health via a dedicated endpoint for alerting, dashboarding, and self-healing remediation.&lt;SEP&gt;Health Endpoint Monitoring is a pattern involving health probes or check functions run regularly from outside an application to monitor its health.&lt;SEP&gt;Health Endpoint Monitoring exposes endpoints to monitor system health, enabling load balancing by routing traffic to verified healthy nodes.&lt;SEP&gt;A design pattern that monitors system health by exposing specifically designed endpoints to help triage issues.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-36d66c785cc9b10b4cf377888b6bd59f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-686a674e18aea8177f756b4cd627bcd9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a7fdbb7d6c79a6437952833a6e0ac9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d81a147434c35f3d6af3a65d96c1c22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3b0773a066490ceb376c40ec81b1c605</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/mission-critical/mission-critical-application-design.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/reliability/design-patterns.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/reliability/monitoring-alerting-strategy.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/performance-efficiency/design-patterns.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/operational-excellence/design-patterns.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776997556</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Retry">
  <data key="d0">Retry</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A design pattern that handles transient failures by canceling, retrying immediately, or retrying after a delay with back-off strategy.&lt;SEP&gt;Retry addresses transient or intermittent failures by retrying operations in a controlled way to improve workload resilience.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-36d66c785cc9b10b4cf377888b6bd59f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-686a674e18aea8177f756b4cd627bcd9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/mission-critical/mission-critical-application-design.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/reliability/design-patterns.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776995589</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Throttling">
  <data key="d0">Throttling</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Throttling is a design pattern that imposes limits on the rate or throughput of incoming requests to a resource or component. Its primary purposes include preventing resource exhaustion, supporting graceful degradation under heavy load, and mitigating congestion. Throttling controls resource consumption by deferring lower-priority operations when load thresholds are reached, helping avoid noisy neighbor scenarios and protecting against automated abuse.

By providing clear upper bounds on utilization, the Throttling pattern also informs resource sizing and cost modeling, enabling organizations to plan capacity more effectively.&lt;SEP&gt;Practice of limiting the rate of requests to a service or resource to protect it from overload.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-36d66c785cc9b10b4cf377888b6bd59f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-686a674e18aea8177f756b4cd627bcd9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fb9874b866ddeb7aca7860d08283d5b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-90dd8914d5ed47ea7dd3fc98eec8c06a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ec74bc9a6ceebb7c4ce58c9f06c2063c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d81a147434c35f3d6af3a65d96c1c22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-55fa8a30517c9b1ec527c6f5d09a0fc9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-563595f22347b72716cb226e6b3627c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c0150f1d7a68414d68f2d36fee4a0389</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/mission-critical/mission-critical-application-design.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/reliability/design-patterns.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/security/design-patterns.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/performance-efficiency/design-patterns.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/cost-optimization/design-patterns.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/cost-optimization/optimize-scaling-costs.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/design-guides/handle-transient-faults.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999209</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Polly">
  <data key="d0">Polly</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A standardized C# package for implementing resiliency patterns.&lt;SEP&gt;A resilience library used for retry handling with Azure SQL Database via ADO.NET, supporting declarative and programmatic configuration.&lt;SEP&gt;Polly is a library for transient fault handling that supports declarative and programmatic retry policies.&lt;SEP&gt;Polly is a .NET resilience library used to implement circuit breaker patterns.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-36d66c785cc9b10b4cf377888b6bd59f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af17a604206f8056ed34d52736aca636&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6fbfe826fe19c233eb2d1b279f7950e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b6dcb04088b404ffb7108762ce20034</data>
