<node id="Azure CNI">
  <data key="d0">Azure CNI</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Azure CNI (Azure Container Networking Interface) is a network plugin that integrates AKS clusters within virtual networks by assigning IP addresses from the same virtual network to nodes, pods, and services. It supports a richer feature set compared to alternatives and enables Azure or Calico network policies for traffic control within AKS clusters. Azure CNI is robust, scalable, and recommended for most AKS scenarios. It is required for specific scenarios such as Windows-based node pools and Kubernetes network policies.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b7e299b337a793955172863127084bd5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5321cfe4fbc08d735ac5eac02e84e4d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c136188a7c5b35c34dacbc95825b4191&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-defd991d185ed0d546fc2887007b9160&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da6c79bd6c72543235e9a779851e5641</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/mission-critical/mission-critical-application-platform.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/mission-critical/mission-critical-networking-connectivity.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-kubernetes-service.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001866</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Azure App Service">
  <data key="d0">Azure App Service</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Azure App Service is a Microsoft Azure Platform-as-a-Service (PaaS) product that provides a fully managed, low-friction container platform for building, deploying, hosting, and scaling web applications, APIs, and application workloads. It serves as an alternative to both Azure Kubernetes Service (AKS) and traditional IIS server setups, supporting multiple web apps and APIs on a single App Service plan. It is also a hosting platform option for deploying models and provides the underlying compute infrastructure for Azure Functions deployments.

Azure App Service offers extensive built-in capabilities including deployment slots that enable deploying live apps with their own host names, supporting gradual rollouts by swapping between slots after deployment. It provides Health Checks for scale-out operations, automatic scaling, integration with Azure Monitor Autoscale, and disaster recovery and backup features. The service supports zone-redundant deployments across availability zones for high availability of consumer applications, FTP deployments, remote debugging via Kudu, and profiling, memory dump collection, analysis, and autohealing features for .NET and Java apps. IPv6 support considerations also apply to its Web Apps feature.

The service operates as a multitenant environment where each instance has a limited number of outbound ports available and can be configured to run multiple instances. It is referenced for troubleshooting intermittent outbound connection errors using connection pooling and as an example for disabling unused ports and protocols during network hardening practices. It can be deployed via virtual network injection and integrates with other Azure services.

Azure App Service includes the Web Apps feature for hosting web application deployments and the WebJob feature for background task execution. It is recommended for low-latency scenarios when hosting web APIs. In practical applications, it is used to host e-commerce websites, as demonstrated in FMA examples with zone-redundant configurations.&lt;SEP&gt;Azure App Service is a PaaS compute platform designed for hosting web applications.&lt;SEP&gt;Azure App Service is a PaaS service that allows direct integration with other PaaS services without deploying virtual networks.</data>
