<node id="Failure Mode Analysis">
  <data key="d0">Failure Mode Analysis</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Failure Mode Analysis (FMA) is a systematic, iterative method for proactively identifying potential points of failure within a workload, its associated flows, and dependencies. It involves assessing the likelihood and severity of each failure mode, determining risk, impact, and breadth of impact, and identifying single points of failure. FMA includes introducing faults into applications and infrastructure to assess redundancy mechanisms.

The goal of FMA is to plan mitigation strategies—including self-healing, disaster recovery, and other reactive and preventative actions—that minimize the widespread effect of failures. FMA is used to define realistic recovery targets such as RTO, RPO, MTTR, and MTBF as part of reliability planning, helping workloads recover gracefully within defined recovery objectives. It also serves to define playbooks or response processes for specific incident types.

As an architecture strategy for reliability, FMA helps distinguish failures from errors, where failures are unexpected events requiring intervention, and recommends separate handling of read and write failures. It is a process for prioritizing faults and developing mitigation strategies, documented and updated during testing. FMA can be applied across various domains, including database failure scenarios paired with targeted mitigation and recovery approaches, messaging service resilience, and infrastructure components such as VM interactions with network and storage. It can also identify potential scenarios when specific components, such as Application Insights, might fail or become unreachable. FMA minimizes points of failure by considering internal dependencies and potential failure scenarios, and is documented across Azure infrastructure, providing structured guidance for identifying potential failure modes and their effects on workloads.&lt;SEP&gt;Failure mode analysis is a method for identifying single points of failure in network connections to mitigate reliability risks.&lt;SEP&gt;Failure mode analysis is a method of regularly analyzing potential failure modes in system components to build resilience.&lt;SEP&gt;Failure mode analysis (FMA) is a process to prepare for and help prevent future outages by identifying potential failure points.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c97a1db66c7ecde12774487f7afb1593&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-efa918a51cded76ba3c94dc0ae8e7139&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21f771a609846ef0b7b2580d4b2e3191&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-12a80a8926c38685348cb6badf1d2485&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-86643535bbddea59b9f2b171ec6790db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-45aecf85df05c73b4035e26a2cb04459&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b48e6023a56c54a7dd2d0d74d805223e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c4dc888d546b77a45fccbd5a3b4695b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a61083a07976793cf6cee9da2a51bdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0075254306371deed306288e9b95eee2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9b99a62e53e59edcc42c5c691f68045&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2ed64d4db4b1d8cf5e3ba5c43f2faf7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3fb455e5de9f1067bb91e069db8c9a2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40e4a31ea43efb7a100f1df033cb0e46&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20168b92f579ebd286ed6e8413a80537&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-973e5b950ce99287babd4d9e8e310ec0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea4e1326f308873031b3e530e46d8478&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37afa41294d82e5e5fb34db4fe81fec3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e409982ea62543ca5f8cfba60ee3111d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-987318a54e0439c65ea9427adb8524a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5cc325eb282741cd4c48968319d834f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba915883bba414ecf1ca82b7ab6c12cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e33a34bfd8e4ddf2903635a31b614a99&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7cffe100ebb087468498365a5360ea32&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9532ce6b51cab52bfc6f5e88ba579fc8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-103d1a840787f3b63521ec34c09294d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-42b3b849ac5683c426e666e8f3f3f48e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03df2eaa58b6c9bb42baf709c977fdda&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7edb1f343516427f2303f45fae771d0c</data>
