<node id="Reliability">
  <data key="d0">Reliability</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Reliability is one of the five pillars of the Azure Well-Architected Framework, focused on ensuring workloads can avoid and recover from malfunctions while consistently meeting service-level objectives, resiliency targets, and recovery goals. It provides continued functionality by building resilience and enabling fast recovery from failures, and is the primary focus for mission-critical workloads, with its own design principles, checklist, and associated tradeoffs.

Key principles of Reliability include maximizing availability through active/active design, fault isolation, health monitoring, automation, self-healing, and complexity avoidance. It emphasizes replication, redundancy, simplicity, and limiting the blast radius of malfunctions, while minimizing points of failure, reducing critical dependencies, and avoiding destabilizing activities. Reliability involves designing redundant, fault-tolerant patterns to ensure applications remain responsive and recover quickly from failures, and covers scaling strategies and disaster recovery strategies.

Reliability concerns are central to Azure deployment approaches, ranging from low in locally redundant configurations to high or very high in zone-redundant and multi-region deployments. Architectural patterns collectively aim to improve Reliability through resilience, failover, and fault mitigation strategies using geographically distributed workloads. Reliability is also a key aspect of SaaS solutions, ensuring consistent performance and availability for customers, and applies to AI workloads to maintain high availability against failures.

Reliability requires tradeoffs with other pillars, including cost implications from deploying extra components, transferring more data, and using resilient SKUs. It is also impacted by operational factors such as host pool golden image updates and platform monitoring. Achieving optimal Reliability requires careful balancing of design decisions to ensure robust infrastructure, uninterrupted user experience, and system resilience.&lt;SEP&gt;Reliability is a pillar focused on platform resiliency, high availability, and protecting critical business data.&lt;SEP&gt;Reliability is a pillar of the Well-Architected Framework where designing reliable workloads can reduce carbon and electricity footprint.&lt;SEP&gt;Reliability is a design principle focused on high availability and resiliency of SAP applications.&lt;SEP&gt;Reliability is a pillar focused on resiliency (withstanding faults) and recoverability (restoring normal operations after disruption) for SAP workloads.&lt;SEP&gt;A design pillar focusing on planning for failures, state preservation, and recovery in HPC environments.</data>
