<node id="Operational Excellence">
  <data key="d0">Operational Excellence</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Operational Excellence is one of the five pillars of the Azure Well-Architected Framework, focused on building reliable, repeatable, and safe operations through DevOps practices, automation, and observability. It encompasses formalized development practices, loosely coupled components, automated build and release processes, infrastructure as code, and standardized workflows that ensure workload quality and team cohesion. Key areas include development practices, observability, release management, safe deployment, configuration, failover, maintenance practices, incident response, testing, and deployment processes.

Operational Excellence represents a continuous improvement journey focused on streamlining practices across workload design, implementation, and support. It is rooted in a culture of blamelessness, respect, and continuous improvement, requiring architectures to be serviceable and observable while prioritizing simplicity and manageable operations. The pillar advocates using well-known design patterns to standardize workload design and recommends maintaining documentation repositories for procedures and topologies.

The pillar establishes clear team standards around responsibility, accountability, and customer outcomes, emphasizing SDLC rigor. It directly impacts application availability by addressing how a workload responds to operational issues and serves the long-term best interests of both the workload and the team. Monitoring is an Operational Excellence approach applied across all pillars of the framework, and safe deployment practices intersect with reliability at Level 4. Operational Excellence principles can transition from the individual workload level to centralized operations at the organizational level at Level 5.

Operational Excellence also serves as a design methodology and impact category focused on delivering capabilities efficiently throughout the development lifecycle, involving repeatable processes, monitoring, governance, and disaster recovery planning. It encompasses shared responsibility, consistent processes, safe change rollouts, and scalable SaaS operations. As a design area impact category, it is associated with tracking dependencies, automation, defining roles and processes, and is impacted by monitoring, awareness of new developments, limit thresholds, and environment management.

The pillar provides operational guidance for specific deployment scenarios, including Azure Local deployments, Azure Virtual Desktop, and Azure VMware Solution. In Azure Virtual Desktop, it is associated with the App Attach solution, shared responsibilities, host pool image updates, and environment management. For Azure VMware Solution, it involves fully utilizing platform capabilities and adhering to best practices to secure, optimize, and scale workloads. Application dashboards contribute to operational excellence as an impact area. The pillar carries its own set of design principles, checklists, tradeoffs when balanced against other pillars, and consolidated guidance including updated safe deployment practices, providing strategic direction for achieving operational requirements and optimizing processes across workload configurations.&lt;SEP&gt;Operational excellence is a pillar of the Well-Architected Framework where effective response to operational issues can reduce carbon emissions.&lt;SEP&gt;Operational excellence is a pillar focused on creating efficient processes to support SAP workloads through assessments, monitoring, and automation.&lt;SEP&gt;Operational excellence in HPC environments requires balancing user autonomy with system reliability through automation, visibility, and repeatability.</data>
