<node id="Security">
  <data key="d0">Security</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Security is one of the five mission-critical design principles and workload quality pillars within the Azure Well-Architected Framework, focused on providing confidentiality, integrity, and availability assurances for workload systems and user data. It addresses the protection of workloads against threats intended to directly or indirectly compromise their reliability, requiring controls for both software development and operational components.

Security covers a broad range of areas including threat modeling, endpoint protection, code vulnerability protection, encryption, least privilege automation, access controls, identity and access management (IAM), logging, testing, compliance, governance, Zero Trust principles, credential management, and ongoing threat assessment. It prioritizes defense in depth, reduced surface area, active security controls, strong segmentation, and current software patching to minimize attack vectors. Security also addresses how a workload mitigates specific threats such as DDoS attacks, insider risk, data loss, and vulnerabilities across operating systems, applications, networking, and data layers.

As a pillar of the Azure Well-Architected Framework, Security applies regardless of deployment design; Azure applies the same security rigor to every region and availability zone. The Security pillar has its own set of design principles, checklists, and associated tradeoffs that must be balanced against other architectural priorities.

Beyond general workload protection, Security serves as a design pillar focused on protecting data integrity, design integrity, and user privacy in AI solutions. It is also a design area for implementing and designing more efficient security solutions on Azure, where considerations such as auto-scaling of security appliances can impact the environment. Security is additionally noted as a design consideration that follows sustainability review as a next step in the architectural planning process.&lt;SEP&gt;Security is a pillar covering identity, access, input validation, data sovereignty, and encryption to protect critical SAP business data.&lt;SEP&gt;A design pillar addressing data protection, network boundaries, identity governance, and compliance in HPC environments.</data>
