<node id="Cost Optimization">
  <data key="d0">Cost Optimization</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Cost Optimization is one of the five pillars of the Azure Well-Architected Framework, focused on maximizing return on investment while minimizing waste, unnecessary expenditure, and underutilized resources. It encompasses resource costs, network traffic charges, and ongoing management costs, with expenses varying from low cost for locally redundant deployments to high cost for multi-region deployments.

As a continuous practice, Cost Optimization emphasizes driving down resource demand to enable the use of cheaper SKUs, fewer instances, and reduced consumption. It involves right-sizing investment as workloads evolve, monitoring ROI trends, evaluating the value of deployed solutions, avoiding over-provisioning, consolidating resources and responsibilities, increasing density, reducing component diversity, minimizing tooling sprawl, and pursuing vendor consolidation. It also focuses on aligning resource usage with billing increments, leveraging favorable billing models, evaluating discounts, and making build-versus-buy decisions. Strategies such as scaling limits, budget management, workload refactoring, governance policies, release gates, infrastructure as code, and cost alerts are central to the practice. Specific techniques include right-sizing VMs, reserved instances, and scaling plans.

Cost Optimization focuses on detecting spend patterns, prioritizing investments in critical areas, and optimizing to meet organizational budgets and business requirements. Practical activities include reviewing charges, analyzing usage data, identifying underused resources, setting limits, configuring alerts, changing scaling dimensions, and continuously monitoring resource utilization through tools such as Log Analytics. In the context of scaling, it specifically addresses removing inefficiencies in workload scaling to reduce costs while meeting nonfunctional requirements.

Cost Optimization seeks to maximize the value produced by a workload's resources without exceeding performance targets, all within financial constraints. It requires balancing value, cost, and risk across production and preproduction environments, including balancing costs with performance in database and workload management and right-sizing GPU resources. The practice demands a balance between automation and manual review, incorporating skills development, financial communication, efficient processes, skilled personnel, proper tooling, and resource management. It is fundamentally about understanding cost-revenue relationships and making informed spending decisions.

The pillar prioritizes long-term financial responsibility through continuous monitoring, repeatable processes, and balancing actual costs versus perceived value. It has its own design principles, checklist, and tradeoffs, covering budgeting, rate optimization, dynamic scaling, governance, and cost guardrails. Cost Optimization requires careful consideration of tradeoffs with other pillars such as reliability and is the most common trade-off when making performance improvements. Additionally, Cost Optimization serves as the initial step in making workloads more sustainable, as reducing unnecessary spending also lowers excessive carbon emissions from cloud workloads, acting as a proxy for emissions reduction.&lt;SEP&gt;Cost optimization aims to lower total cost of ownership while maintaining capabilities and maximizing ROI.&lt;SEP&gt;Cost optimization is a key goal for SAP workloads on Azure, achieved through planning, monitoring, and resizing VMs.&lt;SEP&gt;Cost optimization is a pillar focused on meeting performance expectations while reducing the total cost of ownership for SAP workloads.&lt;SEP&gt;A design pillar focused on balancing spend with performance and reliability through elastic scaling and storage management.&lt;SEP&gt;Cost optimization is a practice for balancing cloud HPC spend with performance and reliability requirements through elastic scaling control.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f86c34d935d91c0feba2784fca188c36&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3f13da121cb986a81d213029e9150d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d1a3bf99f17f7553d47f5ea8122843f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9afb547f72466f2ff769350152ae4ee5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e2cc133f2276ccf7c79c3147bf59683&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3c503260b531691857650e03a6b6b239&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd18a4f6bd04d93d17db01be0273b02f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b284d3b10b64577458ddc2798074113b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31da0f57b1bbfcf80aeba441d56d15f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e9454754aa8d04d2fcd10f9b4227d89&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-475405476dbc7b00e7119497a7834c18&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dcd6623608bb76388ae9e58c2350270b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4024f3337155e70539ba954cfd01caab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21e41edd72b9157b512d1a210db4941b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84ce425f3646f4ada6cca51a61f53897&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0f75cb5e98d1eaef8d795bebeeb3d64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d1d75683e73385fab74d25019f702cd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1f88755bbf9fb155be427dfc8a8708cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ac69076e8ebd4d001c0267c2edca19ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f40a9cc5e9bacfbd14def915787060e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f4d7f07b48cc1c474bedf5d938c8ae09&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8d68f0cdc97e70397b1ec00eda7f2e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76c4bb7431f02f35a05848683404dfc6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0be076c15399d472d831cac4c767ef8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e0544ee58fb5345261d0d125500955a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76e6af9e7f0b10472ec2406ba2334d3b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-517ef83a1fe99178ace6be8c8c95c6c0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9896bbc28e252b9ce2f3f2bb74ad70f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-55fa8a30517c9b1ec527c6f5d09a0fc9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a848e075a72ddab31643b7cadb9103de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9274b98dae68e1fda563c0a186872af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d97489185a44dcbeac38d0a5a48e1097&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-47e7ebdf3ba76338a16386df7ecb89e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5fc827ca305a978ff51b6d10e1dd732&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0410a251086c78865c685a5005e7ecae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6554b8dac9d13f8d208f0bad6db30cf0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-198a3a5ea0d4ee6048ded7764b7c4702&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-86a2a48b425bd11c45a9970a1c6d7737&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9780793a52d779d72d7dfc08b3276f92&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4285f3b1d912059b039db58435cd461a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a5d862a78e37f61b