<node id="Client Side Routing Logic">
  <data key="d0">Client Side Routing Logic</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Client-side logic that handles failover scenarios when the primary global routing technology fails, routing to redundant technology.&lt;SEP&gt;Client-side logic that handles failover scenarios when the primary global routing technology fails, routing to redundant technology.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-38a9b32aa690001ea5387c320074295c</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/mission-critical/mission-critical-networking-connectivity.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776983287</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Session Affinity">
  <data key="d0">Session Affinity</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Session Affinity, also known as session stickiness, is a mechanism that routes requests from the same client or source to the same backend server or origin. It typically uses client-side cookies to ensure that subsequent requests are directed to the same backend that handled the first request, maintaining state continuity across a user's session.

While Session Affinity can be useful for maintaining session state, it has several drawbacks. It ties requests to specific backends, which can negatively impact traffic distribution balance and cause overloading on a single origin server. It also limits scaling ability and introduces single points of failure. For these reasons, Session Affinity is not recommended for stateless applications or from a reliability perspective, as it reduces load balancing flexibility.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-38a9b32aa690001ea5387c320074295c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ce015784fd1d8129dac8c9bbf32bf565&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c387aff770332292e0a1e78af18b8fbe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5658d6f41065712f87008266f41103c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a007dbfba3cef886b2cc00b13f46310&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-24f878ef77f1b4e5dc16c41419b23ea5</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/mission-critical/mission-critical-networking-connectivity.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/reliability/scaling.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-front-door.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001687</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="TLS">
  <data key="d0">TLS</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">TLS, or Transport Layer Security, is the industry standard encryption protocol for securing network connections by encrypting and authenticating data in transit between clients and servers. It ensures confidentiality and integrity of data exchange across networks, standardizing secure communication for protocols such as HTTPS. TLS 1.2 is widely cited as the recommended minimum version for secure client-server communication, while TLS 1.3 is recognized as the latest and most secure version.

In Azure environments, TLS is managed via services such as Azure Front Door's managed certificate service and Application Gateway, providing end-to-end encryption and secure connections. TLS handles encrypted connections from TLS offloading points to application backends and supports traffic isolation in classified data flows for sensitive environments. As part of network transformation, TLS can be turned off and reestablished at different hops with more rigorously managed certificates to meet evolving security requirements.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-38a9b32aa690001ea5387c320074295c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-adc31ff972bb32047bd652b7c4ab8132&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8bfa9de462a82f93657efb2d63f7e3fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e507b943962b0d36b3563fef00c4090&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b663f0176e526e5a609a0de282882878&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4a6f56b52ae33dfac75b83ed7575dc1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fef76463ffa39e57a8a833790934275c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-36dd3a8f5a535bef38f7d594298d02cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0824c72199637e43cfbbf3653d45df65&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c7409b2f79632903a9d9de4c67055d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c0d38621b3ffd595eb404a49344659d5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8a88ba4349c5c5150df583b3f68ce96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f7d57d3c8e9b437c3bf033faee9829a9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/mission-critical/mission-critical-networking-connectivity.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/security/data-classification.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/security/encryption.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/security/maturity-model.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/security/networking.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-api-management.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-application-gateway.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-files.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-sql-database.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777002638</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="X-Azure-FDID">
  <data key="d0">X-Azure-FDID</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">X-Azure-FDID is a header used for filtering and validating that incoming requests flow through the configured Azure Front Door instance.&lt;SEP&gt;X-Azure-FDID is a header used for filtering and validating that incoming requests flow through the configured Azure Front Door instance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-38a9b32aa690001ea5387c320074295c</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/mission-critical/mission-critical-networking-connectivity.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776983287</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Regional Deployment Stamp">
  <data key="d0">Regional Deployment Stamp</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A regional deployment stamp is an independent deployment unit within a region that can be taken out of circulation if dependencies become unhealthy.&lt;SEP&gt;A regional deployment stamp is an independent deployment unit within a region that can be taken out of circulation if dependencies become unhealthy.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-38a9b32aa690001ea5387c320074295c</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/mission-critical/mission-critical-networking-connectivity.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776983287</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Virtual Network">
  <data key="d0">Virtual Network</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Virtual Network is a fundamental networking construct in Azure that enables private connectivity between resources. It provides a logical boundary for network isolation, where by design one virtual network cannot communicate with another unless intentionally connected through mechanisms such as peering. Virtual networks are used to segment and isolate workload components using subnets, supporting various network configurations.

Virtual networks enable organizations to move to private IP address spaces and minimize network line of sight, enhancing security. They can be used to control both outbound and inbound traffic for services such as App Service, enabling network isolation and security. When planning address spaces, they should be kept large enough to support future scaling needs.&lt;SEP&gt;Virtual networks are Azure networking resources used as boundaries for network segmentation between customers, supporting peering and isolation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4bdc71fab10e8d772205c8ac9acd6546&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-47016d300e650b08a3402c1a274475c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0824c72199637e43cfbbf3653d45df65&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96035ef5f5894d52718d074319b896c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-980c7a5d8e921b4d4d355fea3481711c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92525f29689a0dd8e7f5608aa6cf465c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c00db143f490c3d917ea5c2345f79ef2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-13a9dd5e331588bd7de29586bf88d3b4</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/mission-critical/mission-critical-networking-connectivity.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/security/networking.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/security/segmentation.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/app-service-web-apps.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/virtual-network.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/saas/networking.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777004595</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Azure Front Door Premium">
  <data key="d0">Azure Front Door Premium</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Azure Front Door Premium supports private endpoints, enabling internet traffic to flow directly onto Azure virtual networks without public IPs.&lt;SEP&gt;Azure Front Door Premium can route directly from edge nodes to private application endpoints.&lt;SEP&gt;Azure Front Door Premium supports private endpoints, enabling internet traffic to flow directly onto Azure virtual networks without public IPs.&lt;SEP&gt;Azure Front Door Premium can route directly from edge nodes to private application endpoints.&lt;SEP&gt;Azure Front Door Premium is the premium tier required for managed WAF rules and Private Link support.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4bdc71fab10e8d772205c8ac9acd6546&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ce015784fd1d8129dac8c9bbf32bf565&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a007dbfba3cef886b2cc00b13f46310</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/mission-critical/mission-critical-networking-connectivity.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-front-door.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001689</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Azure Bastion">
  <data key="d0">Azure Bastion</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Azure Bastion is a fully platform-managed PaaS service that provides secure RDP and SSH connectivity to Azure Virtual Machines directly from the Azure portal over TLS, without requiring public IP addresses on the VMs. By enabling browser-based connections, it eliminates the need to expose VMs to the public internet, significantly enhancing security.

Beyond VM access, Azure Bastion provides proxied private connections to access the data plane of Azure resources and perform management operations, including administrative tasks on Azure PaaS services over the Internet. This makes it a versatile tool for secure remote access and resource management within Azure environments.&lt;SEP&gt;Azure Bastion is a PaaS resource for secure access to virtual machines as an alternative to jump boxes.&lt;SEP&gt;Azure Bastion provides more secure connectivity to VMs for operational access.&lt;SEP&gt;Azure Bastion is a networking resource charged per instance that can be shared among customers.&lt;SEP&gt;Azure Bastion is a service that provides secure RDP access to session hosts without direct RDP port exposure.&lt;SEP&gt;Azure Bastion secures administrative access by preventing direct exposure of management ports.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4bdc71fab10e8d772205c8ac9acd6546&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c136188a7c5b35c34dacbc95825b4191&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9406ed2aa903d9c51a1e6e1d68aee2dc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0151994e0d96a2045a761ce543e6d2a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-36dd3a8f5a535bef38f7d594298d02cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9641143ef161c630f4c2735baa058dc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40e0fe247c84a41199e470c86f0e983f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0be076c15399d472d831cac4c767ef8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb860ff346987514c80ee21375c28eec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-13a9dd5e331588bd7de29586bf88d3b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c7f6d6f720349d226f2c7824d044fde&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-778fbb08fc2452f88b89df8ede476434</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/mission-critical/mission-critical-networking-connectivity.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/mission-critical/mission-critical-security.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/security/networking.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/cost-optimization/collect-review-cost-data.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/virtual-machines.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/saas/networking.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/azure-virtual-desktop/security.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/hpc/hpc-design-principles.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777006203</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Virtual Private Network">
  <data key="d0">Virtual Private Network</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">VPN provides aggregated bandwidth up to 10 Gbps in hub-and-spoke networks and up to 20 Gbps in Azure Virtual WAN.&lt;SEP&gt;VPN provides aggregated bandwidth up to 10 Gbps in hub-and-spoke networks and up to 20 Gbps in Azure Virtual WAN.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4bdc71fab10e8d772205c8ac9acd6546</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/mission-critical/mission-critical-networking-connectivity.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776983283</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Azure Virtual WAN">
  <data key="d0">Azure Virtual WAN</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Azure Virtual WAN is a Microsoft Azure networking service that provides unified connectivity, security, and routing through a hub-and-spoke architecture for global transit networks. It supports VPN connectivity and organizes cloud infrastructure into logical patterns for redundancy.

The service offers managed secured hubs with routing intent and policies for inter-hub and branch-to-branch communications. Azure Virtual WAN includes architectural best practices aligned with the Well-Architected Framework pillars, along with guidance on cost modeling, hub capacity planning, and Infrastructure as Code (IaC) automation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4bdc71fab10e8d772205c8ac9acd6546&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0b609a802fa10d995d38905fc231483&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20168b92f579ebd286ed6e8413a80537&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea4e1326f308873031b3e530e46d8478&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-11229197c05f5be9b778a1918888ee7a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7cffe100ebb087468498365a5360ea32</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/mission-critical/mission-critical-networking-connectivity.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/redundancy.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/reliability/redundancy.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/whats-new.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-firewall.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-virtual-wan.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777002965</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Azure Firewall">
  <data key="d0">Azure Firewall</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Azure Firewall is a cloud-native, fully stateful, managed Platform-as-a-Service (PaaS) network security service in Microsoft Azure that provides centralized firewall protection for Azure workloads. It filters traffic between cloud resources, the internet, and on-premises networks, supporting both east-west and north-south traffic inspection. It operates as a network edge firewall, typically deployed in centralized hub network models for egress control, requiring a dedicated subnet and available as either a dedicated or centrally shared resource across customers.

Azure Firewall offers layer 3 through layer 7 controls, including NAT, network, and application rules, FQDN filtering (including SQL FQDNs), DNAT with FQDN filtering, IDPS/IDS capabilities, DNS proxy, threat intelligence, and optional TLS inspection/termination for HTTP/HTTPS traffic. It analyzes traffic and blocks connections to untrusted destinations, enabling organizations to filter, restrict, and inspect both outbound and inbound traffic from resources such as Virtual Machines and container environments. It also provides added security for routing traffic through internal load balancers for non-HTTP applications.

Azure Firewall is available in multiple SKUs—Basic, Standard, and Premium—each offering varying feature sets. Advanced capabilities include forced tunneling for egress traffic control, port exhaustion and SNAT exhaustion mitigation, explicit proxy configuration, hierarchical policy rules, packet capture, DNS flow trace logs, and data transformation before log storage. It provides Resource Health monitoring, customer-controlled maintenance schedules, change tracking, diagnostic logs, policy analytics, and network infrastructure automation, supporting scaling up to 20 instances for high availability. It is charged per instance and can be expensive, making centralized shared deployments a common cost optimization pattern.

Azure Firewall integrates with numerous Azure services and architectures. It works with Application Gateway for virtual network protection, Azure Databricks via virtual network injection, Azure Policy for governance, and Microsoft Sentinel via a dedicated data connector. It serves as an egress point for AKS cluster node pools, controlling cluster egress traffic and enforcing security policies. It is deployed in Azure Virtual WAN virtual hubs for centralized application-aware filtering, threat protection, and security inspection. Azure Firewall also provides east-west and north-south protection and filtering for Azure Virtual Desktop workloads, helping lock down and secure session host environments. It is used in multiregion load balancing architectures as a key component for traffic inspection, control, and network egress across various Azure networking scenarios.&lt;SEP&gt;Azure Firewall is a network security device deployed in Azure for monitoring and filtering traffic.&lt;SEP&gt;Azure Firewall is used to inspect Azure VMware Solution traffic to the internet and Azure for internet-facing workloads.&lt;SEP&gt;Azure Firewall is an Azure service used for intrusion detection and prevention in network traffic.&lt;SEP&gt;Azure Firewall protects internet-facing workloads by creating separation between endpoints.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4bdc71fab10e8d772205c8ac9acd6546&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c136188a7c5b35c34dacbc95825b4191&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fd5df507c6d7ae6d803dce8c99d84eb9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0151994e0d96a2045a761ce543e6d2a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96ee1cd59b7faad09ea96a1e39a65bcd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-080b0147971910915ed740faf5b69284&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-36dd3a8f5a535bef38f7d594298d02cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6aa7e37862e9b9978a85e7dde37bac4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4d3c29048704933cd594ead18773c494&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7f01a2d2b2e2dfa5053288e4cf9593f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d2431ac43eabe818a5350eafb1e23d38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6fcebbf5c193f6fa1d943e2af2fe05b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0be076c15399d472d831cac4c767ef8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e0544ee58fb5345261d0d125500955a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50bf24a002558d213cd02700a64f5c07&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ab2911837499c055b346ca8d6a1cac17&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df0cc69c8383501073ee148f940f5d11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a6442b7c1d4b778e4c37906350571fd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb68841440596ce96d4bd54899abbe7d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19bbc6ffc74eee4a356518df32b00c13&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c4aa034c37c80a21f6fbf6660a82591c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa5913e5b110a5e9169b6c4527dd3ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4b8f25dd2dc2bf1ed1488dc9092be0f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-11229197c05f5be9b778a1918888ee7a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-079bbb48956c67b51ccb9053f7fbe162&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eef9765311dfd798d022450d45e1c01d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4fa337fdf4c15ddf378bf74ac66c3ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea213cbc3ff82c4333ed2bb24ec62b5d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca3e496d3c1901ebb6e0f0add1d59b5b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4dd12866b1c0a9d8fe76b6447e59f118&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7918938c3b3fb0cddcc9e93b114d4a3c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc3e5ddb4441b86b0ef64d617182432b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4bffdea3a766285468bd7ae393d75002&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f7d57d3c8e9b437c3bf033faee9829a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-257827ee036f98e6274522ad2bfee550&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a8c2320d6f192d51ba7461cdb73ef6e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7cffe100ebb087468498365a5360ea32&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d73a470d503be7e3a2f2ef63b96ec22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de21122336bd114f1583106e238ec656&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-142487c37cb4da71ab0fecf01ad7e10b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22d333e97696a760d