  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="NAT Gateway">
  <data key="d0">NAT Gateway</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">NAT Gateway is an Azure service that abstracts outbound traffic flows and manages SNAT port inventory, but lacks built-in zone isolation.&lt;SEP&gt;NAT Gateway is an Azure service that abstracts outbound traffic flows and manages SNAT port inventory, but lacks built-in zone isolation.&lt;SEP&gt;Azure NAT Gateway supports reliable egress traffic at scale for clusters with many concurrent outbound connections.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fd5df507c6d7ae6d803dce8c99d84eb9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da6c79bd6c72543235e9a779851e5641</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/mission-critical/mission-critical-networking-connectivity.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-kubernetes-service.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001861</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Virtual WAN">
  <data key="d0">Virtual WAN</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Virtual WAN is an Azure networking service that provides hub-based connectivity infrastructure for wide-area network architectures. It supports hub-and-spoke network topologies with features including VPN gateways (such as S2S VPN), ExpressRoute gateways, hub routers, and comprehensive routing capabilities including routing intent and policies for traffic redirection.

Virtual WAN offers connectivity, security, routing, and monitoring capabilities, along with cost optimization and multiregion architecture support. It serves as a network topology option for deploying and managing Azure Firewall instances, and Azure Firewall Manager can centrally manage these instances within the Virtual WAN environment through secure hubs. Virtual WAN supports sharing Azure Firewall instances across hubs, though Azure DDoS Protection plan association via Firewall Manager does not apply to it.

The service has specific exceptions for forced tunneling and private data plane configurations. Performance indicators, capacity planning, and monitoring practices are defined for Virtual WAN to ensure optimal operation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fd5df507c6d7ae6d803dce8c99d84eb9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4b8f25dd2dc2bf1ed1488dc9092be0f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4fa337fdf4c15ddf378bf74ac66c3ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea213cbc3ff82c4333ed2bb24ec62b5d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca3e496d3c1901ebb6e0f0add1d59b5b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8650a6bc1260a7fa43030a811f587363&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-771b01fe7618e75e298642ca2a2cb0e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d73a470d503be7e3a2f2ef63b96ec22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de21122336bd114f1583106e238ec656&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f3f86dbbc0e68845a4cfbd563dcaafe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c74dac0959c495ccca3e91ce252cb225&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-142487c37cb4da71ab0fecf01ad7e10b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c60b5ebd31506f4de3e92091b80399f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22d333e97696a760da77cbcd035a0d29</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/mission-critical/mission-critical-networking-connectivity.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-firewall.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-virtual-wan.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777002977</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Firewall Manager">
  <data key="d0">Firewall Manager</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Firewall Manager is an Azure security management service that provides centralized deployment and management of Azure Firewall instances and their associated policies through global firewall policies. It enables administrators to configure security partner providers, create and associate Azure DDoS Protection plans with hub virtual networks, and manage firewall policies from a single point of control. Pricing for Firewall Manager is based on firewall associations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fd5df507c6d7ae6d803dce8c99d84eb9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4d3c29048704933cd594ead18773c494&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4b8f25dd2dc2bf1ed1488dc9092be0f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-079bbb48956c67b51ccb9053f7fbe162&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4fa337fdf4c15ddf378bf74ac66c3ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea213cbc3ff82c4333ed2bb24ec62b5d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca3e496d3c1901ebb6e0f0add1d59b5b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/mission-critical/mission-critical-networking-connectivity.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/security/segmentation.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-firewall.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001593</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Availability Zone">
  <data key="d0">Availability Zone</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An Availability Zone is a physically separate and isolated datacenter location within an Azure region, designed to provide high availability, fault isolation, and resiliency against outages. Each Availability Zone consists of one or more datacenters equipped with independent power, cooling, and networking infrastructure, ensuring that a failure in one zone does not affect others. Inter-zone latency within a region is guaranteed to be sub-2ms. Availability Zones are used for deploying resources with redundancy and resiliency considerations, enabling organizations to protect their applications and data from datacenter-level failures.&lt;SEP&gt;An availability zone is a deployment option for session hosts to improve reliability.&lt;SEP&gt;An availability zone is a deployment option for session hosts to protect them from failures in specific fault domains.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fd5df507c6d7ae6d803dce8c99d84eb9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-771f5e6a7f069d0317878849e102ccc7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba037f30d06665208af1b780fac7d88b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ac69076e8ebd4d001c0267c2edca19ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67203c92bdb0aa325b8ab2d546d9ef56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc27c33aba333fbae143b55b08a314fd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-161fa262de0d263c9842f3ec1f7a7795&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b00f2a93a89bf2a6f52f33a9c1e0f13</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/mission-critical/mission-critical-networking-connectivity.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/design-guides/regions-availability-zones.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/performance-efficiency/select-services.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/design-guides/regions-availability-zones.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/azure-virtual-desktop/operations.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777004983</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Private Link">
  <data key="d0">Private Link</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Private Link is an Azure networking feature that enables direct service-to-service communication using private endpoints within or across regions. It provides a way to give a PaaS instance a private IP address, making it unreachable over the public internet, thereby eliminating public internet exposure and ensuring that management traffic remains within the Azure backbone network.

Private Link enables private connectivity from Azure Front Door to supported origins, and is available only in the Premium tier of Azure Front Door for connecting to origins securely. It facilitates secure, private connectivity between Azure Front Door Premium and Azure PaaS services.

Additionally, Private Link limits communications between log sources and workspaces to private networking, providing network isolation for enhanced security.&lt;SEP&gt;Private link connections are used by Machine Learning workspaces for secure network connectivity.&lt;SEP&gt;Private Link enables private connectivity to resources, with cost benefits when used across regional virtual network peering.&lt;SEP&gt;Private Link is an Azure networking feature used to keep traffic on the Microsoft network for secure connectivity.&lt;SEP&gt;Private Link provides secure connectivity to Azure resources by ensuring traffic remains within a private network.&lt;SEP&gt;Private Link is an Azure networking feature that enhances security by keeping traffic on the Microsoft network and off the public internet, though it introduces extra costs.&lt;SEP&gt;Private Link enables secure private connectivity to Azure Virtual Desktop and related services, keeping traffic on the Microsoft network.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fd5df507c6d7ae6d803dce8c99d84eb9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40e0fe247c84a41199e470c86f0e983f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-32d43601fe6822fd1a5a934f2ed75224&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c9c22dcb398bdcbe4d02bc247b58ee8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a007dbfba3cef886b2cc00b13f46310&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-608026926818db1b277afce5e06e8ce8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ab1427ce079159adb889d9285c7e3c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e08b75063568eb6355420ca8b8527f53&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e3b1ef27485e0c9c13eb1505e8179870&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51059245c825ad997eabe5a0dc767a97&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4e1a7707a8a1d8d9f53bc71b18814f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4eb78b84d41322da30efb8dd70aafc73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37837339dd05d2a9f58b6dc734ebd967</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/mission-critical/mission-critical-networking-connectivity.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/security/networking.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-databricks.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-front-door.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-log-analytics.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-machine-learning.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/virtual-network.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/saas/networking.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/azure-virtual-desktop/networking.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777004939</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Virtual Network Peering">
  <data key="d0">Virtual Network Peering</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Virtual Network Peering is a networking method that connects two virtual networks, allowing resources in one network to access IP addresses in the other. It supports connections both within a region and across different regions, while keeping traffic on the Microsoft network.

Virtual Network Peering enables direct peering between virtual networks, bypassing hub routing and firewall processing. This direct connection reduces latency, lowers processing costs, and improves overall efficiency and performance.

Data transfer between peered virtual networks incurs inbound and outbound costs, but Virtual Network Peering efficiently moves data while reducing bandwidth consumption.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fd5df507c6d7ae6d803dce8c99d84eb9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8650a6bc1260a7fa43030a811f587363&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92525f29689a0dd8e7f5608aa6cf465c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e3b1ef27485e0c9c13eb1505e8179870&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c00db143f490c3d917ea5c2345f79ef2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51059245c825ad997eabe5a0dc767a97&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4e1a7707a8a1d8d9f53bc71b18814f3</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/mission-critical/mission-critical-networking-connectivity.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-virtual-wan.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/virtual-network.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/saas/networking.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777004591</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="SNAT Port Inventory">
  <data key="d0">SNAT Port Inventory</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">SNAT port inventory is a resource managed by NAT Gateway that can be pressured by large numbers of outgoing internet connections.&lt;SEP&gt;SNAT port inventory is a resource managed by NAT Gateway that can be pressured by large numbers of outgoing internet connections.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fd5df507c6d7ae6d803dce8c99d84eb9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/mission-critical/mission-critical-networking-connectivity.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776983282</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Azure Region">
  <data key="d0">Azure Region</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">An Azure region is a geographic area containing one or more Availability Zones and data centers.&lt;SEP&gt;An Azure region is a geographic perimeter containing a set of datacenters, with various configurations known as region architectures.&lt;SEP&gt;An Azure region is a geographic area containing one or more Availability Zones and data centers.&lt;SEP&gt;An Azure region is a geographic perimeter containing a set of datacenters, with various configurations known as region architectures.&lt;SEP&gt;An Azure region is a geographic area that can experience outages, requiring cross-region redundancy planning.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fd5df507c6d7ae6d803dce8c99d84eb9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba037f30d06665208af1b780fac7d88b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50815fe6bf25d27a5c4d85a90c7be6cd</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/mission-critical/mission-critical-networking-connectivity.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/design-guides/regions-availability-zones.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/design-guides/regions-availability-zones.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/azure-virtual-desktop/business-continuity.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777004753</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="NVA">
  <data key="d0">NVA</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">NVA (Network Virtual Appliance) is an equivalent alternative to Azure Firewall used in Azure landing zone deployments.&lt;SEP&gt;NVA (Network Virtual Appliance) is an equivalent alternative to Azure Firewall used in Azure landing zone deployments.&lt;SEP&gt;NVAs (Network Virtual Appliances) with native Virtual WAN hub integration provide better reliability and combined Microsoft and partner support.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fd5df507c6d7ae6d803dce8c99d84eb9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c60b5ebd31506f4de3e92091b80399f0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/mission-critical/mission-critical-networking-connectivity.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-virtual-wan.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777002990</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Traffic Manager">
  <data key="d0">Traffic Manager</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Traffic Manager is an Azure service used for non-HTTP/S routing scenarios as a replacement to Azure Front Door.&lt;SEP&gt;Traffic Manager is an Azure service used for non-HTTP/S routing scenarios as a replacement to Azure Front Door.&lt;SEP&gt;An Azure DNS-based traffic load balancer used for priority-based, latency-based, or weighted routing across regions.&lt;SEP&gt;Traffic Manager is an Azure service that uses DNS-based traffic routing, health probes, and failover mechanisms to direct traffic across multiple endpoints.&lt;SEP&gt;Traffic Manager is an Azure service for routing traffic, with profiles containing configuration settings for traffic routing.&lt;SEP&gt;Azure Traffic Manager is a DNS-based traffic load balancer that routes network traffic, supports failover, and provides traffic view and diagnostic capabilities.&lt;SEP&gt;Traffic Manager is an Azure DNS-based traffic load balancer that routes traffic across endpoints using various routing methods.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a6ee1b46c13062b2f84bc4bd8740f6c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-443aa85fac21ccf603c8d3f8c5b909ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-257827ee036f98e6274522ad2bfee550&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2230ce3d7cb1e253eb90d2592e57fab1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4889043b463029eef941a26e276c8840&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a8c2320d6f192d51ba7461cdb73ef6e1</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/mission-critical/mission-critical-networking-connectivity.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/design-guides/disaster-recovery.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-traffic-manager.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777002853</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Azure WAF">
  <data key="d0">Azure WAF</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Azure WAF provides out-of-the-box protection against top 10 OWASP vulnerabilities using managed rule sets, with support for custom rules.&lt;SEP&gt;Azure WAF provides out-of-the-box protection against top 10 OWASP vulnerabilities using managed rule sets, with support for custom rules.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a6ee1b46c13062b2f84bc4bd8740f6c3</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/mission-critical/mission-critical-networking-connectivity.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776983280</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Azure CDN">
  <data key="d0">Azure CDN</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Azure CDN can enable Azure WAF capabilities, currently in public preview.&lt;SEP&gt;Azure Content Delivery Network service for establishing full content delivery networks, more cost effective than Azure Front Door but with fewer advanced routing and WAF capabilities.&lt;SEP&gt;Azure CDN can enable Azure WAF capabilities, currently in public preview.&lt;SEP&gt;Azure Content Delivery Network service for establishing full content delivery networks, more cost effective than Azure Front Door but with fewer advanced routing and WAF capabilities.&lt;SEP&gt;Azure CDN supports file compression to improve performance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a6ee1b46c13062b2f84bc4bd8740f6c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-adc31ff972bb32047bd652b7c4ab8132&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a57afd24805552d7c2ea0ed4b6b7da2</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/mission-critical/mission-critical-networking-connectivity.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/sustainability/sustainability-storage.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777005746</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="TLS Offloading">
  <data key="d0">TLS Offloading</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">TLS Offloading is the practice of decrypting incoming TLS-encrypted traffic at the ingress point, requiring the private key of the TLS certificate.&lt;SEP&gt;TLS Offloading is the practice of decrypting incoming TLS-encrypted traffic at the ingress point, requiring the private key of the TLS certificate.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a6ee1b46c13062b2f84bc4bd8740f6c3</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/mission-critical/mission-critical-networking-connectivity.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776983280</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="OWASP">
  <data key="d0">OWASP</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">OWASP, the Open Worldwide Application Security Project (also known as the Open Web Application Security Project), is a security standards organization that identifies and documents common application security threats. It is best known for its Top 10 document, which lists the most critical web application security risks and attack categories.

OWASP provides a range of resources including source code analysis tools, dependency checks, secure coding practices, and the Top Ten security risks list. Its vulnerability standards are referenced as security requirements that should be addressed upfront in application development.

In the Azure ecosystem, OWASP's Top 10 attack types are used to frequently update Azure Web Application Firewall (WAF) default and managed rule sets. OWASP also defines attack types that require extra security measures when using Azure Traffic Manager.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a6ee1b46c13062b2f84bc4bd8740f6c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6aa7e37862e9b9978a85e7dde37bac4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d45ee5353204fd65637dc950ee85715&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-abf9fd57985f14c81b7d1b9d2924d1bc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a6442b7c1d4b778e4c37906350571fd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-608026926818db1b277afce5e06e8ce8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a76b7951c5b546531ebdfe1e8a4a70df</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/mission-critical/mission-critical-networking-connectivity.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/security/networking.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/security/secure-development-lifecycle.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-container-apps.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-front-door.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-traffic-manager.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777002861</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="DDoS Attack">
  <data key="d0">DDoS Attack</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A DDoS (Distributed Denial of Service) Attack is a type of malicious attack that attempts to render targeted resources unavailable by overwhelming them with illegitimate traffic, exhausting an application's resources and making it unavailable to legitimate users. It represents a failure mode for workloads and typically targets publicly reachable endpoints.

DDoS attacks can disrupt services across multiple network layers. Protection against L3/L4 (network/transport layer) attacks is managed by Microsoft, while L7 (application layer) attacks pose residual risk that requires additional mitigation strategies.&lt;SEP&gt;Distributed denial-of-service (DDoS) attacks are a common risk for internet-facing applications that overwhelm services with traffic.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a6ee1b46c13062b2f84bc4bd8740f6c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21f771a609846ef0b7b2580d4b2e3191&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-86643535bbddea59b9f2b171ec6790db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-47016d300e650b08a3402c1a274475c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6aa7e37862e9b9978a85e7dde37bac4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84edc61960c2c1e2ea0cf79280a5461f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/mission-critical/mission-critical-networking-connectivity.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/reliability/failure-mode-analysis.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/reliability/failure-mode-analysis.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/security/networking.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/saas/networking.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777004597</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Micro-Segmentation">
  <data key="d0">Micro-Segmentation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A network security design pattern used to isolate and secure individual application workloads based on a Zero Trust model.&lt;SEP&gt;A network security design pattern used to isolate and secure individual application workloads based on a Zero Trust model.&lt;SEP&gt;A method of creating separate segments within a private network by grouping parts of a workload and establishing secure communication paths.&lt;SEP&gt;Micro-segmentation uses security rules to restrict network communication between VMs in each segment.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-defd991d185ed0d546fc2887007b9160&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6503993c6e8852f14500c5e6630b88b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2956238f945f53b269eebcd0c33b1c0f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/mission-critical/mission-critical-networking-connectivity.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/security/segmentation.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/azure-vmware/security.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777005468</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Network Security Groups">
  <data key="d0">Network Security Groups</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Network Security Groups (NSGs) are Azure resources that function as Layer 3 and Layer 4 firewalls, filtering and controlling network traffic between resources in virtual networks and external networks. They are applied at the subnet or network interface (NIC) level, acting as perimeter traffic filters that use IP-based access control lists (ACLs) to secure ingress paths based on IP addresses, ports, and protocols. NSGs enable micro-segmentation of application traffic and enforcement of security rules on subnets.

NSGs are widely used across Azure services. They control traffic flow in Azure Databricks virtual network configurations, create micro-segmentation for Event Grid network boundaries, limit access for Azure NetApp Files, filter traffic to back-end servers, provide additional filtering for subnets containing Service Bus consumers and producers, and restrict traffic flow between subnets and node types in Service Fabric clusters. They also control traffic to and from Azure Cosmos DB resources, manage SQL Managed Instance subnet traffic (with unique assignment per subnet), provide subnet-level filtering in spoke networks, and support network-level segmentation for services such as App Service. NSGs serve as an alternative to Azure Firewall for intra-virtual network traffic control, restrict access to private endpoint IP addresses, manage access control in virtual network peering scenarios, and control access to databases as part of the Zero Trust security model's network segmentation strategy.&lt;SEP&gt;Network Security Groups (NSGs) are used to segment and isolate subnets and workloads by controlling traffic flow.&lt;SEP&gt;Network Security Groups (NSGs) are used to configure network rules based on least-privilege access principles.&lt;SEP&gt;Network security groups filter network traffic to and from Azure Virtual Desktop workloads using service tags and rules.&lt;SEP&gt;Network Security Groups filter network traffic at the source to eliminate unnecessary data transmission.&lt;SEP&gt;Azure feature used to filter network traffic at the source, reducing unnecessary data transmission.&lt;SEP&gt;NSGs filter network traffic to and from SAP workloads by defining allow/deny rules.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-defd991d185ed0d546fc2887007b9160&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9406ed2aa903d9c51a1e6e1d68aee2dc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0151994e0d96a2045a761ce543e6d2a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6aa7e37862e9b9978a85e7dde37bac4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40e0fe247c84a41199e470c86f0e983f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7f01a2d2b2e2dfa5053288e4cf9593f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e035fd4bdb926eb43402af9b5f4b298b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa5913e5b110a5e9169b6c4527dd3ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-935a5d6a7d3ef900c2c2a6bad10cdf79&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4dd12866b1c0a9d8fe76b6447e59f118&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4bffdea3a766285468bd7ae393d75002&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4057517613635840bc1340d6413748b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-653342d517c710c9f67df8e6155d05a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af193ca86913bf526ac2830012c0e345&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8cca5d0852b439b0c20120d19e126b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de21122336bd114f1583106e238ec656&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c4ef1aec0a62de4cc107952af4e1f676&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92a07b964260a4bef1cc690bb26c2030&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7cd6df2b9534dcc70f59a3fbb6607582&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-42b3b849ac5683c426e666e8f3f3f48e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4e1a7707a8a1d8d9f53bc71b18814f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4eb78b84d41322da30efb8dd70aafc73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37837339dd05d2a9f58b6dc734ebd967&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c7f6d6f720349d226f2c7824d044fde&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ca4d2bd438b809671ecf2d090095b06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-776c12fee2375faef068c91c8f0e42d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7b6ef60fdfefa17516a41d6cfc052155</data>
