  <data key="d5">1776996745</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Security Operation Center">
  <data key="d0">Security Operation Center</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The Security Operation Center (SOC) is a centralized function within an organization that employs specialized IT professionals to detect incidents in real time and address potential threats.&lt;SEP&gt;The Security Operation Center (SOC) is a centralized function within an organization that employs specialized IT professionals to detect incidents in real time and address potential threats.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-727509da0a6c0effe0339afa582c8da4</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/security/principles.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/security/principles.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776996680</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Least Privilege">
  <data key="d0">Least Privilege</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Least privilege is a security principle where access is granted only on a need-to-know basis, minimizing access permissions and exposure time.&lt;SEP&gt;Least privilege is a security principle that minimizes access based on privilege, scope, and time to limit potential damage.&lt;SEP&gt;Least privilege is the principle that an identity must not be allowed to do more than it needs to do.&lt;SEP&gt;Least privilege is a security principle where roles are granted with minimal permissions initially, adding more based on operational or data access needs.&lt;SEP&gt;Least privilege is a security principle where access is granted only on a need-to-know basis, minimizing access permissions and exposure time.&lt;SEP&gt;Least privilege is a security principle that minimizes access based on privilege, scope, and time to limit potential damage.&lt;SEP&gt;A security principle limiting agents to only the permissions needed to perform their tasks.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-727509da0a6c0effe0339afa582c8da4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3a879a1f2cc37839210f7fa1a38c26a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-660c07f6382831457c45f8649ff82cf8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ea57e7a575650ba4d31b8ba51cf8991&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc830fbd68e22812a74bf3c788ef10f4</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/security/principles.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/security/identity-access.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/security/principles.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/operational-excellence/maturity-model.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776997897</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Encryption">
  <data key="d0">Encryption</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Encryption is a method used to safeguard data at rest, in transit, and during processing, preventing unauthorized reading of sensitive data.&lt;SEP&gt;Encryption for data at rest and in transit increases resource demand.&lt;SEP&gt;The process by which data is made unreadable and locked with a secret code.&lt;SEP&gt;Encryption is a method used to safeguard data at rest, in transit, and during processing, preventing unauthorized reading of sensitive data.&lt;SEP&gt;Encryption for data at rest and in transit increases resource demand.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-727509da0a6c0effe0339afa582c8da4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3c503260b531691857650e03a6b6b239&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60be6d579871b518c685612e6a815820</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/security/principles.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/security/tradeoffs.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/security/application-secrets.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/security/principles.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/security/tradeoffs.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776996863</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Data Classification">
  <data key="d0">Data Classification</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Data classification is the process of categorizing, labeling, and organizing data based on its type, sensitivity level, potential risk, information type, compliance requirements, and other organizational criteria. Common sensitivity levels include Public, General, Confidential, and Highly Confidential.

Data classification serves as a foundational practice that guides security measures, influences architectural decisions, determines encryption requirements, ensures appropriate access controls and protection levels, and supports compliance reporting. By assigning confidentiality levels to data assets, organizations can prioritize resources and apply proportionate safeguards.

While essential for effective data governance and security, data classification can be expensive in terms of costs and effort, particularly when applied across large and complex workload environments.&lt;SEP&gt;Data classification involves categorizing data based on its importance and prioritization for backup to focus resources on critical data.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-727509da0a6c0effe0339afa582c8da4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8bfa9de462a82f93657efb2d63f7e3fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-36890a0152ce0c702b07e77948e4fe1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6949410eeaa7d29fe48e0341815607d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e507b943962b0d36b3563fef00c4090&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50e84f8f3385ed4e029dfd1f75a5fc3e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fef76463ffa39e57a8a833790934275c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f42998a4e0c9bc3045bdc13dd319dd6a</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/security/principles.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/security/data-classification.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/security/encryption.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/security/maturity-model.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/security/principles.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/cost-optimization/optimize-data-costs.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776998690</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Role-Based Training">
  <data key="d0">Role-Based Training</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Role-based training focuses on developing deep expertise in platform offerings and security features that address specific workload concerns.&lt;SEP&gt;Role-based training focuses on developing deep expertise in platform offerings and security features that address specific workload concerns.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-727509da0a6c0effe0339afa582c8da4</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/security/principles.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/security/principles.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776996682</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Data Exfiltration">
  <data key="d0">Data Exfiltration</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Data exfiltration refers to unauthorized data transfer resulting from malicious or inadvertent access to data.&lt;SEP&gt;Data exfiltration refers to unauthorized data transfer resulting from malicious or inadvertent access to data.&lt;SEP&gt;Data exfiltration is a security risk mitigated by implementing network isolation and allowing only approved outbound access from Machine Learning workspaces.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-727509da0a6c0effe0339afa582c8da4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e7c50c3fc68e8ca3e2a2600f5ec515c5</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/security/principles.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/security/principles.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-machine-learning.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777002246</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Access Controls">
  <data key="d0">Access Controls</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Access controls are mechanisms that grant access only on a need-to-know basis to protect workloads from unauthorized access.&lt;SEP&gt;Strong access controls authenticate and authorize access to systems, minimizing access based on privilege, scope, and time.&lt;SEP&gt;Access controls are mechanisms that grant access only on a need-to-know basis to protect workloads from unauthorized access.&lt;SEP&gt;Strong access controls authenticate and authorize access to systems, minimizing access based on privilege, scope, and time.&lt;SEP&gt;Access controls are restrictions that prevent overspending and ensure only authorized individuals can consume resources.&lt;SEP&gt;Access controls ensure that only authorized users can interact with specific data subsets, applying least privilege on relevant data.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-727509da0a6c0effe0339afa582c8da4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3a879a1f2cc37839210f7fa1a38c26a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d57924d25d627fa240cecd66ef26712&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96152a32ed0c284eafe7250fb96edb70</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/security/principles.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/security/principles.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/cost-optimization/set-spending-guardrails.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/ai/training-data-design.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777004059</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Audit Trail">
  <data key="d0">Audit Trail</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">An audit trail is a record of all types of access activities that supports faster detection of security incidents.&lt;SEP&gt;Audit trail maintains logs of all access activities to support faster detection, recovery, and ongoing security monitoring.&lt;SEP&gt;An audit trail ensures the system is auditable, validates assume-breach strategies, and tracks compliance requirements.&lt;SEP&gt;An audit trail is a record of all types of access activities that supports faster detection of security incidents.&lt;SEP&gt;Audit trail maintains logs of all access activities to support faster detection, recovery, and ongoing security monitoring.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-727509da0a6c0effe0339afa582c8da4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3a879a1f2cc37839210f7fa1a38c26a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-385f36a776384c86dd2e520eb0f51b04</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/security/principles.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/security/identity-access.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/security/principles.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776996683</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Confidentiality">
  <data key="d0">Confidentiality</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Confidentiality is a security design goal focused on preventing exposure to privacy, regulatory, application, and proprietary information through access restrictions and obfuscation techniques.&lt;SEP&gt;Confidentiality is a security principle focused on protecting data as it flows through system components, enforced through consistent hardening levels.&lt;SEP&gt;Confidentiality is a security design goal focused on preventing exposure to privacy, regulatory, application, and proprietary information through access restrictions and obfuscation techniques.&lt;SEP&gt;Confidentiality is a security principle focused on protecting data as it flows through system components, enforced through consistent hardening levels.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-727509da0a6c0effe0339afa582c8da4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3a879a1f2cc37839210f7fa1a38c26a3</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/security/principles.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/security/principles.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776996683</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Security Compliance Requirements">
  <data key="d0">Security Compliance Requirements</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Security compliance requirements are standards imposed by organizational policies, regulatory compliance, and industry standards that influence security design and assurances.&lt;SEP&gt;Security compliance requirements are standards imposed by organizational policies, regulatory compliance, and industry standards that influence security design and assurances.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-727509da0a6c0effe0339afa582c8da4</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/security/principles.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/security/principles.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776996683</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="SDL">
  <data key="d0">SDL</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">SDL (Security Development Lifecycle) is a method that brings clarity around security features and helps maintain an inventory of workload assets and their security reports.&lt;SEP&gt;SDL (Security Development Lifecycle) is a method that brings clarity around security features and helps maintain an inventory of workload assets and their security reports.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-18570fb8b58b59977b15628b836fce56</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/security/principles.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/security/principles.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776996677</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Automation Of Repetitive Tasks">
  <data key="d0">Automation Of Repetitive Tasks</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Automation of repetitive tasks is a concept that decreases the chance of human error that can create risk.&lt;SEP&gt;Automation of repetitive tasks is a concept that decreases the chance of human error that can create risk.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-18570fb8b58b59977b15628b836fce56</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/security/principles.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/security/principles.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776996677</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Integrity">
  <data key="d0">Integrity</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Integrity is a security principle aimed at preventing corruption of design, implementation, operations, and data throughout the workload lifecycle.&lt;SEP&gt;Integrity is a security principle aimed at preventing corruption of design, implementation, operations, and data throughout the workload lifecycle.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3a879a1f2cc37839210f7fa1a38c26a3</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/security/principles.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/security/principles.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776996684</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Supply Chain Security">
  <data key="d0">Supply Chain Security</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Supply chain security involves continuously protecting against and detecting vulnerabilities in software dependencies during build time and runtime.&lt;SEP&gt;Supply chain security involves continuously protecting against and detecting vulnerabilities in software dependencies during build time and runtime.&lt;SEP&gt;Protection of the entire software supply chain, including third-party components, development tools, and processes from compromise or tampering.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3a879a1f2cc37839210f7fa1a38c26a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d45ee5353204fd65637dc950ee85715</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/security/principles.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/security/principles.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/security/secure-development-lifecycle.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776996737</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cryptography">
  <data key="d0">Cryptography</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Cryptography techniques including attestation, code signing, certificates, and encryption used to establish trust and verify data integrity.&lt;SEP&gt;Cryptography techniques including attestation, code signing, certificates, and encryption used to establish trust and verify data integrity.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3a879a1f2cc37839210f7fa1a38c26a3</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/security/principles.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/security/principles.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776996685</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="DDoS Attacks">
  <data key="d0">DDoS Attacks</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Distributed denial of service attacks are malicious actions that cause resource exhaustion and block system access.&lt;SEP&gt;Distributed denial-of-service attacks are a type of threat that Azure provides detection and protection against.&lt;SEP&gt;Distributed denial of service attacks are malicious actions that cause resource exhaustion and block system access.&lt;SEP&gt;Distributed denial of service attacks that Azure Front Door's CDN capabilities help withstand.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3a879a1f2cc37839210f7fa1a38c26a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-080b0147971910915ed740faf5b69284&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5658d6f41065712f87008266f41103c</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/security/principles.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/security/monitor-threats.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/security/principles.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-front-door.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001683</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Just-In-Time Access">
  <data key="d0">Just-In-Time Access</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Just-in-time (JIT) access is a time-based security mode that replaces standing permissions to minimize risk exposure.&lt;SEP&gt;Just-in-time access is a standardized default mechanism for granting human accounts access to sensitive resources.&lt;SEP&gt;A security feature that locks down access to sensitive components by granting temporary access only when needed.&lt;SEP&gt;Just-in-time (JIT) access is a control method for sensitive components that grants temporary access as needed.&lt;SEP&gt;Just-in-time (JIT) access is a time-based security mode that replaces standing permissions to minimize risk exposure.&lt;SEP&gt;Just-in-time access minimizes exposure windows and reduces risk of unauthorized access for VMs not protected by a firewall.&lt;SEP&gt;Just-in-time access minimizes exposure windows and reduces risk of unauthorized access for VMs requiring direct admin access.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3a879a1f2cc37839210f7fa1a38c26a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e0d4c6d0c4bc09abb77f7cb933aa9a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50e84f8f3385ed4e029dfd1f75a5fc3e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-734874c613782da336574a76536f1d22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4eb78b84d41322da30efb8dd70aafc73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37837339dd05d2a9f58b6dc734ebd967</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/security/principles.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/security/maturity-model.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/security/principles.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/azure-virtual-desktop/networking.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777004940</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Just-Enough-Access">
  <data key="d0">Just-Enough-Access</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Just-enough-access (JEA) is a security mode that limits access scope to only what is necessary for a task.&lt;SEP&gt;Just-enough-access (JEA) is a security mode that limits access scope to only what is necessary for a task.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3a879a1f2cc37839210f7fa1a38c26a3</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/security/principles.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/security/principles.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776996685</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Code Signing">
  <data key="d0">Code Signing</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Code signing is a cryptographic technique used to verify software authenticity and integrity.&lt;SEP&gt;Code signing is a cryptographic technique used to verify software authenticity and integrity.&lt;SEP&gt;A method requiring code used for training in Machine Learning compute environments to be signed, ensuring it is from a trusted source and untampered.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3a879a1f2cc37839210f7fa1a38c26a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e7c50c3fc68e8ca3e2a2600f5ec515c5</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/security/principles.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/security/principles.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-machine-learning.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777002246</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Data Integrity">
  <data key="d0">Data Integrity</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Data integrity ensures that data remains consistent and trustworthy during incidents and after system recovery.&lt;SEP&gt;Data integrity ensures that data remains consistent and trustworthy during incidents and after system recovery.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3a879a1f2cc37839210f7fa1a38c26a3</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/security/principles.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/security/principles.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776996686</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Attack Surface">
  <data key="d0">Attack Surface</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Attack surface refers to the total number of exploitable vectors in a system that can be reduced through security measures.&lt;SEP&gt;Attack surface refers to the total number of exploitable vectors in a system that can be reduced through security measures.</data>
