<node id="Safe Deployment Practices">
  <data key="d0">Safe Deployment Practices</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Safe Deployment Practices (SDP) are processes, procedures, and strategies that define how to safely make and deploy changes to a workload, focusing on managing risk, minimizing human error, and keeping systems stable during routine, feature, and emergency updates in production. They serve as standards for integrating new functionality that ensure safe and proper operation of workloads.

SDP aim to achieve consistency in deployments using guardrails that reduce the effect of errors and unexpected conditions. They encompass a wide range of techniques including automation, Infrastructure as Code (IaC), branching strategies, feature flags, progressive exposure rollout, canary deployments, blue-green deployments, and dark launches to enhance flexibility and reliability. These practices often involve deploying multiple concurrent instances to shift traffic in a controlled way, which can increase resource costs but provides greater safety and control over the deployment process.

SDP include code promotion through environments and quality gate checks, which are followed even during emergency deployments albeit with accelerated timelines. They are enforced through CI/CD pipelines that prohibit manual deployment activities, ensuring consistency and repeatability. These practices also ensure safety around sensitive production data and align with zero-downtime requirements.

Additionally, Safe Deployment Practices apply to AI workloads, treating AI workload APIs the same as any other API by placing them behind a gateway and handling code with established deployment standards.&lt;SEP&gt;Safe deployment practices involve deploying updates progressively to customer groups using strategies like progressive exposure and health models.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-02d45413152a840f1fd2444de10c78fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31da0f57b1bbfcf80aeba441d56d15f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44641efcd9d3edd6dd3a0ceda8c1536b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-082de75994ad13c747e8ee30b159eda8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-adeaccdaf088dd43cb5b66e1bb18c9ac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ed16a2af63252a2201b654a7b2b61614&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8fe53a5474c5e4934795683b73b06789&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-87618a32c6b3af420a6fe25de69e11bb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ea600d3306dde466b8e4fa453de97e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9d58b81465c40024b1e7d72578e9d17&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a6bdb7b9ebcb55bb58103735c0c58a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-222e44d67db5ca1f719f77ecd8131ebe</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/operational-excellence/principles.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/operational-excellence/tradeoffs.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/operational-excellence/safe-deployments.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/reliability/maturity-model.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/operational-excellence/devops-culture.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/operational-excellence/maturity-model.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/operational-excellence/principles.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/operational-excellence/safe-deployments.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/operational-excellence/tradeoffs.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/design-guides/incident-management.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/ai/application-platform.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/ai/operations.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/saas/devops.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777004397</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Automated Pipelines">
  <data key="d0">Automated Pipelines</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Automated pipelines deploy code and infrastructure changes across all environments to ensure consistent and repeatable deployments.&lt;SEP&gt;Automated pipelines deploy code and infrastructure changes across all environments to ensure consistent and repeatable deployments.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-02d45413152a840f1fd2444de10c78fa</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/operational-excellence/principles.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/operational-excellence/principles.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776997979</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Progressive Exposure">
  <data key="d0">Progressive Exposure</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Progressive exposure is a deployment strategy that involves the gradual rollout of new features or changes to successively larger groups of users, rather than releasing to all users simultaneously. This approach minimizes the potential blast radius of deployment-caused issues by controlling adoption in stages, allowing teams to uncover problems before broader rollout.

Progressive exposure can be implemented through various techniques, including feature flags, staged rollouts, canary releases, and side-by-side deployments. For example, canary releases target a small initial group of users first, enabling teams to monitor for issues and contain the impact of any vulnerabilities or defects before expanding to a wider audience. This controlled approach applies not only to traditional software changes but also to integrating models into production environments, making it a versatile risk-mitigation pattern across different deployment scenarios.&lt;SEP&gt;Progressive exposure is a deployment strategy that rolls out updates to customer groups in phases, starting with less-sensitive or early-adopter customers.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-02d45413152a840f1fd2444de10c78fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44641efcd9d3edd6dd3a0ceda8c1536b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d45ee5353204fd65637dc950ee85715&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-abf9fd57985f14c81b7d1b9d2924d1bc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95f95636f4fc764df073f790408dc229&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f31ccebc2a168b87f30309e8394d245&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-222e44d67db5ca1f719f77ecd8131ebe</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/operational-excellence/principles.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/operational-excellence/safe-deployments.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/security/secure-development-lifecycle.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/operational-excellence/principles.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/operational-excellence/safe-deployments.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/operational-excellence/testing.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/ai/mlops-genaiops.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/saas/devops.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777004398</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Configuration Drift">
  <data key="d0">Configuration Drift</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Configuration drift is a deviation from desired infrastructure state that IaC helps detect and mitigate.&lt;SEP&gt;Configuration drift is the risk of environments becoming inconsistent, which IaC deployed through CI/CD pipelines helps minimize.&lt;SEP&gt;Unintended changes in production environment configurations that agents can autonomously monitor and correct.&lt;SEP&gt;Configuration drift is the gradual divergence of a runtime environment from its intended state.&lt;SEP&gt;Configuration drift is a deviation from desired infrastructure state that IaC helps detect and mitigate.&lt;SEP&gt;Configuration drift occurs when test environments diverge from production, creating false confidence in quality.&lt;SEP&gt;Configuration drift refers to deviations from standardized configurations that can cause operational problems.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-02d45413152a840f1fd2444de10c78fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a87895e99b347e4b871e414149100339&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc830fbd68e22812a74bf3c788ef10f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b61803d2562ce9d74f9843d45925671b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f5f9d8aded9b458939437b400ad44c9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f94f551ab29e43c1a22f727367fe189</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/operational-excellence/principles.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/operational-excellence/infrastructure-as-code-design.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/operational-excellence/maturity-model.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/operational-excellence/principles.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/operational-excellence/testing.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-log-analytics.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777002159</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Technical Debt">
  <data key="d0">Technical Debt</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Technical debt refers to the accumulated inefficiencies, suboptimal design choices, shortcuts, or deferred work taken during development to deliver code faster or meet deadlines. These less-than-ideal implementations go live and complicate maintenance over time, affecting overall system performance, maintainability, scalability, and slowing growth. It also encompasses the accumulated cost of maintaining underutilized or poorly designed features that hinder productivity, as well as drift from original design principles due to rapid iteration.

Technical debt accumulates when a lack of automation in system design promotes repetitive tasks, or when teams take shortcuts under time pressure. It represents the estimated future cost of addressing these known problems and accumulated code quality issues in a workload, requiring ongoing effort to resolve.

Beyond being a liability, technical debt can serve as a strategic tool in development—capturing short-term decisions and acting as a motivator for incremental updates. When incurred, it should be intentional, documented, and repayable. Workload teams should regularly address technical debt as a recurring backlog task, prioritizing and managing it to prevent compounding inefficiencies over time.&lt;SEP&gt;Technical debt refers to accepted risks and design shortcomings that require intentional decision-making, documentation, and proactive management by architects and workload teams.&lt;SEP&gt;Accumulated suboptimal technical decisions that architects systematically address through workload optimization.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-02d45413152a840f1fd2444de10c78fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-720e4839ab18a5ecc8b875712ef0f4cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48dc8704fc8585c17685d5d5ff321ca8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f8d7568e4aed8a2c3665910aa5492c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8fe53a5474c5e4934795683b73b06789&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85dfff44e8e4c1e2d34f3f2fbc239ab2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b61803d2562ce9d74f9843d45925671b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f73ba7b617cf9dce0d1fe9a51c2649a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9297ee0ed639131ecea37be73ac398ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-80ff8d586800116f4a9c0f2e6c0039f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f7f774cbe85718aeb4ca8377bca84b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ae74d8c186fbc9d0dd83b114ed0e8e5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e98ea85178a5817d9a2ef65e41464e96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d5158d59c7d2bc03597e0221545a1a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8e1b9f43effe85bb4f106ab0a823c7d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06d07c7fd7e0ea2028615959913f9c73</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/operational-excellence/principles.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/performance-efficiency/continuous-performance-optimize.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/operational-excellence/maturity-model.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/operational-excellence/principles.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/operational-excellence/tools-processes.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/cost-optimization/optimize-component-costs.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/cost-optimization/optimize-personnel-time.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/design-guides/implementing-recommendations.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/architect-role/collaboration.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/architect-role/ongoing-support.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/whats-new.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/workloads.md</data>
