<node id="Service-Level Indicator">
  <data key="d0">Service-Level Indicator</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A quantitative measurement of a particular aspect of a service's performance, used to measure compliance with an SLO.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0a1ff429a57f5b474e0f29cc10e052a7</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/reliability/metrics.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776995823</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Service-Level Agreement">
  <data key="d0">Service-Level Agreement</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A contractual agreement between service provider and customer with potential financial consequences for failure to meet terms.&lt;SEP&gt;A Service-Level Agreement (SLA) is a contractual agreement between service provider and customer defining service-level objectives, with potential financial consequences for failure.&lt;SEP&gt;A service-level agreement (SLA) defines limits for overall operation time including all timeouts and delays.&lt;SEP&gt;A formal commitment specifying expected service availability and performance levels, often as a percentage of uptime.&lt;SEP&gt;A service-level agreement (SLA) defines commitments for core capabilities or infrastructure offerings between teams.&lt;SEP&gt;SLAs define availability targets for Azure VMware Solution applications.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0a1ff429a57f5b474e0f29cc10e052a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5fc827ca305a978ff51b6d10e1dd732&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06ce09d2bcc53388fe6e985bfc862f65&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f62c9cb7be167228d33fac3b26a501c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17e137434baf52b7c6f746bc411962c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d2316cc990e8eb48dc944c11d4a38990</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/reliability/metrics.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/cost-optimization/optimize-environment-costs.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/design-guides/handle-transient-faults.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/design-guides/optimize-workload-using-flows.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/workloads.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/azure-vmware/infrastructure.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777005236</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mean Time To Recover">
  <data key="d0">Mean Time To Recover</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The time taken to restore a component after a failure is detected.&lt;SEP&gt;MTTR is a business metric measuring the average time to recover from a failure.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0a1ff429a57f5b474e0f29cc10e052a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2ed64d4db4b1d8cf5e3ba5c43f2faf7</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/reliability/metrics.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/reliability/simplify.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776996023</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mean Time Between Failure">
  <data key="d0">Mean Time Between Failure</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The duration a workload can perform without interruption until it fails.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0a1ff429a57f5b474e0f29cc10e052a7</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/reliability/metrics.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776995824</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Availability Targets">
  <data key="d0">Availability Targets</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Quality standards defining when a system remains accessible and functional, checked using SLOs.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0a1ff429a57f5b474e0f29cc10e052a7</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/reliability/metrics.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776995824</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Recovery Targets">
  <data key="d0">Recovery Targets</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Targets corresponding to RTO, RPO, MTTR, and MTBF metrics for business continuity and disaster recovery.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0a1ff429a57f5b474e0f29cc10e052a7</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/reliability/metrics.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776995824</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Correctness Targets">
  <data key="d0">Correctness Targets</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Targets ensuring a workload properly performs its functions with consistent quality.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0a1ff429a57f5b474e0f29cc10e052a7</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/reliability/metrics.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776995825</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Reliability Targets">
  <data key="d0">Reliability Targets</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The desired quality goal of a workload including availability and recoverability, promised to users and business stakeholders.&lt;SEP&gt;Reliability targets assess each flow's importance and set performance thresholds, SLAs, and SLOs, with higher criticality flows requiring more stringent targets.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0a1ff429a57f5b474e0f29cc10e052a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef06ef7197a8a530ba87de793b6cb9e5</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/reliability/metrics.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/design-guides/optimize-workload-using-flows.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999433</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="SLA">
  <data key="d0">SLA</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A Service Level Agreement is a formal contract between an organization and its customers with direct financial and legal implications.&lt;SEP&gt;Service Level Agreement (SLA) is a Microsoft commitment providing insight into availability of specific areas, but not guaranteeing an offering as a whole.&lt;SEP&gt;Service Level Agreement set at 99.90% availability per month for the workload, covering two core user flows and including performance considerations.&lt;SEP&gt;Service Level Agreements define limits within which chaos testing should stay to avoid reputation or financial effects.&lt;SEP&gt;SLAs are service level agreements representing promises from service providers that capacity plans should align with.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3d531693488b5e9a3be349c75f47f4c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0075254306371deed306288e9b95eee2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6461a458cf4d4525d7fe6f8f9cfa20e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3fb455e5de9f1067bb91e069db8c9a2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a9cc1e862b6a71315c64180df14f2e6</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/reliability/metrics.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/reliability/testing-strategy.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/performance-efficiency/capacity-planning.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776996904</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Workload Team">
  <data key="d0">Workload Team</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The Workload Team is a cross-functional team comprising members with both technical and business disciplines—including legal, finance, business, and engineering departments—focused on the success of a workload throughout its full lifecycle. It serves as the direct team handling most workload requirements and builds relationships with other teams to co-deliver on workload goals.

The Workload Team is responsible for implementing architectural specifications designed by architects. Its responsibilities span a wide range of operational and technical tasks, including configuring Azure Virtual Desktop deployments, managing user access, monitoring, compliance, and general operational tasks. Additionally, the team handles performance analysis, OS and application updates, key management, and FSLogix management. It is also responsible for application-level backups, data recovery, application configurations, and application-specific credentials.

The Workload Team is accountable for its own documented practices, including documenting any extra steps or intentional deviations from standard procedures.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3d531693488b5e9a3be349c75f47f4c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e98ea85178a5817d9a2ef65e41464e96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06d07c7fd7e0ea2028615959913f9c73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17e137434baf52b7c6f746bc411962c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-07c9f016b38b353425dfad31fd5db923&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-291c7f730b8dea7d3fc0379e8208d526&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f8759d38d5ccbdc62d8b354e52ff647&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-32aeab96a805c5243f99de82ce31a9a4</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/reliability/metrics.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/architect-role/collaboration.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/workloads.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/ai/responsible-ai.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/azure-virtual-desktop/landing-zone-integration.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/azure-vmware/landing-zone-integration.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777005274</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="API">
  <data key="d0">API</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">The API for which a response time SLO is set, excluding client processing time and internet network traversal.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3d531693488b5e9a3be349c75f47f4c3</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/reliability/metrics.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776995825</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="MTTR">
  <data key="d0">MTTR</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Mean Time To Recovery, a reliability metric for measuring recovery performance.&lt;SEP&gt;Mean Time To Recovery is a recovery metric target defined through failure mode analysis and disaster recovery planning.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-354f0a4ce38a1d015d0e524baae4a06a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0075254306371deed306288e9b95eee2</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/reliability/metrics.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776995826</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="MTBF">
  <data key="d0">MTBF</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Mean Time Between Failures, a reliability metric that can be challenging to define for PaaS or SaaS models.&lt;SEP&gt;Mean Time Between Failures is a recovery metric target defined through failure mode analysis and disaster recovery planning.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-354f0a4ce38a1d015d0e524baae4a06a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0075254306371deed306288e9b95eee2</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/reliability/metrics.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776995826</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="SQL Managed Instance">
  <data key="d0">SQL Managed Instance</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">SQL Managed Instance is an Azure database service that provides broad SQL Server compatibility using an instance-scoped model where all databases share compute resources such as vCores, memory, IOPS, and storage, supporting only vertical scaling. It is owned and managed by a separate team and serves as a critical dependency for certain workloads.

SQL Managed Instance offers a 99.99% SLA, though this may be adjusted (e.g., to a 99.80% SLO) to account for data operation failures. It supports zone redundancy, failover groups, geo-replication, and managed maintenance windows for high availability and disaster recovery. Deployment configurations include instance pools, stop/start schedules, and failover groups.

The service deploys into dedicated virtual network subnets and provides instance-scoped controls, including SQL Agent, CLR assemblies, linked servers, and Enterprise Edition capabilities. It supports Microsoft Entra authentication, auditing, Transparent Data Encryption (TDE), managed identities, and various other security configurations.

SQL Managed Instance uses an always-provisioned compute model with vCore-based billing and bills for reserved storage. It can be governed through Azure Policy and Azure Advisor recommendations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-354f0a4ce38a1d015d0e524baae4a06a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e048464dc928ee7d9ace1aae32cd84e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0428e305303dcb842ea973ff0a9c850a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2db34598644f37a302573882b84524ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-defb38181cf95696eca523469c9e1024&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b7590943dc4adb5e3d7d1a82fbd3716f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dffaff7bec32455c14addc7b21c4afa3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-99ff69f495aed35c047df8c9f7f551df&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f2023b0dcac7ae7c7d25fc08064528b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-101349d8f262064be3735fbc48d13b8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8cca5d0852b439b0c20120d19e126b0</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/reliability/metrics.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-sql-managed-instance.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777002767</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Azure SLA">
  <data key="d0">Azure SLA</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Microsoft commitments for uptime and connectivity, including procedures for service credits.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-354f0a4ce38a1d015d0e524baae4a06a</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/reliability/metrics.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776995829</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Multiregion Deployment">
  <data key="d0">Multiregion Deployment</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Multiregion deployment implements redundancy to mitigate regional outage risk and improve SLOs through failover capabilities.&lt;SEP&gt;Multiregion deployment spreads solutions across regions for higher resiliency through data replication between regions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0075254306371deed306288e9b95eee2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e985b627cce63375e11a2952b871309a</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/reliability/metrics.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/saas/data.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777004307</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="High Availability Pattern">
  <data key="d0">High Availability Pattern</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">High availability pattern distributes load across regions for more capacity, with the entire system's performance contributing to the SLO.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0075254306371deed306288e9b95eee2</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/reliability/metrics.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776995830</data>
