<node id="API Management">
  <data key="d0">API Management</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Azure API Management is an Azure platform service designed for hosting, managing, and securing APIs. It provides a comprehensive set of features including gateway traffic handling, policy configuration, versioning, backends, diagnostics, and lifecycle operations such as backup/restore, zone redundancy, and multiregion deployment.

API Management supports rate limiting and request throttling, protecting APIs from abuse and enabling different API product tiers. It offers security controls, managed identities, workspaces, and deployment pipelines. The service supports density through API policies and provides considerations for both distributed and centralized deployment models.

Azure API Management includes limits, constraints, and configuration options such as autoscaling and gateway units. Notably, scaling operations can take up to 45 minutes to complete.&lt;SEP&gt;API Management is an API gateway used for calculating chargebacks for business units utilizing shared resources.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c65b26c693a23d19a5f6c2c2910aca49&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e0544ee58fb5345261d0d125500955a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f9c864d1abe92319ff199943a677dc7c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95454c21cf94702a963cb14772e7fc53&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9c53b990e9918af85864a95bf9284ab0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c7409b2f79632903a9d9de4c67055d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfcc3c8f8098ec40d6c8eea6fb30a4a4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1851b3d40bd0c571dbe26a4fe3cd7a31&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-960ce6b41ae564fa5b6c6cd61ca41bea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10a01f49cd31e39d688d04081e77099c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6791a2ed934654a8da6b225e44dac9e3</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/reliability/scaling.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/cost-optimization/consolidation.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/cost-optimization/optimize-scaling-costs.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-api-management.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/ai/application-design.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777003415</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Vertical Partitioning">
  <data key="d0">Vertical Partitioning</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Vertical partitioning divides fields of data store items according to their usage pattern, with each partition holding a subset of fields.&lt;SEP&gt;Vertical partitioning divides a table into smaller tables by selecting specific columns or fields for each partition.&lt;SEP&gt;Vertical partitioning divides a dataset based on specific attributes or columns, allowing more efficient access to required data.&lt;SEP&gt;Vertical partitioning separates frequently accessed attributes from less frequently accessed ones for efficient data retrieval.&lt;SEP&gt;Vertical partitioning stores different properties of an item in different partitions to reduce I/O and performance costs associated with fetching frequently accessed items.&lt;SEP&gt;A partitioning strategy that divides data by columns or fields rather than rows, storing different subsets of fields in separate partitions based on access patterns.&lt;SEP&gt;Vertical partitioning splits data by columns or fields, allowing queries to specify the partition directly.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6cc0d4e3f080b4962999c8f4d2734bc9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-059c3b1921ce4b9169eff7763d1acd07&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6542d2e0c59d483460cb92d7687c6c5e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c944e9a44adf39c12919b36fa366a12e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c0f1c2454a3cd326399dd189c571e67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-103386df6f9acd0832fa49dd897fcede&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dbc8280bb50c2a5f4a589617c1bed437</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/reliability/scaling.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/performance-efficiency/optimize-data-performance.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/performance-efficiency/scale-partition.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/design-guides/partition-data.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999506</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Functional Partitioning">
  <data key="d0">Functional Partitioning</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Functional partitioning aggregates data according to how each bounded context in the system uses the data.&lt;SEP&gt;Functional partitioning divides data based on specific functions or operations that need to be performed.&lt;SEP&gt;Functional partitioning separates data by function, allowing each partition to focus on specific operations independently.&lt;SEP&gt;Functional partitioning separates data by bounded context or business area to improve isolation and data access performance.&lt;SEP&gt;A partitioning strategy that aggregates and stores data according to how each bounded context or business domain uses it.&lt;SEP&gt;A partitioning strategy based on entity throughput and query performance requirements.&lt;SEP&gt;Functional partitioning separates data by bounded context or function, allowing queries to specify the partition.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6cc0d4e3f080b4962999c8f4d2734bc9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6542d2e0c59d483460cb92d7687c6c5e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c944e9a44adf39c12919b36fa366a12e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f5c02d75cf91d6692adb66113df89eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c0f1c2454a3cd326399dd189c571e67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-103386df6f9acd0832fa49dd897fcede&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dbc8280bb50c2a5f4a589617c1bed437</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/reliability/scaling.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/performance-efficiency/scale-partition.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/design-guides/partition-data.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999505</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Azure Monitor Autoscale">
  <data key="d0">Azure Monitor Autoscale</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Azure Monitor Autoscale provides common autoscaling functionality for Azure Virtual Machine Scale Sets, Azure App Service, Azure Spring Apps and more.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6cc0d4e3f080b4962999c8f4d2734bc9</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/reliability/scaling.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776995950</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="KEDA">
  <data key="d0">KEDA</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">KEDA is a tool for event-driven autoscaling available in Azure Kubernetes Service and Azure Container Apps.&lt;SEP&gt;Kubernetes Event-Driven Autoscaling (KEDA) can scale applications based on scalers such as the length of a Kafka topic.&lt;SEP&gt;Kubernetes Event-driven Autoscaling (KEDA) is an open-source technology used by Azure Container Apps for event-driven scaling.&lt;SEP&gt;Kubernetes Event Driven Autoscaler (KEDA) allows applications to scale based on events from a rich catalog of sources.&lt;SEP&gt;Kubernetes Event Driven Autoscaler (KEDA) allows applications to scale based on events with over 50 available scalers.&lt;SEP&gt;KEDA is a Kubernetes-based event-driven autoscaler that allows applications to scale based on events such as queue messages or topic lag.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6cc0d4e3f080b4962999c8f4d2734bc9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-066af31d500f1a0a71053da1bd80b73e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7424eb960d7c722faf499211634269a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8103c464163f3ff725a5c23642a7e869&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e7ebf4632700c0d791afc29f31a4749&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f2789d17a06aaa84a6ce5fba59172ad</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/reliability/scaling.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/cost-optimization/optimize-scaling-costs.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/design-guides/background-jobs.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-kubernetes-service.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001870</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Azure Automation">
  <data key="d0">Azure Automation</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Azure Automation is an Azure-hosted platform for running PowerShell and Python code across cloud and on-premises environments. It provides features such as runbooks, logging, and source control integration, enabling users to automate manual, repetitive tasks and administrative operations.

Key use cases for Azure Automation include designing custom automatic scaling operations using APIs, executing automated remediation actions when alerts are triggered, shutting down virtual machines during inactivity to reduce costs, and scheduling regular backups of Azure Databricks workspace artifacts. It is also used to orchestrate routine tasks, lifecycle management, and scheduling environment shutdowns through runbooks.

Azure Automation supports implementing automated failover for activating secondary consumers and handles operations requiring Azure Resource Manager interaction. Its runbooks can manage access policy rotation and throughput changes to reduce manual overhead. The service has also been evaluated for workflow automation capabilities for Service Bus operations.&lt;SEP&gt;Azure Automation enables creation of runbooks for repetitive Virtual WAN tasks like gateway scaling and VPN site management.&lt;SEP&gt;Azure Automation is a solution used to manage OS updates and maintain security compliance by making critical updates.&lt;SEP&gt;Azure Automation is a tool used to automate tasks such as certificate updates and renewals.&lt;SEP&gt;Azure Automation is used for automating IT processes in Azure VMware Solution management.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6cc0d4e3f080b4962999c8f4d2734bc9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2bbb7e81c733c8a34be73ab3d43902b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48dc8704fc8585c17685d5d5ff321ca8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6542d2e0c59d483460cb92d7687c6c5e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d2431ac43eabe818a5350eafb1e23d38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d97489185a44dcbeac38d0a5a48e1097&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-198a3a5ea0d4ee6048ded7764b7c4702&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ec7a8b0cbc84ecca1daa616149db819&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f08277df10a908d985f4ac74cf88b922&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b6dcb04088b404ffb7108762ce20034&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4d0ea951af59993c2b22cf6dbc254d79&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db510f47656045373bcac562ba7e53ca&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1bb68bab7c1b474f207b34f9a74328e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2cc6dd32c7c960a85b9a30c11bc43659&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e70d002685d5e46f8ea08751d291ae2b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-defb38181cf95696eca523469c9e1024&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c74dac0959c495ccca3e91ce252cb225&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb860ff346987514c80ee21375c28eec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-108ead3dd7f225a59ab1119690c2ecfc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c45aa9611048afab38755ade22af2f4f</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/reliability/scaling.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/performance-efficiency/continuous-performance-optimize.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/performance-efficiency/scale-partition.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/operational-excellence/enable-automation.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/cost-optimization/optimize-component-costs.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/cost-optimization/optimize-personnel-time.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-databricks.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-event-grid.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-event-hubs.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-service-bus.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-sql-managed-instance.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-virtual-wan.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/virtual-machines.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/azure-vmware/application-platform.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/azure-vmware/operations.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777005394</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Azure Spring Apps">
  <data key="d0">Azure Spring Apps</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Azure Spring Apps is an Azure service supported by Azure Monitor Autoscale for scaling functionality.&lt;SEP&gt;Azure Spring Apps is an Azure service that can be deployed via virtual network injection.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6cc0d4e3f080b4962999c8f4d2734bc9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40e0fe247c84a41199e470c86f0e983f</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/reliability/scaling.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/security/networking.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776996663</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Vertical Scaling">
  <data key="d0">Vertical Scaling</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Vertical scaling, also known as scaling up, is a scaling approach that increases the capacity of existing resources by adding more compute power to a single machine or instance. This typically involves upgrading to a larger server or instance size by increasing resources such as CPU, memory, or other capabilities rather than adding more machines.

Vertical scaling is particularly suitable for stateful applications where distributing workloads across multiple servers may be complex. In cloud environments, it can involve selecting a larger VM SKU or upgrading instance sizes, though this process often requires downtime to implement the changes. In the context of messaging services, vertical scaling may involve Premium tier messaging unit increases that provide dedicated capacity with automatic adjustment capabilities for dynamic workloads.

While vertical scaling can leverage larger and more powerful resources, it is generally considered more complex and less reliable than horizontal scaling. It may also involve higher costs due to the premium pricing of more powerful hardware. Additionally, there are inherent physical limits to how much a single machine can be scaled up, which can constrain long-term growth compared to horizontal alternatives.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c387aff770332292e0a1e78af18b8fbe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c944e9a44adf39c12919b36fa366a12e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-078fb475b8b7efe4018b64a1ed512f54&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-568f4a02582f7c3e1a2aba678f415b7c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-498530bb6096692fd24e326f3f0018c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e7401b60ee469e374fd0c1ceb511518&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-119b4c66eb667c03ba64de0007c8067a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc9c17e8a9e3ca66742a781b355fe68c</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/reliability/scaling.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/performance-efficiency/scale-partition.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/app-service-web-apps.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-service-bus.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/saas/compute.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/azure-vmware/infrastructure.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777005243</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Stateless Design">
  <data key="d0">Stateless Design</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Stateless design is a principle recommending services avoid requiring requests to be routed to the same instance, enabling effective horizontal scaling.&lt;SEP&gt;Stateless design involves designing applications without server-side session state, using external state stores for session data management.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c387aff770332292e0a1e78af18b8fbe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e4f212602314ae8aa68b2a4631872d3</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/reliability/scaling.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/performance-efficiency/scale-partition.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776997472</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Load Patterns">
  <data key="d0">Load Patterns</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Load patterns are identifiable usage patterns for user and system flows that inform scaling operations, including static, dynamic regular, dynamic irregular predictable, and dynamic irregular unpredictable types.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c387aff770332292e0a1e78af18b8fbe</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/reliability/scaling.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776995953</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Microservices">
  <data key="d0">Microservices</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Microservices is an architectural design pattern in which applications are structured as small, independent, loosely coupled services, each focusing on specific business functionalities. These services communicate via well-defined standards and can be developed, deployed, and scaled independently.

A key advantage of microservices is their ability to decouple components, achieving containment and isolation in architecture. By segmenting services from one another, microservices reduce the risk of extensive problems arising from single component failures, effectively isolating faults. This pattern is also favored for workload refactoring to gain efficiencies and reduce costs.

However, microservices can introduce complexity, overhead, and dependency issues, and the pattern is not the best option for every application. Careful consideration is needed to determine whether the benefits of isolation and independent scalability outweigh the added architectural complexity.&lt;SEP&gt;Microservices is a design pattern enabling individual components to scale independently to accommodate varying loads.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ef12664b5845bb63109916b6e16079b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2ed64d4db4b1d8cf5e3ba5c43f2faf7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-230e614bb0d26b9bf3245480dced7db5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3df9290c84fe42909028ddfaafd17299&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e4f212602314ae8aa68b2a4631872d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53c726f154bb635219bc21e5a4c3c1ce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5ec8d1aedbc1588b6b1ad6ff2df36aaa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9274b98dae68e1fda563c0a186872af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-adaa1027480290045f9552546fffa81d</data>
