  <data key="d5">1776999774</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Dead-Letter Queue">
  <data key="d0">Dead-Letter Queue</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A dead-letter queue is a pattern for handling corrupt messages by moving problematic messages out of the main processing queue.&lt;SEP&gt;Special queue that stores messages that don't process successfully after multiple attempts, preventing poison messages from blocking the main processing queue.&lt;SEP&gt;Special queue that stores messages that cannot be processed successfully after multiple attempts.&lt;SEP&gt;A dead-letter queue approach ensures information from incoming invocations is not lost after all retry attempts are exhausted.&lt;SEP&gt;Dead-letter queues automatically isolate undeliverable messages, preventing them from blocking healthy message flow.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3df6e00fa9a90ca53e8d360c865875b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0c3ed9c705aa5674f170118b9caf9fbf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c0150f1d7a68414d68f2d36fee4a0389&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06ce09d2bcc53388fe6e985bfc862f65&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b49c55356fee42f19cd603d7edc0b8b6</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/reliability/self-preservation.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/design-guides/background-jobs.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/design-guides/handle-transient-faults.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-service-bus.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777002492</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
