</node>
<node id="Assume-Breach">
  <data key="d0">Assume-Breach</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Assume-breach is a security mentality applied in security hardening that assumes attackers may already have access to the system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6f8c5639aa16c2329aa96771580d1b65</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/security/harden-resources.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776996334</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Software Supply Chain">
  <data key="d0">Software Supply Chain</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The software supply chain refers to the sources from which components are obtained, requiring all components to come from trusted sources as part of hardening.&lt;SEP&gt;Software supply chain hardening involves ensuring CI/CD pipelines include container-aware scanning for workload security.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6f8c5639aa16c2329aa96771580d1b65&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc3e5ddb4441b86b0ef64d617182432b</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/security/harden-resources.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-kubernetes-service.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001884</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Network Segmentation">
  <data key="d0">Network Segmentation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Network segmentation divides a network into segments to isolate critical assets and sensitive data from less secure assets, reducing lateral movements by attackers.&lt;SEP&gt;Network segmentation is a strategy that divides a network into small, isolated segments with security controls applied at boundaries to protect resources from hostile networks.&lt;SEP&gt;Network segmentation involves creating intentional segmentation and perimeters in network design with defense-in-depth principles using localized network controls at all network boundaries.&lt;SEP&gt;Network segmentation in Virtual WAN is controlled through routing tables to isolate routing domains and prevent unauthorized traffic.&lt;SEP&gt;Network segmentation is the practice of dividing networks into dedicated virtual networks and subnets to prevent IP conflicts, simplify management, and support scalability.&lt;SEP&gt;Network segmentation implements boundaries to isolate applications and prevent unauthorized access to Azure VMware Solution components.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6f8c5639aa16c2329aa96771580d1b65&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0824c72199637e43cfbbf3653d45df65&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f7d57d3c8e9b437c3bf033faee9829a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de21122336bd114f1583106e238ec656&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37837339dd05d2a9f58b6dc734ebd967&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2956238f945f53b269eebcd0c33b1c0f</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/security/harden-resources.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/security/networking.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-sql-database.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-virtual-wan.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/azure-virtual-desktop/networking.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/azure-vmware/security.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777005467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Safe Decommissioning">
  <data key="d0">Safe Decommissioning</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Safe decommissioning is a process used to mitigate the risk of accidental loss of critical functionality when removing resources or identities.&lt;SEP&gt;Safe decommissioning is a structured, safety-first approach to removing or deprecating components, treating deletions as irreversible with steps like validating inactivity, preserving state, disabling before deleting, and monitoring.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6f8c5639aa16c2329aa96771580d1b65&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-973e5b950ce99287babd4d9e8e310ec0</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/security/harden-resources.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/whats-new.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999940</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Continuous Threat Detection">
  <data key="d0">Continuous Threat Detection</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Continuous threat detection is performed to maintain protective measures and ensure hardening efforts remain dependable over time.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6f8c5639aa16c2329aa96771580d1b65</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/security/harden-resources.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776996335</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="DNS">
  <data key="d0">DNS</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Domain Name System is a network infrastructure component that requires hardening against cache poisoning, DDoS, and amplification attacks.&lt;SEP&gt;Domain Name System is a naming system whose propagation delay after failover must be accounted for by configuring applications to retry dropped connections.&lt;SEP&gt;Domain Name System (DNS) records are automatically updated after geo-failover to redirect endpoints to the new region.&lt;SEP&gt;DNS (Domain Name System) is a networking service placed in the hub network for shared use, with complexities that Azure networking teams must understand.&lt;SEP&gt;DNS is a networking service intended for shared use, placed in the hub network and centrally managed.&lt;SEP&gt;Domain Name System is required for proper name resolution in the Azure Virtual Desktop networking environment.&lt;SEP&gt;DNS (Domain Name System) is used for FQDN resolution, with Azure VMware Solution supporting up to three DNS servers per FQDN zone.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-654e7aada501f6fea6718cd59dbed297&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db2648910473195dad09b71af47dc280&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51a42def26d6149ba32bd2654d702dd4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-90098779392fd33c8719164d18403b49&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c00db143f490c3d917ea5c2345f79ef2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f8759d38d5ccbdc62d8b354e52ff647&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a6302e71e0ce865b4ad1deecc215e545</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/security/harden-resources.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-database-for-mysql.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-sql-database.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/virtual-network.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/azure-virtual-desktop/landing-zone-integration.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/azure-vmware/networking.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777005351</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="DDoS Protection">
  <data key="d0">DDoS Protection</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">DDoS Protection refers to distributed denial-of-service protection, a security mechanism designed to detect, filter, and mitigate volumetric and malicious traffic before it reaches application tiers or customer services. It defends against distributed denial-of-service attacks at an abstracted layer, operating at the virtual network or individual public IP level.

Cloud providers, notably Azure, offer DDoS Protection as a native service. Azure DDoS Protection specifically protects entire virtual networks (VNets) and public IP addresses, helping block malicious traffic before it reaches the gateway. Both basic and advanced tiers are typically available from cloud providers.

DDoS Protection is commonly implemented and managed by platform teams to monitor and secure network traffic. It is also a type of service that can be consolidated across workloads, enabling centralized protection for multiple applications and resources.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-654e7aada501f6fea6718cd59dbed297&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-734874c613782da336574a76536f1d22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-36dd3a8f5a535bef38f7d594298d02cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4bbc30e44876977540e409cc0b86f704&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37b84c5a9a2c854b590a8a22c511c52b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22d333e97696a760da77cbcd035a0d29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f8759d38d5ccbdc62d8b354e52ff647&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ca4d2bd438b809671ecf2d090095b06</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/security/harden-resources.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/security/maturity-model.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/security/networking.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/cost-optimization/consolidation.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-api-management.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-virtual-wan.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/azure-virtual-desktop/landing-zone-integration.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/sustainability/sustainability-security.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777005713</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Input Validation And Sanitization">
  <data key="d0">Input Validation And Sanitization</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A method to prevent injection attacks such as SQL injection and XSS by validating and sanitizing all user inputs.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-654e7aada501f6fea6718cd59dbed297</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/security/harden-resources.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776996335</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Public IP Addresses">
  <data key="d0">Public IP Addresses</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Public IP addresses are treated as high-risk assets due to their broad accessibility from the internet.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-654e7aada501f6fea6718cd59dbed297</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/security/harden-resources.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776996336</data>
