  <data key="d5">1777004629</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Virtual Networks">
  <data key="d0">Virtual Networks</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Virtual networks provide network-level containment of resources without adding traffic between two virtual networks, created in private address spaces within a subscription.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d7f01a2d2b2e2dfa5053288e4cf9593f</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/security/segmentation.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776996805</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Azure Firewall Manager">
  <data key="d0">Azure Firewall Manager</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Azure Firewall Manager is used to create rules or policies that allow or deny traffic.&lt;SEP&gt;Azure Firewall Manager is an Azure resource related to managing Azure Firewall configurations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d7f01a2d2b2e2dfa5053288e4cf9593f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-11229197c05f5be9b778a1918888ee7a</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/security/segmentation.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-firewall.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001592</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Identity Perimeter">
  <data key="d0">Identity Perimeter</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A security boundary that validates access control at access time, independent of network, focused on least privilege.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b6503993c6e8852f14500c5e6630b88b</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/security/segmentation.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776996806</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Network Perimeter">
  <data key="d0">Network Perimeter</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A security boundary that augments identity controls by controlling blast radius, blocking unsafe access, and obfuscating workload resources.&lt;SEP&gt;The network perimeter establishes the security boundary between an application and other networks, distinguishing ingress, internal, and egress traffic.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b6503993c6e8852f14500c5e6630b88b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-13a9dd5e331588bd7de29586bf88d3b4</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/security/segmentation.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/saas/networking.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777004595</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Demilitarized Zone">
  <data key="d0">Demilitarized Zone</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">DMZs are network zones placed in front of applications with proper controls via firewalls.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b6503993c6e8852f14500c5e6630b88b</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/security/segmentation.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776996806</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Time-Limited Access">
  <data key="d0">Time-Limited Access</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A security practice of limiting access by time to reduce the attack surface, especially applicable to critical accounts.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b6503993c6e8852f14500c5e6630b88b</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/security/segmentation.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776996807</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Black-Box Testing">
  <data key="d0">Black-Box Testing</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A testing methodology that validates externally visible application behavior without knowledge of system internals.&lt;SEP&gt;Black-box testing is a test type where the internals of the system aren't visible to the tester.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-13e4093031cc92a18b0a5ae7643e4edc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5200ddd62ebcb8e16dece7ddc1422557</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/security/test.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776996849</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="White-Box Testing">
  <data key="d0">White-Box Testing</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A testing methodology where the structure of the code is known to the practitioner.&lt;SEP&gt;White-box testing is a test type where the tester has understanding of the application and access to code, logs, resource topology, and configurations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-13e4093031cc92a18b0a5ae7643e4edc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5200ddd62ebcb8e16dece7ddc1422557</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/security/test.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776996849</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Red Team">
  <data key="d0">Red Team</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A team that plays the role of an adversary and attempts to hack the system in a war game exercise.&lt;SEP&gt;The red team is the adversary in war game exercises that attempts to model real-world attacks to find security gaps.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-13e4093031cc92a18b0a5ae7643e4edc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5200ddd62ebcb8e16dece7ddc1422557</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/security/test.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776996850</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Blue Team">
  <data key="d0">Blue Team</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A team that defends against red team attacks in a war game exercise.&lt;SEP&gt;The blue team is the workload team that defends against attacks, testing their ability to detect, respond, and remediate.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-13e4093031cc92a18b0a5ae7643e4edc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5200ddd62ebcb8e16dece7ddc1422557</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/security/test.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776996850</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
