<node id="Hot Paths">
  <data key="d0">Hot Paths</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Hot paths are essential or high-usage sections of a program that require high performance and low latency.&lt;SEP&gt;Hot paths are critical or frequently executed sections of a program that require high performance and low latency and have significant effect on performance and resource usage.&lt;SEP&gt;Hot paths are frequently executed code paths that should be optimized for performance improvements.&lt;SEP&gt;Hot paths are critical or frequently run sections of a program that require high performance and low latency.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cc4aed87d4e81857a842b49249489eb9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-94fc40a37b0b592b4e03905809e7d296&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c232479b618fe94240caca6ac9d17c65&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84440da606fb675f9849ae8355d4e8c5</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/performance-efficiency/maturity-model.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/performance-efficiency/optimize-code-infrastructure.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/cost-optimization/optimize-code-costs.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776998582</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Concurrency">
  <data key="d0">Concurrency</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Concurrency handles multiple tasks by switching between them to improve application efficiency.&lt;SEP&gt;Concurrency is when multiple tasks or processes are performed at once but not necessarily at the exact same time.&lt;SEP&gt;Concurrency is the ability of a system to run multiple tasks or processes, enabling different parts of a program to make progress independently.&lt;SEP&gt;Concurrency is a measure of simultaneous users or actions, a capacity metric for performance targets.&lt;SEP&gt;Concurrency involves using asynchronous processing, multithreading, or multiprocessing to maximize resource utilization and reduce expenses.&lt;SEP&gt;Concurrency is the execution of multiple processes at the same time.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cc4aed87d4e81857a842b49249489eb9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-94fc40a37b0b592b4e03905809e7d296&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e31fdc4d1c3251e67cb0588dd3047a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e4e354767f44f79da40710542ed5310&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-947fd1cb8f3c22420b922791439dc161&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84440da606fb675f9849ae8355d4e8c5</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/performance-efficiency/maturity-model.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/performance-efficiency/optimize-code-infrastructure.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/performance-efficiency/performance-targets.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/cost-optimization/optimize-code-costs.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776998576</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Parallelism">
  <data key="d0">Parallelism</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Parallelism processes multiple tasks simultaneously to improve application efficiency.&lt;SEP&gt;Parallelism is when multiple tasks or processes are performed at the same time.&lt;SEP&gt;Parallelism is the ability of a system to simultaneously trigger multiple tasks or processes on multiple computing resources.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cc4aed87d4e81857a842b49249489eb9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-94fc40a37b0b592b4e03905809e7d296&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e31fdc4d1c3251e67cb0588dd3047a1</data>
