  <data key="d0">Profiling Tools</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Profiling tools identify performance bottlenecks in code by providing insights into execution time and memory usage.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0d20a250447d571c0a31d1f1f211c8db</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/performance-efficiency/performance-test.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776997333</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Test Scenarios">
  <data key="d0">Test Scenarios</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Test scenarios are designed situations or conditions for testing workload performance, emulating realistic user behavior and workload patterns.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0d20a250447d571c0a31d1f1f211c8db</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/performance-efficiency/performance-test.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776997334</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Test Scripts">
  <data key="d0">Test Scripts</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Test scripts automate the execution of defined test scenarios, consisting of sequences of actions, HTTP requests, or interactions with workload APIs or user interfaces.&lt;SEP&gt;Test scripts are configurable scripts with variables and parameters used to simulate real-world scenarios and user behaviors.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0d20a250447d571c0a31d1f1f211c8db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0678a076bfce587ea6972a7bddad9432</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/performance-efficiency/performance-test.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776997334</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Load Test">
  <data key="d0">Load Test</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A performance test that measures system performance under typical and heavy load to determine load tolerance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-03e5fd47376a7e9081496e03cedc06a3</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/performance-efficiency/performance-test.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776997334</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Stress Test">
  <data key="d0">Stress Test</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A performance test that overloads a system until it breaks to determine resilience and robustness.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-03e5fd47376a7e9081496e03cedc06a3</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/performance-efficiency/performance-test.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776997335</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Synthetic Test">
  <data key="d0">Synthetic Test</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A performance test that simulates user requests in an application.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-03e5fd47376a7e9081496e03cedc06a3</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/performance-efficiency/performance-test.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776997335</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Acceptance Criteria">
  <data key="d0">Acceptance Criteria</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Specific performance requirements that a workload needs to meet to be considered acceptable, derived from performance targets.&lt;SEP&gt;Predefined standards and performance goals against which test results are compared.&lt;SEP&gt;Acceptance criteria are standardized, unambiguous conditions that verify work completion through tests.&lt;SEP&gt;Acceptance criteria are clear definitions that workloads must meet to address customer needs and expectations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-03e5fd47376a7e9081496e03cedc06a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0678a076bfce587ea6972a7bddad9432&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c1c379f7c96adb8c669329b1ce128b9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f5f9d8aded9b458939437b400ad44c9</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/performance-efficiency/performance-test.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/operational-excellence/formalize-development-practices.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/operational-excellence/testing.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776998074</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Test Environment">
  <data key="d0">Test Environment</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A test environment is the infrastructure, software, and network configurations set up to closely resemble a production environment for performance testing.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0678a076bfce587ea6972a7bddad9432</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/performance-efficiency/performance-test.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776997335</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Performance Metrics">
  <data key="d0">Performance Metrics</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Performance metrics include response time, throughput, CPU and memory utilization, error rates, and network latency.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0678a076bfce587ea6972a7bddad9432</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/performance-efficiency/performance-test.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776997336</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Baselines">
  <data key="d0">Baselines</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Baselines are reference points of meaningful performance snapshots used for comparing performance results over time.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0678a076bfce587ea6972a7bddad9432</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/performance-efficiency/performance-test.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776997336</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Continuous Testing">
  <data key="d0">Continuous Testing</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Continuous testing involves ongoing monitoring and refinement of workload performance.&lt;SEP&gt;Continuous testing involves ongoing monitoring and refinement of tests to maintain consistent and acceptable performance levels.&lt;SEP&gt;A mindset of ongoing testing before and after launch, regularly reviewing and expanding test suites to maintain long-term quality.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0678a076bfce587ea6972a7bddad9432&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-997b67d2a5ddf57a24dd0380ce47e074&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95f95636f4fc764df073f790408dc229</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/performance-efficiency/performance-test.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/operational-excellence/testing.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776998070</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="CI/CD Pipeline">
  <data key="d0">CI/CD Pipeline</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">CI/CD pipeline is a continuous integration and continuous delivery workflow used for automating software deployment and testing.&lt;SEP&gt;CI/CD pipelines integrate parameter validation to verify critical server parameters match expected configuration before completing deployments.&lt;SEP&gt;CI/CD pipeline is used for phased deployment of Virtual WAN configurations across development, test, and production environments.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-997b67d2a5ddf57a24dd0380ce47e074&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-249415e2f114bf589c9d5262bc0bf6bc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c74dac0959c495ccca3e91ce252cb225</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/performance-efficiency/performance-test.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-database-for-mysql.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-virtual-wan.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777002989</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Rate Limiting Pattern">
  <data key="d0">Rate Limiting Pattern</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The Rate Limiting pattern is an Azure architecture pattern that provides guidance on rate limiting implementations.&lt;SEP&gt;The Rate Limiting pattern is a design pattern for which Azure provides detailed guidance and sample implementation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6b0c9390a87db2dcb2296e0f0d2ad3b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-617354e6d70206a2787d2a92e6998746</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/performance-efficiency/prioritize-critical-flows.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776997381</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Priority Queue Pattern">
  <data key="d0">Priority Queue Pattern</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The Priority Queue pattern is an Azure architecture pattern that provides guidance on implementing priority queue processing.&lt;SEP&gt;The Priority Queue pattern is a design pattern for processing messages based on their priority.&lt;SEP&gt;Priority Queue pattern ensures more important tasks run before less important ones.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6b0c9390a87db2dcb2296e0f0d2ad3b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-617354e6d70206a2787d2a92e6998746&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7faed93ac7c8b81fc0be047d2c1f773f</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/performance-efficiency/prioritize-critical-flows.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/design-guides/background-jobs.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999055</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Noncritical Flows">
  <data key="d0">Noncritical Flows</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Noncritical flows are medium to low priority processes that are not business critical and can tolerate delays.&lt;SEP&gt;Noncritical flows are lower-priority flows that should yield resource usage to critical flows when resources are limited, characterized by low usage, batch processing, and lower latency sensitivity.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-617354e6d70206a2787d2a92e6998746&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ce0ca00b92b2b19805cdb05c0d7a7e45</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/performance-efficiency/prioritize-critical-flows.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776997382</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Logical Segmentation">
  <data key="d0">Logical Segmentation</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Logical segmentation uses virtualization and containerization tools like Docker or Kubernetes to isolate flows at the software level.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-617354e6d70206a2787d2a92e6998746</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/performance-efficiency/prioritize-critical-flows.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776997382</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Capacity Allocation">
  <data key="d0">Capacity Allocation</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Capacity allocation explicitly allocates a fixed set of capacity such as CPU, memory, and disk I/O to critical flows.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-617354e6d70206a2787d2a92e6998746</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/performance-efficiency/prioritize-critical-flows.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776997383</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Priority Queue Processing">
  <data key="d0">Priority Queue Processing</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Priority queue processing is the act of processing high-priority tasks before low-priority tasks.&lt;SEP&gt;Priority queue processing assigns high priority to certain requests in a queue, overriding the default FIFO structure.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-617354e6d70206a2787d2a92e6998746&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ce0ca00b92b2b19805cdb05c0d7a7e45</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/performance-efficiency/prioritize-critical-flows.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776997383</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Docker">
  <data key="d0">Docker</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Docker is a containerization tool used for logical segmentation of flows.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-617354e6d70206a2787d2a92e6998746</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/performance-efficiency/prioritize-critical-flows.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776997383</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Analytic And Tracking Tools">
  <data key="d0">Analytic And Tracking Tools</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Analytic and tracking tools provide insights into user behavior and interactions within an application, helping identify common flows and bottlenecks.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ce0ca00b92b2b19805cdb05c0d7a7e45</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/performance-efficiency/prioritize-critical-flows.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776997385</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Logging And Debugging Tools">
  <data key="d0">Logging And Debugging Tools</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Logging and debugging tools capture and analyze logs and debug information to trace flow execution and identify issues or errors.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ce0ca00b92b2b19805cdb05c0d7a7e45</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/performance-efficiency/prioritize-critical-flows.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776997385</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Self-Prevention">
  <data key="d0">Self-Prevention</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Self-prevention refers to implementations within a workload to prevent potential issues and failures.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-61b04e78e2d91d6d0df8535ea9be2c6c</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/performance-efficiency/respond-live-performance-issues.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776997412</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Data Correlation">
  <data key="d0">Data Correlation</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Data correlation involves aligning logs, metrics, and events from various parts of a workload to pinpoint underlying causes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-61b04e78e2d91d6d0df8535ea9be2c6c</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/performance-efficiency/respond-live-performance-issues.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776997412</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Triage Plan">
  <data key="d0">Triage Plan</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A triage plan is a structured approach to identify, escalate, analyze, prioritize, and communicate live-site performance issues.&lt;SEP&gt;The triage plan is a documented plan including steps, processes, and best practices for responding to performance issues, made accessible to team members.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-61b04e78e2d91d6d0df8535ea9be2c6c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ef6111a0cc4b828fa5b996fe5d89475</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/performance-efficiency/respond-live-performance-issues.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776997412</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Live Performance Issues">
  <data key="d0">Live Performance Issues</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Live performance issues refer to real-time challenges and bottlenecks that can hinder the optimal functioning of a workload.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-61b04e78e2d91d6d0df8535ea9be2c6c</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/performance-efficiency/respond-live-performance-issues.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776997412</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="}Self-Healing And Self-Preservation">
  <data key="d0">}Self-Healing And Self-Preservation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Self-healing and self-preservation are reliability recommendations referenced in related documentation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df125b1159752fb88280cd8ef02f3320</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/performance-efficiency/respond-live-performance-issues.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776997412</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Self-Healing And Self-Preservation">
  <data key="d0">Self-Healing And Self-Preservation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Self-healing and self-preservation are reliability recommendations referenced in related documentation.&lt;SEP&gt;Self-healing and self-preservation are architecture strategies for reliability, including automated actions like using dead-letter queues to handle corrupt messages.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df125b1159752fb88280cd8ef02f3320&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea4e1326f308873031b3e530e46d8478</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/performance-efficiency/respond-live-performance-issues.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/whats-new.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999946</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Vendor Support">
  <data key="d0">Vendor Support</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Vendor support involves engaging product vendors who have expertise, tools, and resources to help fix performance issues, with support level determined by the support agreement.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4ef6111a0cc4b828fa5b996fe5d89475</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/performance-efficiency/respond-live-performance-issues.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776997413</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Communication Plan">
  <data key="d0">Communication Plan</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A communication plan is designed to keep stakeholders informed about the status of performance issues and resolution progress through regular updates, status reports, and clear communication channels.&lt;SEP&gt;A communication plan defines responsibilities across workload teams, support teams, and emergency response personnel, standardizing status updates and external communications.&lt;SEP&gt;An established plan followed by the Communications Lead for stakeholder notifications during incidents.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4ef6111a0cc4b828fa5b996fe5d89475&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b61803d2562ce9d74f9843d45925671b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-059dfb0d1deec0d27f10ad0c5e0f86d2</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/performance-efficiency/respond-live-performance-issues.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/operational-excellence/maturity-model.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/design-guides/disaster-recovery.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999139</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Data Skew">
  <data key="d0">Data Skew</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Data skew occurs when certain partitions receive a disproportionate amount of data or workload, causing performance imbalances.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6542d2e0c59d483460cb92d7687c6c5e</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/performance-efficiency/scale-partition.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776997471</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Infrastructure To Scale">
  <data key="d0">Infrastructure To Scale</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Designing infrastructure to scale is the process of creating an architecture that can handle increasing demands by adding or adjusting resources horizontally or vertically.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4e4f212602314ae8aa68b2a4631872d3</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/performance-efficiency/scale-partition.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776997471</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Singleton">
  <data key="d0">Singleton</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A singleton is a single, centralized resource for an entire workload that can become a bottleneck, reducing scalability and fault tolerance.&lt;SEP&gt;Design pattern ensuring only one instance of a background job runs at a time, even when multiple compute instances are available.&lt;SEP&gt;Singleton approach defines background tasks so that only one instance runs, eliminating resource conflicts but reducing reliability and performance benefits.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4e4f212602314ae8aa68b2a4631872d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0c3ed9c705aa5674f170118b9caf9fbf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7faed93ac7c8b81fc0be047d2c1f773f</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/performance-efficiency/scale-partition.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/design-guides/background-jobs.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fan-Out/Fan-In">
  <data key="d0">Fan-Out/Fan-In</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Fan-out/Fan-in is a design pattern that enables distribution of processing tasks across multiple resources to promote scalability.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4e4f212602314ae8aa68b2a4631872d3</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/performance-efficiency/scale-partition.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776997471</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Decoupling Components">
  <data key="d0">Decoupling Components</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Decoupling application components involves breaking down an application into smaller, independent components that can operate and scale independently.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4e4f212602314ae8aa68b2a4631872d3</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/performance-efficiency/scale-partition.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776997472</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Client Affinity">
  <data key="d0">Client Affinity</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Client affinity is the intentional routing of requests from a single client to a single server instance for consistent session management.&lt;SEP&gt;Client affinity (sticky sessions) routes all requests from a client to the same replica, which can limit scalability and performance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4e4f212602314ae8aa68b2a4631872d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-078fb475b8b7efe4018b64a1ed512f54</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/performance-efficiency/scale-partition.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776997473</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Load Balancing Algorithm">
  <data key="d0">Load Balancing Algorithm</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A load balancing algorithm distributes client requests across multiple back-end instances, but can cause artificial client pinning if misconfigured.&lt;SEP&gt;A load balancing algorithm distributes requests across back-end instances but can cause unintentional client pinning if misconfigured.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4e4f212602314ae8aa68b2a4631872d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b047e8b449398992f0dfab15265a69c0</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/performance-efficiency/scale-partition.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776997473</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Data Locking">
  <data key="d0">Data Locking</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Data locking ensures consistency but has performance disadvantages including lock escalations.&lt;SEP&gt;Data locking is a mechanism used to prevent simultaneous updates to the same data.&lt;SEP&gt;Data locking ensures consistency but has performance disadvantages including lock escalations and negatively affects concurrency, latency, and availability.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4e4f212602314ae8aa68b2a4631872d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-078fb475b8b7efe4018b64a1ed512f54&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b047e8b449398992f0dfab15265a69c0</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/performance-efficiency/scale-partition.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776997473</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Asynchronous Communication">
  <data key="d0">Asynchronous Communication</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Asynchronous communication patterns such as message queues or event-driven architectures decouple components further, allowing independent processing at their own pace.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4e4f212602314ae8aa68b2a4631872d3</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/performance-efficiency/scale-partition.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776997473</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Loose Coupling">
  <data key="d0">Loose Coupling</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Loose coupling is a design principle where components communicate through well-defined interfaces and protocols, reducing dependencies between them.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4e4f212602314ae8aa68b2a4631872d3</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/performance-efficiency/scale-partition.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776997473</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Separation Of Concerns">
  <data key="d0">Separation Of Concerns</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Separation of concerns involves identifying application responsibilities and dividing them into separate components based on specific tasks.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4e4f212602314ae8aa68b2a4631872d3</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/performance-efficiency/scale-partition.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776997474</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Flapping">
  <data key="d0">Flapping</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Flapping is a looping condition where one scale event triggers an opposite scale event, creating continuous back-and-forth scaling action.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c944e9a44adf39c12919b36fa366a12e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b047e8b449398992f0dfab15265a69c0</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/performance-efficiency/scale-partition.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776997474</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Buffer">
  <data key="d0">Buffer</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A buffer is unused capacity used to handle spikes in demand for both horizontal and vertical scaling.&lt;SEP&gt;A buffer is unused capacity reserved to handle unexpected spikes in demand for both horizontal and vertical scaling.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c944e9a44adf39c12919b36fa366a12e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b047e8b449398992f0dfab15265a69c0</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/performance-efficiency/scale-partition.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776997474</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Scaling">
  <data key="d0">Scaling</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Scaling is the ability to increase or decrease resources allocated to a workload based on demand.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-078fb475b8b7efe4018b64a1ed512f54</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/performance-efficiency/scale-partition.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776997475</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="State Affinity">
  <data key="d0">State Affinity</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">State affinity is the storage of client session data on a single server so that the same server handles subsequent requests from the same client.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-078fb475b8b7efe4018b64a1ed512f54</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/performance-efficiency/scale-partition.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776997476</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Scalability">
  <data key="d0">Scalability</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Scalability is the ability of a workload to dynamically change its capacity limits to accommodate varying levels of demand.&lt;SEP&gt;Scalability without effective workload planning increases costs due to incorrect instance sizing and overprovisioning.&lt;SEP&gt;Scalability is a key design objective for applications in Azure VMware Solution, enabling them to handle higher user demands without degradation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-078fb475b8b7efe4018b64a1ed512f54&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06fda024896224771dc739306ae7edcd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a6b0395ca88e97fa04e354f84b77afba</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/performance-efficiency/scale-partition.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-local.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/azure-vmware/application-platform.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777005141</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Client Pinning">
  <data key="d0">Client Pinning</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Client pinning occurs when too many requests are sent to one back-end instance due to load balancing misconfiguration.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b047e8b449398992f0dfab15265a69c0</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/performance-efficiency/scale-partition.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776997476</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Dynamic Service Discovery">
  <data key="d0">Dynamic Service Discovery</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Dynamic service discovery automatically detects and registers services in a distributed system, allowing clients to discover services without tight coupling.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b047e8b449398992f0dfab15265a69c0</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/performance-efficiency/scale-partition.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776997476</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Scaling Boundaries">
  <data key="d0">Scaling Boundaries</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Scaling boundaries include scaling limits, increments, and restrictions that determine a service's ability to handle expected workload.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b047e8b449398992f0dfab15265a69c0</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/performance-efficiency/scale-partition.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776997476</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Load Metrics">
  <data key="d0">Load Metrics</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Load metrics are scaling triggers including CPU, memory, queue depth, SQL queries, custom metrics, and HTTP queue length.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b047e8b449398992f0dfab15265a69c0</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/performance-efficiency/scale-partition.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776997476</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Proxy">
  <data key="d0">Proxy</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A proxy acts as an intermediary between clients and services, distributing and redistributing client connections to avoid direct dependencies on specific instances.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b047e8b449398992f0dfab15265a69c0</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/performance-efficiency/scale-partition.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776997476</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Scaling Limits">
  <data key="d0">Scaling Limits</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Scaling limits are the maximum capacity a location or service can handle, requiring workload partitioning if exceeded.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b047e8b449398992f0dfab15265a69c0</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/performance-efficiency/scale-partition.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776997476</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Scaling Increments">
  <data key="d0">Scaling Increments</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Scaling increments are defined units by which services scale, such as instances, pods, transaction units, or virtual cores.</data>
