<node id="Web Apps And Webjobs">
  <data key="d0">Web Apps And Webjobs</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">WebJobs is a feature of App Service that runs custom jobs as background tasks in a web app, supporting continuous, scheduled, and on-demand execution.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-464884618643684fc043c8a50e0bf16f</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/background-jobs.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999052</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Azurewebjobsstorage">
  <data key="d0">Azurewebjobsstorage</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">AzureWebJobsStorage is a connection string providing access to Storage queues, blobs, tables, and Service Bus for messaging.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-464884618643684fc043c8a50e0bf16f</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/background-jobs.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999052</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Azurewebjobsdashboard">
  <data key="d0">Azurewebjobsdashboard</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">AzureWebJobsDashboard is a connection string providing access to WebJob action log files.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-464884618643684fc043c8a50e0bf16f</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/background-jobs.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999053</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Schedule-Driven Triggers">
  <data key="d0">Schedule-Driven Triggers</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Schedule-driven triggers are timer-based mechanisms that invoke background tasks on a schedule.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1a0f7011b209ee2d53115a29ddfa186f</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/background-jobs.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999053</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Background Task">
  <data key="d0">Background Task</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Background tasks are asynchronous jobs that run in separate processes or locations from the UI or calling process.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1a0f7011b209ee2d53115a29ddfa186f</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/background-jobs.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999053</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Leader Election Pattern">
  <data key="d0">Leader Election Pattern</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Leader Election pattern is an architecture pattern relevant to partitioning background jobs.&lt;SEP&gt;Leader Election pattern is an architecture pattern for coordinating distributed instances.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1a0f7011b209ee2d53115a29ddfa186f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7faed93ac7c8b81fc0be047d2c1f773f</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/background-jobs.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999053</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fire And Forget">
  <data key="d0">Fire And Forget</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Fire and forget describes operations where the calling process doesn't wait for task completion.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1a0f7011b209ee2d53115a29ddfa186f</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/background-jobs.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999053</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Service Bus">
  <data key="d0">Service Bus</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Service Bus provides queues and message sessions for communication with background tasks, supporting state management and ordered delivery.&lt;SEP&gt;Service Bus is an Azure messaging service that supports message sessions, dead letter queues, duplicate removal, and dequeue count properties.&lt;SEP&gt;Service Bus is an Azure service that can provide custom metrics for autoscaling.&lt;SEP&gt;Service Bus is an Azure messaging service comparable to Event Grid and Event Hubs.&lt;SEP&gt;Service Bus is a messaging platform that provides features like geo-replication, dead-letter queues, partitioned entities, and tiered infrastructure for reliable message processing.&lt;SEP&gt;Azure Service Bus is a messaging service that supports queues, topics, subscriptions, and various messaging patterns for cloud applications.&lt;SEP&gt;Azure Service Bus is a cloud messaging service that supports message processing workloads with features like batch processing, session-based messaging, and auto-scaling.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9c1dd7c66529e2ab2455ebd5bb312625&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7faed93ac7c8b81fc0be047d2c1f773f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e0f0a67e7828ea196dc49ba03dc0402e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b0629c90da136e31cc7058b5a6a724b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2cc6dd32c7c960a85b9a30c11bc43659&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-498530bb6096692fd24e326f3f0018c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b49c55356fee42f19cd603d7edc0b8b6</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/background-jobs.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-container-apps.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-event-grid.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-service-bus.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777002483</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Azure Storage Queues">
  <data key="d0">Azure Storage Queues</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Azure Storage queues are a messaging system that supports a dequeue count property for handling repeated and poison messages.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9c1dd7c66529e2ab2455ebd5bb312625</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/background-jobs.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999054</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="WebJobs">
  <data key="d0">WebJobs</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">WebJobs is a feature of App Service for running custom jobs based on scripts or programs within a web app, scaling with its associated Web Apps instance by default.&lt;SEP&gt;WebJobs are tasks hosted by web apps that can be continuous or triggered, requiring the Always On setting for reliable execution.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9c1dd7c66529e2ab2455ebd5bb312625&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e035fd4bdb926eb43402af9b5f4b298b</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/background-jobs.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/app-service-web-apps.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777000133</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Poison Messages">
  <data key="d0">Poison Messages</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Poison messages are messages that repeatedly cause errors during processing.&lt;SEP&gt;Poison messages consistently cause errors in consumers and must be detected and removed from queues.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9c1dd7c66529e2ab2455ebd5bb312625&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7faed93ac7c8b81fc0be047d2c1f773f</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/background-jobs.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999054</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Dead Letter Queue">
  <data key="d0">Dead Letter Queue</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A dead letter queue is an associated queue where poison messages are moved to, either automatically or manually.&lt;SEP&gt;Dead letter queue stores undeliverable events to prevent data loss and supports monitoring with alerts for volume thresholds.&lt;SEP&gt;Dead letter queue accumulates undeliverable events for later troubleshooting and replay.&lt;SEP&gt;DLQ policies prevent message loss without exposing sensitive data as part of security testing scenarios.&lt;SEP&gt;Dead letter queue stores failed messages and requires cleanup procedures to prevent unbounded storage accumulation.&lt;SEP&gt;Dead letter queue is a Service Bus feature that holds undeliverable messages, requiring cleanup automation to prevent resource accumulation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9c1dd7c66529e2ab2455ebd5bb312625&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a84d105f29ba765f53cc6dcefef77ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4d0ea951af59993c2b22cf6dbc254d79&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-653342d517c710c9f67df8e6155d05a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2cc6dd32c7c960a85b9a30c11bc43659&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e70d002685d5e46f8ea08751d291ae2b</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/background-jobs.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-event-grid.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-service-bus.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777002482</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Idempotent Message Processing">
  <data key="d0">Idempotent Message Processing</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Idempotent message processing ensures processing the same message more than once doesn't cause errors or data inconsistency.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9c1dd7c66529e2ab2455ebd5bb312625</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/background-jobs.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999054</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Scheduler Agent Supervisor Pattern">
  <data key="d0">Scheduler Agent Supervisor Pattern</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Scheduler Agent Supervisor pattern manages orchestration of steps for a task and designs reliable multistep processes.&lt;SEP&gt;Scheduler Agent Supervisor pattern is an Azure architecture pattern for coordinating actions across distributed services.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-41b934336b9f0ae48abbe56045f1da0e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7faed93ac7c8b81fc0be047d2c1f773f</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/background-jobs.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999055</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Pessimistic Locking">
  <data key="d0">Pessimistic Locking</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Pessimistic locking is an approach to prevent competing instances from concurrently accessing a service or corrupting data.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7faed93ac7c8b81fc0be047d2c1f773f</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/background-jobs.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999055</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Checkpoints">
  <data key="d0">Checkpoints</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Checkpoints save the state of jobs in persistent storage so tasks can resume from the last known state instead of restarting.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7faed93ac7c8b81fc0be047d2c1f773f</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/background-jobs.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999055</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Resource Contention">
  <data key="d0">Resource Contention</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Resource contention occurs when multiple instances of background jobs compete for access to resources and services, causing availability conflicts and data integrity issues.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7faed93ac7c8b81fc0be047d2c1f773f</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/background-jobs.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999056</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Message Sessions">
  <data key="d0">Message Sessions</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Message sessions in Service Bus enable saving and retrieving application processing state and guaranteeing correct order of message delivery.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7faed93ac7c8b81fc0be047d2c1f773f</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/background-jobs.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999056</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Active-Active At Capacity">
  <data key="d0">Active-Active At Capacity</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An active-active approach where each region handles a share of production load and can scale up to absorb full load during regional failure.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-443aa85fac21ccf603c8d3f8c5b909ec</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/disaster-recovery.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999131</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Active-Active Overprovisioned">
  <data key="d0">Active-Active Overprovisioned</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An active-active approach where each region runs at full scale at all times to independently handle 100% of traffic.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-443aa85fac21ccf603c8d3f8c5b909ec</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/disaster-recovery.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999131</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Failover Runbook">
  <data key="d0">Failover Runbook</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A documented set of manual or orchestrated failover procedures including activation steps, DNS updates, and rollback processes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-443aa85fac21ccf603c8d3f8c5b909ec</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/disaster-recovery.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999131</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tier 0">
  <data key="d0">Tier 0</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Mission-critical tier where downtime isn't an option, requiring SLOs above 99.99%, RTO in seconds, and RPO approaching zero via active-active multi-region deployment.&lt;SEP&gt;Tier 0 (Mission Critical) is the highest criticality tier with active-active deployments and near-zero downtime at the highest cost.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b8efaee6ae92cff9320730dd7f435d7b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fdbffb4e1f51b21a7612a357a00679dd</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/disaster-recovery.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999132</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tier 1">
  <data key="d0">Tier 1</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Business-critical tier tolerating brief disruption, requiring SLOs around 99.95%, RTO and RPO in minutes, using active-active or warm standby deployments.&lt;SEP&gt;Tier 1 (Business Critical) uses warm standby environments with partial-scale compute and auto-scaling to balance cost and recovery.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b8efaee6ae92cff9320730dd7f435d7b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fdbffb4e1f51b21a7612a357a00679dd</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/disaster-recovery.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999132</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tier 2">
  <data key="d0">Tier 2</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Business-operational tier supporting internal teams, targeting SLOs around 99.9%, RTO and RPO in hours, using active-passive warm/cold deployment.&lt;SEP&gt;Tier 2 (Business Operational) uses cold standby setups with pay-as-you-go options like spot instances to optimize costs.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b8efaee6ae92cff9320730dd7f435d7b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fdbffb4e1f51b21a7612a357a00679dd</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/disaster-recovery.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999132</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tier 3">
  <data key="d0">Tier 3</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Administrative tier for non-critical workloads, SLOs below 99.9%, RTO from hours to days, using backup and restore strategy.&lt;SEP&gt;Tier 3 (Administrative) prioritizes cost savings using backup and archival storage with longer recovery windows.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b8efaee6ae92cff9320730dd7f435d7b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fdbffb4e1f51b21a7612a357a00679dd</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/disaster-recovery.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999132</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Disaster Recovery Strategy">
  <data key="d0">Disaster Recovery Strategy</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A strategy for recovering workloads after disruptions, built on RTO and RPO metrics and workload classification by business impact.&lt;SEP&gt;A high-level approach defining goals, principles, and recovery posture for responding to catastrophic failures across workloads.&lt;SEP&gt;A disaster recovery strategy defines how backup, geo-redundancy, and cross-region recovery meet RPO and RTO requirements.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b8efaee6ae92cff9320730dd7f435d7b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ec310894211cb31c51a678d1c91d5a8d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db2648910473195dad09b71af47dc280</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/disaster-recovery.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-database-for-mysql.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777000762</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Backup And Restore">
  <data key="d0">Backup And Restore</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A cost-effective recovery method minimizing ongoing infrastructure costs while preserving recoverability.&lt;SEP&gt;A foundational recovery strategy that must be part of all recovery strategies.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b8efaee6ae92cff9320730dd7f435d7b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-855b48574d32952d11c2de78f45cb1a0</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/disaster-recovery.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999133</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Warm Standby">
  <data key="d0">Warm Standby</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Warm standby is a deployment method where secondary environments are partially ready, balancing resiliency with cost.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b8efaee6ae92cff9320730dd7f435d7b</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/disaster-recovery.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999133</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Load Distribution Behavior">
  <data key="d0">Load Distribution Behavior</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Load distribution behavior refers to configuring how load is distributed across regions, including thresholds, scale-up behavior during failures, and handling of network partitioning.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c5f8dfbcb0c9d573b955de90b96d6926</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/disaster-recovery.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Failover Drill">
  <data key="d0">Failover Drill</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Full or partial failover drills validate actual recovery steps and timings by simulating regional outages.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-855b48574d32952d11c2de78f45cb1a0</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/disaster-recovery.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999137</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Runbook">
  <data key="d0">Runbook</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A document containing detailed recovery steps, target environments, contact lists, and escalation procedures that must be rehearsed regularly.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-855b48574d32952d11c2de78f45cb1a0</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/disaster-recovery.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999137</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cross-Region Data Replication">
  <data key="d0">Cross-Region Data Replication</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A method enabling data synchronization across regions using built-in replication for Azure services and storage.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-855b48574d32952d11c2de78f45cb1a0</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/disaster-recovery.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999138</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="DR Runbook">
  <data key="d0">DR Runbook</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A DR runbook provides step-by-step technical instructions for failover and failback procedures, replacing abstract strategies with actionable structure.&lt;SEP&gt;The DR runbook contains automated and manual steps for disaster recovery execution, treated like production code with version control.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-059dfb0d1deec0d27f10ad0c5e0f86d2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fdbffb4e1f51b21a7612a357a00679dd</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/disaster-recovery.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999138</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="DR Escalation Plan">
  <data key="d0">DR Escalation Plan</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A plan that defines escalation triggers, chain of command, and incident severity levels to ensure rapid alerting of the right people during disaster recovery.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-059dfb0d1deec0d27f10ad0c5e0f86d2</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/disaster-recovery.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999138</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Operations Team">
  <data key="d0">Operations Team</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Team responsible for infrastructure preparation, data integrity, traffic cutover, and monitoring during failover/failback.&lt;SEP&gt;The operations team uses telemetry data for monitoring and is responsible for restoring the telemetry flow after failures.&lt;SEP&gt;The operations team is a stakeholder for User Flow 5: Authentication and Authorization.&lt;SEP&gt;The Operations Team interacts with the AI Data Engineer as part of operations workflows.&lt;SEP&gt;The Operations Team is involved in production monitoring and interacts with multiple personas.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-059dfb0d1deec0d27f10ad0c5e0f86d2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5bf352261b44afadc521c1b327650cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2611a82124cb2d05996b048b560df89&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a945886318d32ffa9cc3299a5304135e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f04c6874b6ce40b7e121a05d7a90d38d</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/disaster-recovery.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/design-guides/optimize-workload-using-flows.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/ai/personas.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777003897</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Incident Manager">
  <data key="d0">Incident Manager</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Person responsible for closing incidents, documenting post-mortems, communicating failback plans, and finalizing recovery.&lt;SEP&gt;The Incident Manager is the authorized person responsible for handling all mitigation actions during an incident.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-059dfb0d1deec0d27f10ad0c5e0f86d2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-87618a32c6b3af420a6fe25de69e11bb</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/disaster-recovery.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/design-guides/incident-management.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999315</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Communications Lead">
  <data key="d0">Communications Lead</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Person responsible for following the Communication Plan to notify stakeholders.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-059dfb0d1deec0d27f10ad0c5e0f86d2</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/disaster-recovery.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999138</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Failover Process">
  <data key="d0">Failover Process</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A 10-step process for disaster recovery failover from primary to secondary infrastructure.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-059dfb0d1deec0d27f10ad0c5e0f86d2</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/disaster-recovery.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999138</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Failback Process">
  <data key="d0">Failback Process</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A 9-step process for returning operations from secondary back to primary region after recovery.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-059dfb0d1deec0d27f10ad0c5e0f86d2</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/disaster-recovery.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999138</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Senior Ops/BCDR Lead">
  <data key="d0">Senior Ops/BCDR Lead</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Person responsible for declaring outages for High and Critical severity incidents during disaster recovery.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-059dfb0d1deec0d27f10ad0c5e0f86d2</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/disaster-recovery.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999139</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Application Owner">
  <data key="d0">Application Owner</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Person responsible for assessing business impact during failback and validating application functionality.&lt;SEP&gt;The individual or team responsible for deployment, configuration, monitoring, and maintenance of applications in Azure VMware Solution.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-059dfb0d1deec0d27f10ad0c5e0f86d2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a6b0395ca88e97fa04e354f84b77afba</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/disaster-recovery.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/azure-vmware/application-platform.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777005141</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Incident Severity Levels">
  <data key="d0">Incident Severity Levels</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A classification system categorizing incidents as Low, Medium, High, or Critical based on impact.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-059dfb0d1deec0d27f10ad0c5e0f86d2</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/disaster-recovery.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999139</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="DR Communication Plan">
  <data key="d0">DR Communication Plan</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A DR communication plan ensures the right information reaches the right people during a disruption, covering activation criteria, roles, channels, and escalation paths.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fdbffb4e1f51b21a7612a357a00679dd</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/disaster-recovery.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999140</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="DR Lead">
  <data key="d0">DR Lead</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The DR lead is the person responsible for formally declaring a disaster and initiating a structured failover process.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fdbffb4e1f51b21a7612a357a00679dd</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/disaster-recovery.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999141</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="DR Activation">
  <data key="d0">DR Activation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The formal decision to initiate disaster recovery procedures, typically requiring executive authorization.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ec310894211cb31c51a678d1c91d5a8d</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/disaster-recovery.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999142</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Transient Fault">
  <data key="d0">Transient Fault</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A temporary failure that is self-correcting and likely to succeed if retried after a suitable delay, including momentary network loss, service unavailability, or timeouts.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c0150f1d7a68414d68f2d36fee4a0389</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/handle-transient-faults.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999209</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Exponential Back-Off">
  <data key="d0">Exponential Back-Off</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Retry strategy where wait time between retry attempts increases exponentially to prevent overwhelming a recovering service.&lt;SEP&gt;A retry strategy where the application exponentially increases the wait time between each subsequent retry attempt.&lt;SEP&gt;Exponential back-off is an interval type used in retry policies where wait times increase exponentially between retries.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c0150f1d7a68414d68f2d36fee4a0389&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3207502b3998d10c15635a0423fbe72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb8602faa58c6ec0ac39453f0668455f</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/handle-transient-faults.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999209</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Retry Policy">
  <data key="d0">Retry Policy</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Combination of all retry strategy elements including detection mechanism, interval type, interval values, and number of retry attempts.&lt;SEP&gt;A policy that defines how retries are performed, including count, interval, and actions after failure.&lt;SEP&gt;A retry policy is a combination of all elements of a retry strategy, defining detection mechanisms, interval types, interval values, and retry counts.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c0150f1d7a68414d68f2d36fee4a0389&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3207502b3998d10c15635a0423fbe72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb8602faa58c6ec0ac39453f0668455f</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/handle-transient-faults.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999209</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Jitter">
  <data key="d0">Jitter</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Random delay added to retry intervals to prevent multiple clients from retrying simultaneously.&lt;SEP&gt;A random delay added to exponential back-off to prevent simultaneous retries from multiple clients.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c0150f1d7a68414d68f2d36fee4a0389&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3207502b3998d10c15635a0423fbe72</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/handle-transient-faults.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999209</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Timeout">
  <data key="d0">Timeout</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Maximum duration to wait for an operation to complete before considering it failed.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c0150f1d7a68414d68f2d36fee4a0389</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/handle-transient-faults.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999210</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Retry Pattern">
  <data key="d0">Retry Pattern</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Azure architecture pattern documentation for implementing retries in application code to handle transient faults.&lt;SEP&gt;A design pattern for handling transient faults by retrying failed operations with configurable intervals and retry counts.&lt;SEP&gt;Retry pattern is a design pattern for handling transient faults by retrying failed operations.&lt;SEP&gt;The Retry pattern is a design pattern used to mitigate failure of external dependencies.&lt;SEP&gt;The Retry pattern handles transient failures from network glitches, dropped database connections, or busy services.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c0150f1d7a68414d68f2d36fee4a0389&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af17a604206f8056ed34d52736aca636&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6fbfe826fe19c233eb2d1b279f7950e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-369fa461119956df38997654d134395c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-568f4a02582f7c3e1a2aba678f415b7c</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/handle-transient-faults.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/app-service-web-apps.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777000123</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cloud Environment">
  <data key="d0">Cloud Environment</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Computing environment using shared resources, redundancy, automatic failover, and dynamic resource allocation, where transient faults are more likely to occur.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c0150f1d7a68414d68f2d36fee4a0389</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/handle-transient-faults.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999210</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Retry Guidance For Azure Services">
  <data key="d0">Retry Guidance For Azure Services</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Azure architecture best practices documentation providing retry guidance specific to Azure services.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c0150f1d7a68414d68f2d36fee4a0389</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/handle-transient-faults.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999210</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Randomization">
  <data key="d0">Randomization</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A technique adding random delays to retry attempts to prevent multiple clients from retrying simultaneously.&lt;SEP&gt;Randomization is a technique used to prevent multiple client instances from sending retry attempts at the same time, often combined with other retry strategies.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-06ce09d2bcc53388fe6e985bfc862f65&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3207502b3998d10c15635a0423fbe72</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/handle-transient-faults.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999210</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Exponential Back-Off With Jitter">
  <data key="d0">Exponential Back-Off With Jitter</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">An exponential back-off with jitter strategy is recommended for background operations, using increasing delays with randomization between retry attempts.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-06ce09d2bcc53388fe6e985bfc862f65</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/handle-transient-faults.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999210</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Retry Strategy">
  <data key="d0">Retry Strategy</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A retry strategy defines how failed operations are retried, including delay intervals, retry counts, and back-off approaches.&lt;SEP&gt;A retry strategy encompasses exception handling, instrumentation, and logging of retry attempts to manage transient and nontransient faults.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-06ce09d2bcc53388fe6e985bfc862f65&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb8602faa58c6ec0ac39453f0668455f</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/handle-transient-faults.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999211</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Chaos Engineering And Fault Injection">
  <data key="d0">Chaos Engineering And Fault Injection</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Chaos engineering and fault injection practices involve purposely introducing transient faults to test retry strategies.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-06ce09d2bcc53388fe6e985bfc862f65</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/handle-transient-faults.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999211</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Regular Interval Retry">
  <data key="d0">Regular Interval Retry</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Regular interval retry is a strategy using fixed intervals between retries, recommended for interactive operations but discouraged for Azure services with high retry counts.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-06ce09d2bcc53388fe6e985bfc862f65</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/handle-transient-faults.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999211</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="HTTP 503 Service Unavailable">
  <data key="d0">HTTP 503 Service Unavailable</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">HTTP code 503 with a Retry-After header indicates how long an error might last or that the service has failed.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-06ce09d2bcc53388fe6e985bfc862f65</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/handle-transient-faults.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999211</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
