<node id="Sharding Pattern">
  <data key="d0">Sharding Pattern</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The Sharding pattern is an Azure architecture pattern providing guidance on horizontal partitioning.&lt;SEP&gt;A data distribution pattern that ensures even distribution of data across partitions using a shard key.&lt;SEP&gt;Sharding pattern is an architectural pattern for partitioning data across multiple databases.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3f5c02d75cf91d6692adb66113df89eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-428d5f718f38d153cbe16fd48c1637de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-103386df6f9acd0832fa49dd897fcede</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/partition-data.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999505</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Azure Table Storage">
  <data key="d0">Azure Table Storage</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A cloud storage service with limits on the volume of requests a single partition can handle.&lt;SEP&gt;Azure Table Storage is a secure shared storage option for agent state persistence.&lt;SEP&gt;Azure Table Storage is a simpler, lower-cost option for read-heavy workloads in batch inference scenarios.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3f5c02d75cf91d6692adb66113df89eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e4237497869de86a9398c01400b4293&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88acce1b2be2296ca1e97b9e9e3c8cad</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/partition-data.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/ai/application-design.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/ai/data-platform.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777003561</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Shard Key">
  <data key="d0">Shard Key</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The data attribute or combination of attributes that determines which shard contains a particular data item, also known as a partition key.&lt;SEP&gt;A key used in horizontal partitioning to ensure even data distribution and efficient query routing.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3f5c02d75cf91d6692adb66113df89eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dbc8280bb50c2a5f4a589617c1bed437</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/partition-data.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999506</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Offline Migration">
  <data key="d0">Offline Migration</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Offline migration involves making partitions unavailable while relocating data to reduce the chance of issues during rebalancing.&lt;SEP&gt;Offline migration is a data migration technique where partitions are made unavailable during data relocation to reduce contention.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5c0f1c2454a3cd326399dd189c571e67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6fcddf7ff1ed7cd474439efcf9fe04d7</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/partition-data.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999507</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Online Migration">
  <data key="d0">Online Migration</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Online migration involves migrating data between partitions while they are still in use.&lt;SEP&gt;Online migration is a data migration technique that is more complex but less disruptive, allowing data access during migration from original and new partitions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5c0f1c2454a3cd326399dd189c571e67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6fcddf7ff1ed7cd474439efcf9fe04d7</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/partition-data.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999507</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Shard Rebalancing">
  <data key="d0">Shard Rebalancing</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Shard rebalancing periodically redistributes data by size and workload to minimize hotspots and maximize query performance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5c0f1c2454a3cd326399dd189c571e67</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/partition-data.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999507</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cross-Partition Joins">
  <data key="d0">Cross-Partition Joins</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Cross-partition joins are inefficient query operations that join data across multiple partitions, typically requiring consecutive queries based on keys and foreign keys.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5c0f1c2454a3cd326399dd189c571e67</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/partition-data.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999507</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Static Reference Data">
  <data key="d0">Static Reference Data</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Static reference data such as postal code tables or product lists can be replicated across all partitions to reduce separate lookup operations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5c0f1c2454a3cd326399dd189c571e67</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/partition-data.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999507</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Referential Integrity">
  <data key="d0">Referential Integrity</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Referential integrity across partitions must be maintained by the application logic rather than the data store in vertical and functional partitioning schemes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5c0f1c2454a3cd326399dd189c571e67</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/partition-data.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999507</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Index Table Pattern">
  <data key="d0">Index Table Pattern</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Index Table pattern is an architectural pattern for efficient data querying.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-428d5f718f38d153cbe16fd48c1637de</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/partition-data.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999508</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Materialized View Pattern">
  <data key="d0">Materialized View Pattern</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Materialized View pattern is an architectural pattern for precomputed query results.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-428d5f718f38d153cbe16fd48c1637de</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/partition-data.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999508</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Data Factory">
  <data key="d0">Data Factory</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Data Factory is an Azure service for data integration and movement.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-428d5f718f38d153cbe16fd48c1637de</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/partition-data.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999508</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="SQL Data Sync">
  <data key="d0">SQL Data Sync</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">SQL Data Sync is a service for replicating the shard map manager database across regions periodically.&lt;SEP&gt;SQL Data Sync is an Azure service for synchronizing data across SQL databases.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-428d5f718f38d153cbe16fd48c1637de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6fcddf7ff1ed7cd474439efcf9fe04d7</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/partition-data.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999508</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Elastic Database Tools">
  <data key="d0">Elastic Database Tools</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Elastic Database tools enable multi-shard queries by sending individual queries to each database and merging results.&lt;SEP&gt;Elastic database tools are Azure SQL Database tools that support multi-shard querying and scaling across partitions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-428d5f718f38d153cbe16fd48c1637de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e47e51139cb584d95de7bb450c2af708</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/partition-data.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999508</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Elastic Pools">
  <data key="d0">Elastic Pools</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Elastic Pools are a feature of Azure SQL Database that allow managing, scaling, and sharing resources across multiple databases within a single resource pool on a single server. They are a cost-effective solution for workloads with varying usage demands, commonly used in SaaS solutions.

Elastic Pools support horizontal scaling by partitioning data into shards spread across multiple databases, reducing contention. They allow dynamic addition and removal of shards as data volume changes. The feature includes vCore-based resource limits and resource governance considerations, helping optimize resource use through flexible scaling capabilities.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-428d5f718f38d153cbe16fd48c1637de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e47e51139cb584d95de7bb450c2af708&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6fcddf7ff1ed7cd474439efcf9fe04d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a363f9bc0a4fd35ef60bc7f3c1bce53&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfe464ce8a55b32a868b7ba10711df9b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c70229b1bcbf083a0dcea43a7ae158bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f7fca38ac08e844d0fae7a5f7036423c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc7b59ec61dfec2c82ddf6c12087f39d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e985b627cce63375e11a2952b871309a</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/partition-data.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-sql-database.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/saas/data.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777004303</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Shardlet">
  <data key="d0">Shardlet</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A shardlet is a dataset within a shard, which can be a single data item or group of items sharing the same shardlet key.&lt;SEP&gt;A shardlet is a unit of data within a shard, which can be range-based or list-based.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e47e51139cb584d95de7bb450c2af708&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6fcddf7ff1ed7cd474439efcf9fe04d7</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/partition-data.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999509</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Shard Map">
  <data key="d0">Shard Map</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A shard map addresses shardlets within shards and organizes data partitioning.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e47e51139cb584d95de7bb450c2af708</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/partition-data.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999509</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Split-Merge Tool">
  <data key="d0">Split-Merge Tool</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">The split-merge tool is an Azure web service that splits or combines shards and migrates data safely.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e47e51139cb584d95de7bb450c2af708</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/partition-data.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999509</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Block Blob">
  <data key="d0">Block Blob</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Block blobs are used for quickly uploading or downloading large volumes of data.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e47e51139cb584d95de7bb450c2af708</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/partition-data.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999510</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Page Blob">
  <data key="d0">Page Blob</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Page blobs are used for random access to parts of data.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e47e51139cb584d95de7bb450c2af708</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/partition-data.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999510</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Multi-Shard Query">
  <data key="d0">Multi-Shard Query</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A multi-shard query sends individual queries to each database and merges the results to work around cross-database join limitations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e47e51139cb584d95de7bb450c2af708</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/partition-data.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999510</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Blob Lease">
  <data key="d0">Blob Lease</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A blob lease is a write lock mechanism used to ensure consistency when write operations span blocks, pages, or blobs.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e47e51139cb584d95de7bb450c2af708</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/partition-data.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999510</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sharding Key">
  <data key="d0">Sharding Key</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The sharding key determines how data is distributed across shards; choosing the right key is critical for even workload distribution.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-103386df6f9acd0832fa49dd897fcede</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/partition-data.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999510</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hot Partitions">
  <data key="d0">Hot Partitions</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Hot partitions are partitions with unbalanced access patterns that can negatively affect performance and availability.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-103386df6f9acd0832fa49dd897fcede</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/partition-data.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999510</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="HBase">
  <data key="d0">HBase</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">HBase is a column-oriented data store suited for vertical partitioning.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-103386df6f9acd0832fa49dd897fcede</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/partition-data.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999510</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Shard Map Manager">
  <data key="d0">Shard Map Manager</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A separate SQL database that acts as a global manager maintaining a list of all shards and shardlets in the system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6fcddf7ff1ed7cd474439efcf9fe04d7</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/partition-data.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999511</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Elastic Database Client Library">
  <data key="d0">Elastic Database Client Library</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A client library for the Elastic Database feature of SQL Database, available for Java and .NET, that handles data routing to appropriate shards.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6fcddf7ff1ed7cd474439efcf9fe04d7</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/partition-data.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999511</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="List Shard Map">
  <data key="d0">List Shard Map</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A sharding scheme that associates a single key with a shardlet, providing isolation by holding each shardlet in its own shard.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6fcddf7ff1ed7cd474439efcf9fe04d7</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/partition-data.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999511</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Range Shard Map">
  <data key="d0">Range Shard Map</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A sharding scheme that associates contiguous key values with a shardlet, less expensive but providing less isolation than list shard maps.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6fcddf7ff1ed7cd474439efcf9fe04d7</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/partition-data.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999511</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Data Partitioning Strategy">
  <data key="d0">Data Partitioning Strategy</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A strategy for designing how data is divided across database and data storage technologies to improve reliability, scalability, performance, security, and availability.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-dbc8280bb50c2a5f4a589617c1bed437</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/partition-data.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999511</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hot Partition">
  <data key="d0">Hot Partition</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A partition that experiences disproportionately high access rates or load, causing performance bottlenecks and availability problems.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-dbc8280bb50c2a5f4a589617c1bed437</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/partition-data.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999511</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Rebalancing">
  <data key="d0">Rebalancing</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The process of redistributing data across partitions to maintain even load distribution and prevent hot partitions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-dbc8280bb50c2a5f4a589617c1bed437</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/partition-data.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999511</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Replica">
  <data key="d0">Replica</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A copy of a partition maintained in a separate location for redundancy and improved read performance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-dbc8280bb50c2a5f4a589617c1bed437</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/partition-data.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999512</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cross-Partition Operation">
  <data key="d0">Cross-Partition Operation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A query or transaction that accesses data across multiple partitions, typically having higher latency and complexity than single-partition operations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-dbc8280bb50c2a5f4a589617c1bed437</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/partition-data.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999512</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Test-Driven Development">
  <data key="d0">Test-Driven Development</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Test-driven development (TDD) is a practice where developers write tests before writing the code that satisfies those tests.&lt;SEP&gt;Test-driven development is a software development approach where tests are written before code implementation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6ac8f606b24c325a1ba25f35deaa3143&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-83c12853dd39d9a9a4beefa63014102d</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/release-engineering-continuous-integration.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999601</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Pytest">
  <data key="d0">Pytest</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Pytest is a popular Python testing framework used to run CI tests.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6ac8f606b24c325a1ba25f35deaa3143</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/release-engineering-continuous-integration.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999602</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="PublishTestResults">
  <data key="d0">PublishTestResults</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">PublishTestResults is an Azure DevOps task that consumes test results and publishes them as an integrated report.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6ac8f606b24c325a1ba25f35deaa3143</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/release-engineering-continuous-integration.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999603</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Microsoft-Hosted Agent">
  <data key="d0">Microsoft-Hosted Agent</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Microsoft-hosted agents are build machines managed by Microsoft that handle maintenance and upgrades automatically.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6ac8f606b24c325a1ba25f35deaa3143</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/release-engineering-continuous-integration.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999603</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Nightly Build">
  <data key="d0">Nightly Build</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A nightly build compiles code and runs larger test suites including integration, UI, or smoke tests on a regular cadence.&lt;SEP&gt;A nightly build is a scheduled build that runs at regular intervals to run longer-running test suites such as integration and UI tests.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6ac8f606b24c325a1ba25f35deaa3143&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb22f500a9418e75c787f58356ea6d23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-83c12853dd39d9a9a4beefa63014102d</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/release-engineering-continuous-integration.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999604</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Release Build">
  <data key="d0">Release Build</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A release build compiles code, runs tests, and also compiles API documentation, compliance reports, and code signing.&lt;SEP&gt;A release build compiles code, runs tests, compiles API documentation, compliance reports, code signing, and produces the golden copy for production deployment.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6ac8f606b24c325a1ba25f35deaa3143&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb22f500a9418e75c787f58356ea6d23</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/release-engineering-continuous-integration.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999604</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Failed Tests">
  <data key="d0">Failed Tests</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Failed tests temporarily block deployment and lead to deeper analysis or refinement of tests or code changes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6ac8f606b24c325a1ba25f35deaa3143</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/release-engineering-continuous-integration.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999605</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Parallel Jobs">
  <data key="d0">Parallel Jobs</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Parallel jobs speed up the build process by running multiple jobs or test suites concurrently.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6ac8f606b24c325a1ba25f35deaa3143</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/release-engineering-continuous-integration.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999608</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Release Pipeline">
  <data key="d0">Release Pipeline</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The release pipeline receives the golden copy from the release build to deploy in the production environment.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eb22f500a9418e75c787f58356ea6d23</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/release-engineering-continuous-integration.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999608</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="CI Pipeline">
  <data key="d0">CI Pipeline</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A CI pipeline is an automated workflow that builds, tests, and validates code changes when developers commit to source control.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-83c12853dd39d9a9a4beefa63014102d</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/release-engineering-continuous-integration.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999608</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Pull Request">
  <data key="d0">Pull Request</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A pull request is a request to merge code changes from one branch into another, triggering the CI pipeline to validate proposed changes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-83c12853dd39d9a9a4beefa63014102d</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/release-engineering-continuous-integration.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999608</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Automated Test">
  <data key="d0">Automated Test</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An automated test is a test that runs automatically as part of the CI pipeline to validate code quality and detect problems without manual intervention.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-83c12853dd39d9a9a4beefa63014102d</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/release-engineering-continuous-integration.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999609</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Build Agent">
  <data key="d0">Build Agent</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A build agent is a self-hosted or cloud-hosted compute resource that runs the tasks and operations within a CI pipeline.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-83c12853dd39d9a9a4beefa63014102d</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/release-engineering-continuous-integration.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999609</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Status Badge">
  <data key="d0">Status Badge</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A status badge is a visual indicator that displays the current state of builds or tests, often shown in repository documentation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-83c12853dd39d9a9a4beefa63014102d</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/design-guides/release-engineering-continuous-integration.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999610</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Architecture Decision Record">
  <data key="d0">Architecture Decision Record</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">An architecture decision record (ADR) is a document that captures all key architectural decisions, including context, justifications, alternatives considered, and implications, serving as an append-only log throughout a workload's lifespan.&lt;SEP&gt;A document that captures context, consequences, justifications, tradeoffs, and rejected options for architectural decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;Architecture Decision Record (ADR) is a document that demonstrates design choices were made deliberately and aligned with requirements.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5102b1a81559c73e0355392894e19a84&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70d263497f5caeee5776927a85b56895&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d5158d59c7d2bc03597e0221545a1a7</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/architect-role/architecture-decision-record.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/architect-role/fundamentals.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/architect-role/ongoing-support.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999754</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Solution Architect">
  <data key="d0">Solution Architect</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A solution architect is a role responsible for creating and maintaining the ADR and helping workload teams establish documentation repositories.&lt;SEP&gt;The solution architect role encompasses responsibilities for aligning architecture with business requirements and delivering adaptable solutions.&lt;SEP&gt;The solution architect role involves balancing competing constraints, disclosing design shortcomings, validating assumptions with proof of concepts, and managing technical debt.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5102b1a81559c73e0355392894e19a84&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20168b92f579ebd286ed6e8413a80537&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8e1b9f43effe85bb4f106ab0a823c7d4</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/architect-role/architecture-decision-record.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/whats-new.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999936</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Architecturally Significant Requirements">
  <data key="d0">Architecturally Significant Requirements</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Architecturally significant requirements are requirements that affect the system's structure, key quality attributes, or are difficult to reverse.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5102b1a81559c73e0355392894e19a84</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/architect-role/architecture-decision-record.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999631</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Workload Documentation Repository">
  <data key="d0">Workload Documentation Repository</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A workload documentation repository is a shared, openly accessible storage location for all workload documentation and assets, serving as a single source of truth.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5102b1a81559c73e0355392894e19a84</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/architect-role/architecture-decision-record.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999631</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Architectural Decision Records Github Organization">
  <data key="d0">Architectural Decision Records Github Organization</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The Architectural Decision Records GitHub organization provides template examples for ADRs at adr.github.io.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5102b1a81559c73e0355392894e19a84</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/architect-role/architecture-decision-record.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999632</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Workload Architecture Design Specification">
  <data key="d0">Workload Architecture Design Specification</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A detailed specification describing design choices supported by architecture diagrams and justifications, addressing functional and nonfunctional choices.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3e0681994766d1adfc077b31464e02a0</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/architect-role/architecture-design-specification.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999648</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Technical Specification">
  <data key="d0">Technical Specification</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A specification describing the technical "how" based on scope and goals, designed for engineering teams as a plan-of-record during implementation.&lt;SEP&gt;A detailed document describing design choices accompanied by diagrams, produced after consensus on business requirements.&lt;SEP&gt;A detailed document describing design choices, accompanied by diagrams.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3e0681994766d1adfc077b31464e02a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c4a8fb08ec746dc096a95ff9e697739&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4c2387b8105af22eb64a3df537a7a6b</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/architect-role/architecture-design-specification.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/architect-role/design-business-requirements.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999689</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Functional Specification">
  <data key="d0">Functional Specification</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A specification describing scope and goals aligned with business requirements that informs the technical specification.&lt;SEP&gt;A document that defines what a workload or feature must achieve and why it matters, including scope, metrics, user flows, compliance requirements, and rollout strategy.&lt;SEP&gt;A document capturing business requirements after gaining consensus from stakeholders.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3e0681994766d1adfc077b31464e02a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c4a8fb08ec746dc096a95ff9e697739&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4c2387b8105af22eb64a3df537a7a6b</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/architect-role/architecture-design-specification.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/architect-role/design-business-requirements.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999689</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Security And Compliance Documentation">
  <data key="d0">Security And Compliance Documentation</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Documentation highlighting security and compliance affordances in the design, including compensating controls when requirements can't be met directly.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3e0681994766d1adfc077b31464e02a0</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/architect-role/architecture-design-specification.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999649</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Architect">
  <data key="d0">Architect</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The architect role is responsible for delivering architecture specifications, collaborating with workload teams, managing technical debt, and mentoring implementation teams.&lt;SEP&gt;Architects are professionals who stay involved with workloads over time, providing design insight, modeling support, improvement recommendations, and review assistance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e98ea85178a5817d9a2ef65e41464e96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d5158d59c7d2bc03597e0221545a1a7</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/architect-role/collaboration.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/architect-role/ongoing-support.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999754</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Golden Paths">
  <data key="d0">Golden Paths</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Golden paths are secure and automated ways to consume platform resources, advocated by architects when collaborating with platform teams.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e98ea85178a5817d9a2ef65e41464e96</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/architect-role/collaboration.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999666</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Architects">
  <data key="d0">Architects</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Architects remain engaged with business stakeholders and technical teams throughout implementation to ensure correct design interpretation and alignment with business goals.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8c4a8fb08ec746dc096a95ff9e697739</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/architect-role/design-business-requirements.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999689</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Architecture Design Specification">
  <data key="d0">Architecture Design Specification</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A specification document for architecture design, referenced as a next step after functional specification.&lt;SEP&gt;A structured packet accompanied by diagrams that meets functional and nonfunctional requirements and includes provisions for routine, ad hoc, and emergency operations.&lt;SEP&gt;Guidance on developing architecture design specifications aligned with business needs and stakeholder goals.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8c4a8fb08ec746dc096a95ff9e697739&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70d263497f5caeee5776927a85b56895&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20168b92f579ebd286ed6e8413a80537</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/architect-role/design-business-requirements.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/architect-role/fundamentals.md&lt;SEP&gt;well-architected/whats-new.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999936</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Evaluate">
  <data key="d0">Evaluate</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A step in the architecture process that tests feasibility, constraints, and trade-offs against defined requirements, focusing on cost, complexity, security, and operational effort.&lt;SEP&gt;The fourth step in the architectural discovery process where options are assessed against requirements.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d4c2387b8105af22eb64a3df537a7a6b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3043a3f717f6c39dced287af093a6db</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/architect-role/design-business-requirements.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999690</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Recommend">
  <data key="d0">Recommend</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A step that proposes technical strategy by turning discovery information into actionable direction aligned with business needs and constraints.&lt;SEP&gt;The fifth step in the architectural discovery process where the architect proposes alternatives that achieve desired outcomes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d4c2387b8105af22eb64a3df537a7a6b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3043a3f717f6c39dced287af093a6db</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/architect-role/design-business-requirements.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999690</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Multi-Region Active-Active Architecture">
  <data key="d0">Multi-Region Active-Active Architecture</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An infrastructure pattern that runs services across multiple regions simultaneously for high availability, with significant cost and complexity implications.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d4c2387b8105af22eb64a3df537a7a6b</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/architect-role/design-business-requirements.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999690</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Order Placement">
  <data key="d0">Order Placement</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A business flow requiring high availability because downtime has direct revenue impact.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d4c2387b8105af22eb64a3df537a7a6b</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/architect-role/design-business-requirements.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999690</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Catalog Browsing">
  <data key="d0">Catalog Browsing</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A business flow that can degrade briefly without critical harm.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d4c2387b8105af22eb64a3df537a7a6b</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/architect-role/design-business-requirements.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999690</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Order History">
  <data key="d0">Order History</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A business flow that can tolerate planned maintenance windows.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d4c2387b8105af22eb64a3df537a7a6b</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/architect-role/design-business-requirements.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999691</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cloud Architect">
  <data key="d0">Cloud Architect</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A cloud architect is a role responsible for creating clarity, making architectural decisions, and aligning technical strategy with business requirements.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b3043a3f717f6c39dced287af093a6db</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/architect-role/design-business-requirements.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999691</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="5-Step Process">
  <data key="d0">5-Step Process</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A 5-step architectural discovery process consisting of Listen, Probe, Clarify, Evaluate, and Recommend steps for gathering context and building shared understanding before designing.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b3043a3f717f6c39dced287af093a6db</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/architect-role/design-business-requirements.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999691</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Listen">
  <data key="d0">Listen</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The first step in the architectural discovery process where the architect captures stakeholder requests, absorbs desired outcomes, and identifies assumptions and solution bias.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b3043a3f717f6c39dced287af093a6db</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/architect-role/design-business-requirements.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999691</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Probe">
  <data key="d0">Probe</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The second step where the architect asks "why" repeatedly to understand motivations, pressures, constraints, and incentives behind business requests.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b3043a3f717f6c39dced287af093a6db</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/architect-role/design-business-requirements.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999691</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Clarify">
  <data key="d0">Clarify</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The third step where requests are translated into concrete outcomes and measurable requirements with business consensus.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b3043a3f717f6c39dced287af093a6db</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/architect-role/design-business-requirements.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999691</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Solution Architect's Fundamentals">
  <data key="d0">Solution Architect's Fundamentals</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A guide providing an overview of the solution architect role and what defines success, recommended for newcomers.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b3043a3f717f6c39dced287af093a6db</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/architect-role/design-business-requirements.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999691</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Business Stakeholders">
  <data key="d0">Business Stakeholders</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Business stakeholders include product owners, business analysts, and domain experts who provide vision, budget constraints, and documented requirements.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b3043a3f717f6c39dced287af093a6db</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/architect-role/design-business-requirements.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999691</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="High-Level System Diagram">
  <data key="d0">High-Level System Diagram</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A high-level system or container diagram provides a broad overview of a workload, decomposing it into main components, relationships, and data flow.&lt;SEP&gt;A high-level system diagram provides a broad overview of a workload, decomposing it into main components, relationships, and data flow, useful for stakeholder communication.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-94ddb1fca24e8f66e1403bf34e0339ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7eb71511d377d5a69b2902c6777be774</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/architect-role/design-diagrams.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999717</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Block Diagram">
  <data key="d0">Block Diagram</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A block diagram breaks down a workload into technology-agnostic functional blocks, ideal for early domain modeling and requirements gathering.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-94ddb1fca24e8f66e1403bf34e0339ed</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/architect-role/design-diagrams.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999717</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Component Diagram">
  <data key="d0">Component Diagram</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A component diagram replaces generic blocks with specific technologies and integration points, serving as a visual bill of materials for architecture.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-94ddb1fca24e8f66e1403bf34e0339ed</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/architect-role/design-diagrams.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999717</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Deployment Diagram">
  <data key="d0">Deployment Diagram</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A deployment diagram shows how infrastructure, COTS software, and custom code are deployed into hosting environments, useful for DevOps planning.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-94ddb1fca24e8f66e1403bf34e0339ed</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/architect-role/design-diagrams.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999717</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Data-Flow Diagram">
  <data key="d0">Data-Flow Diagram</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A data-flow diagram illustrates how data moves, transforms, is stored, and exits a system, supporting governance and performance analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-94ddb1fca24e8f66e1403bf34e0339ed</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/architect-role/design-diagrams.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999717</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sequence Diagram">
  <data key="d0">Sequence Diagram</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A sequence diagram depicts temporal ordering of interactions for a single use case, valuable for API design.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-94ddb1fca24e8f66e1403bf34e0339ed</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/architect-role/design-diagrams.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999717</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="User-Flow Diagram">
  <data key="d0">User-Flow Diagram</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A user-flow diagram shows end-to-end steps a user takes across interfaces and services.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-94ddb1fca24e8f66e1403bf34e0339ed</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/architect-role/design-diagrams.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999717</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Entity-Relationship Diagram">
  <data key="d0">Entity-Relationship Diagram</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">An ERD represents logical or physical data model structure including entities, attributes, keys, and cardinalities.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-94ddb1fca24e8f66e1403bf34e0339ed</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/architect-role/design-diagrams.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999717</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Network Connectivity Diagram">
  <data key="d0">Network Connectivity Diagram</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A network diagram illustrates network infrastructure, segmentation, and failure points, often referenced during security audits and incident response.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-94ddb1fca24e8f66e1403bf34e0339ed</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/architect-role/design-diagrams.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999718</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="State Diagram">
  <data key="d0">State Diagram</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A state diagram shows possible states of a domain object or workflow and the conditions triggering transitions between states.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-94ddb1fca24e8f66e1403bf34e0339ed</data>
  <data key="d4">well-architected/architect-role/design-diagrams.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1776999718</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Flowchart">
