  <data key="d5">1777001277</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Auto-Inflation">
  <data key="d0">Auto-Inflation</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Auto-inflation is an automatic scaling feature for Event Hubs Standard and Premium tier namespaces that removes manual intervention during traffic spikes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bd9ecc5b1e909344bcefaee765852bdf</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-event-hubs.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001278</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Event Processor Host">
  <data key="d0">Event Processor Host</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Event processor host manages partitions automatically and avoids manual assignment for Event Hubs consumers.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bd9ecc5b1e909344bcefaee765852bdf</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-event-hubs.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001278</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Consumer Group">
  <data key="d0">Consumer Group</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Consumer groups are dedicated processing channels configured for downstream services like Stream Analytics and Azure Functions to optimize processing.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bd9ecc5b1e909344bcefaee765852bdf</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-event-hubs.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001278</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="AMQP">
  <data key="d0">AMQP</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">AMQP is a messaging protocol supported by Azure Event Hubs for event streaming.&lt;SEP&gt;AMQP is a protocol used for optimization in Azure Event Hubs communication.&lt;SEP&gt;AMQP (Advanced Message Queuing Protocol) over TLS provides secure messaging with authentication capabilities.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-db510f47656045373bcac562ba7e53ca&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4d10ef40f70919a984cf031275099f00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-653342d517c710c9f67df8e6155d05a8</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-event-hubs.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-service-bus.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777002480</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Publish-Subscribe">
  <data key="d0">Publish-Subscribe</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Publish-subscribe is an architectural pattern applied for optimal performance with Azure Event Hubs.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-db510f47656045373bcac562ba7e53ca</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-event-hubs.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001285</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Stream Analytics">
  <data key="d0">Stream Analytics</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Stream Analytics is a service that provides connectivity integration with Azure Event Hubs.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-db510f47656045373bcac562ba7e53ca</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-event-hubs.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001285</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Geo-Disaster Recovery">
  <data key="d0">Geo-Disaster Recovery</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Geo-disaster recovery enables metadata replication between paired namespaces across regions for Standard and Premium tiers, providing automated failover during regional outages.&lt;SEP&gt;Geo-disaster recovery replicates only namespace metadata without message data, suitable for applications that handle their own data replication.&lt;SEP&gt;Geo-disaster recovery replicates only namespace metadata without message data, suitable for scenarios where applications handle their own data replication.&lt;SEP&gt;Geo-disaster recovery offers lightweight metadata-only protection for applications with custom data replication or acceptable message loss.&lt;SEP&gt;Geo-disaster recovery provides metadata-only automated regional failover for Service Bus when properly configured.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-245038d4b86625c6550cd7ef29cef86c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e33a34bfd8e4ddf2903635a31b614a99&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb40dfedb445538dcaca461fef2278f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e70d002685d5e46f8ea08751d291ae2b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b49c55356fee42f19cd603d7edc0b8b6</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-event-hubs.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-service-bus.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777002476</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Auto-Inflate">
  <data key="d0">Auto-Inflate</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Auto-inflate automatically scales throughput units during traffic spikes in Standard and Premium tiers.&lt;SEP&gt;Auto-inflate automatically scales throughput units for Standard tier namespaces based on peak load projections to prevent ingestion throttling.&lt;SEP&gt;Auto-inflate is a feature that automatically scales throughput units with configurable maximum limits to prevent unexpected cost increases.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-245038d4b86625c6550cd7ef29cef86c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4d10ef40f70919a984cf031275099f00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1bb68bab7c1b474f207b34f9a74328e7</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-event-hubs.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001286</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hash-Based Partition Keys">
  <data key="d0">Hash-Based Partition Keys</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Hash-based partition keys distribute events across partitions to prevent hotspots and enable horizontal scaling.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-245038d4b86625c6550cd7ef29cef86c</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-event-hubs.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001287</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="EventHubsException">
  <data key="d0">EventHubsException</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">EventHubsException is an SDK exception that should be caught through exception handling in consumer isolation scenarios.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-245038d4b86625c6550cd7ef29cef86c</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-event-hubs.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001288</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Capture Feature">
  <data key="d0">Capture Feature</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The capture feature provides durable storage for event replay and recovery scenarios in Azure Event Hubs.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-245038d4b86625c6550cd7ef29cef86c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4d10ef40f70919a984cf031275099f00</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-event-hubs.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001288</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Event Hubs Security Baseline">
  <data key="d0">Event Hubs Security Baseline</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">The Event Hubs security baseline addresses key security domains including encryption, network isolation, and granular access control mechanisms.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-245038d4b86625c6550cd7ef29cef86c</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-event-hubs.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001288</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Standard Tier">
  <data key="d0">Standard Tier</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The Standard tier of Azure Event Hubs offers 99.9% SLA and supports auto-inflate for scaling throughput units.&lt;SEP&gt;Standard tier uses shared infrastructure with variable capacity but lacks dedicated throughput guarantees and auto-scaling capabilities.&lt;SEP&gt;Standard tier of Service Bus offers cost savings for non-production environments but lacks native geographic redundancy features.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4d10ef40f70919a984cf031275099f00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e36fb889991403b16d790b11d406033&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b49c55356fee42f19cd603d7edc0b8b6</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-event-hubs.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-service-bus.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777002483</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Dedicated Tier">
  <data key="d0">Dedicated Tier</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The Dedicated tier offers 99.99% SLA, reserved capacity, and geo-replication for cross-region failover.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4d10ef40f70919a984cf031275099f00</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-event-hubs.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001291</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Azure Data Lake Store">
  <data key="d0">Azure Data Lake Store</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Azure Data Lake Store is a storage destination for Event Hubs Capture data.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1bb68bab7c1b474f207b34f9a74328e7</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-event-hubs.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001292</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Service Health">
  <data key="d0">Service Health</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Service Health notifies users about planned and unplanned maintenance for ExpressRoute circuits.&lt;SEP&gt;Service Health notifies users about planned and unplanned maintenance for ExpressRoute circuits and allows configuring alerts in the Azure portal.&lt;SEP&gt;Service Health provides notifications about planned and unplanned maintenance, outages, and Root Cause Analysis for ExpressRoute circuits.&lt;SEP&gt;Service Health is a category of alerting for Azure Virtual Desktop environments.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a385162f5986599f4f996387e830cb95&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1aa82d5677c4e2d0032fac6a0dce0d32&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-840c9812065b0dae17f67ebda4bb6ea9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5158a76c261c507648b548ec947486da</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-expressroute.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/azure-virtual-desktop/monitoring.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777004902</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ExpressRoute Traffic Collector">
  <data key="d0">ExpressRoute Traffic Collector</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">ExpressRoute Traffic Collector enables sampling of network flows over ExpressRoute circuits, supporting both Private and Microsoft peering with near real-time visibility.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a385162f5986599f4f996387e830cb95</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-expressroute.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001380</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Network Insights">
  <data key="d0">Network Insights</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Network Insights for ExpressRoute visualizes topological maps and health dashboards for configuration status monitoring.&lt;SEP&gt;Network Insights provides a centralized platform to review and analyze ExpressRoute circuits, gateways, connections metrics, health dashboards, and topology views.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a385162f5986599f4f996387e830cb95&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1aa82d5677c4e2d0032fac6a0dce0d32</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-expressroute.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001380</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="BGP">
  <data key="d0">BGP</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">BGP (Border Gateway Protocol) is used for dynamic routing in ExpressRoute, providing automatic route updates and optimal path selection.&lt;SEP&gt;BGP (Border Gateway Protocol) is a dynamic routing protocol used for route advertisement, peering, and failover in network configurations.&lt;SEP&gt;BGP (Border Gateway Protocol) is a routing protocol whose peer status is monitored for routing problems affecting network segmentation.&lt;SEP&gt;BGP (Border Gateway Protocol) is used by on-premises routers to advertise routes to Virtual WAN hubs with expected metrics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a385162f5986599f4f996387e830cb95&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d73a470d503be7e3a2f2ef63b96ec22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-142487c37cb4da71ab0fecf01ad7e10b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c60b5ebd31506f4de3e92091b80399f0</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-expressroute.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-virtual-wan.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777002980</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ExpressRoute Direct">
  <data key="d0">ExpressRoute Direct</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">ExpressRoute Direct allows extending on-premises networks directly into the Microsoft network at a peering location.&lt;SEP&gt;ExpressRoute Direct is an ExpressRoute option that has a monthly port fee including circuit fees for Local and Standard SKU circuits.&lt;SEP&gt;ExpressRoute Direct is a variant of ExpressRoute for which metrics are collected including connectivity diagnostics and performance data.&lt;SEP&gt;ExpressRoute Direct is a connectivity option within Azure ExpressRoute that has metrics collected for diagnosing connectivity problems and understanding performance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a385162f5986599f4f996387e830cb95&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df87985019f98b613c9173ac606646be&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0970265d9d614fdbdc9d79d3bcda0f0b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5abe8178a23baa37e13d7019242d00c</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-expressroute.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001380</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ExpressRoute Gateway">
  <data key="d0">ExpressRoute Gateway</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">ExpressRoute Virtual Network Gateway can automatically receive public IP addresses during deployment, simplifying infrastructure provisioning across multiple virtual networks.&lt;SEP&gt;ExpressRoute gateway is a Virtual WAN component that provides private connectivity and requires diagnostic logging for operational monitoring.&lt;SEP&gt;ExpressRoute gateway is a Virtual WAN component providing dedicated private connectivity, with metrics like BitsInPerSecond and BitsOutPerSecond.&lt;SEP&gt;ExpressRoute gateway is a Virtual WAN component providing private connectivity with configurable capacity.&lt;SEP&gt;ExpressRoute gateway is an Azure resource with throughput metrics for monitoring.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a385162f5986599f4f996387e830cb95&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-771b01fe7618e75e298642ca2a2cb0e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f3f86dbbc0e68845a4cfbd563dcaafe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c74dac0959c495ccca3e91ce252cb225&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-142487c37cb4da71ab0fecf01ad7e10b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-expressroute.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-virtual-wan.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777002977</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Expressroute Direct">
  <data key="d0">Expressroute Direct</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">ExpressRoute Direct is an alternative connection option to ExpressRoute circuits for connecting to Azure.&lt;SEP&gt;ExpressRoute Direct is a resource type to which MACsec encryption is applied for link-layer security.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e6da263826443aba6bd2ff3b90cd482c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-840c9812065b0dae17f67ebda4bb6ea9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-expressroute.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001381</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Expressroute Global Reach">
  <data key="d0">Expressroute Global Reach</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">ExpressRoute Global Reach is a Premium SKU feature that links on-premises networks across different geographical locations directly through the Azure backbone network.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e6da263826443aba6bd2ff3b90cd482c</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-expressroute.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001381</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Site-To-Site VPN">
  <data key="d0">Site-To-Site VPN</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Site-to-site VPN serves as a backup to ExpressRoute private peering, providing an additional layer of redundancy.&lt;SEP&gt;Site-to-site VPN is a backup connectivity option configured as a fallback to ExpressRoute private peering for redundancy.&lt;SEP&gt;Site-to-Site VPN over Microsoft peering serves as a backup to ExpressRoute private peering, providing additional redundancy.&lt;SEP&gt;A VPN connection type suitable for development, testing, and nonproduction scenarios, with each scale unit providing 500 Mbps.&lt;SEP&gt;Site-to-site VPN is a technology providing secure connections between cloud providers or to on-premises networks.&lt;SEP&gt;Site-to-site VPN creates a secure tunnel through the internet between two networks, offering low-cost connectivity across cloud providers and on-premises locations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e6da263826443aba6bd2ff3b90cd482c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1aa82d5677c4e2d0032fac6a0dce0d32&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0970265d9d614fdbdc9d79d3bcda0f0b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8650a6bc1260a7fa43030a811f587363&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51059245c825ad997eabe5a0dc767a97&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4e1a7707a8a1d8d9f53bc71b18814f3</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-expressroute.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-virtual-wan.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/saas/networking.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777004587</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Active-Active Connectivity">
  <data key="d0">Active-Active Connectivity</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Active-active connectivity is a recommended mode for operating both connections of an ExpressRoute circuit to improve high availability and resiliency.&lt;SEP&gt;Active-active connectivity is a configuration for ExpressRoute dedicated circuits that provides higher availability by maintaining active connections between on-premises and Azure.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e6da263826443aba6bd2ff3b90cd482c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0970265d9d614fdbdc9d79d3bcda0f0b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-expressroute.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001382</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Expressroute Circuit SKU">
  <data key="d0">Expressroute Circuit SKU</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">ExpressRoute circuit SKUs (Local, Standard, Premium) provide redundancy through geographic expansion at different scopes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e6da263826443aba6bd2ff3b90cd482c</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-expressroute.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001382</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ExpressRoute Virtual Network Gateway">
  <data key="d0">ExpressRoute Virtual Network Gateway</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">ExpressRoute virtual network gateways pass traffic into a virtual network over private peering, charged at an hourly rate plus circuit cost, with scalable gateway options.&lt;SEP&gt;ExpressRoute Virtual Network Gateway connects on-premises networks to Azure virtual networks and can be upgraded via SKU for higher throughput.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df87985019f98b613c9173ac606646be&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5abe8178a23baa37e13d7019242d00c</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-expressroute.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001383</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Global Reach">
  <data key="d0">Global Reach</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Global Reach is an ExpressRoute feature that links on-premises networks across different geographical locations through the Azure backbone network, providing redundancy.&lt;SEP&gt;Global Reach is an ExpressRoute add-on that incurs additional costs for data transfer between different geographical locations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df87985019f98b613c9173ac606646be&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0970265d9d614fdbdc9d79d3bcda0f0b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-expressroute.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001384</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Power Platform">
  <data key="d0">Power Platform</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Microsoft Power Platform is a low-code application platform that can be configured to work with ExpressRoute.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cc00fa2fd7ff6664f6bb092b239d2134</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-expressroute.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001384</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Bi-Directional Forwarding Detection">
  <data key="d0">Bi-Directional Forwarding Detection</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">BFD is a protocol configured over private and Microsoft Peering for rapid detection of link failures, enabling quicker failover to backup paths.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1aa82d5677c4e2d0032fac6a0dce0d32</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-expressroute.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001384</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ExpressRoute Global Reach">
  <data key="d0">ExpressRoute Global Reach</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">ExpressRoute Global Reach links ExpressRoute circuits together to create a private network between on-premises networks across different geographical locations through the Azure backbone.&lt;SEP&gt;ExpressRoute Global Reach is a feature that links ExpressRoute circuits to create a private network between on-premises networks and Azure regions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1aa82d5677c4e2d0032fac6a0dce0d32&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ea3b54c45d951e732cca09b525690b9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-expressroute.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/sap/design-areas/networking-and-connectivity.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777005955</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Azure Connectivity Toolkit">
  <data key="d0">Azure Connectivity Toolkit</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Azure Connectivity Toolkit is a tool for testing performance across ExpressRoute circuits to understand bandwidth capacity and latency.&lt;SEP&gt;The Azure Connectivity Toolkit is used for reliability testing to ensure network design resilience and ability to withstand failures.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1aa82d5677c4e2d0032fac6a0dce0d32&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0970265d9d614fdbdc9d79d3bcda0f0b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5abe8178a23baa37e13d7019242d00c</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-expressroute.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001385</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Virtual Network Gateway">
  <data key="d0">Virtual Network Gateway</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Virtual Network Gateway is an Azure component used with ExpressRoute that can be configured for zone redundancy, auto-scaling, and regional resiliency.&lt;SEP&gt;Virtual Network Gateways for ExpressRoute can be zone-redundant and scalable, supporting up to 40-Gbps connectivity with auto-scaling.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1aa82d5677c4e2d0032fac6a0dce0d32&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0970265d9d614fdbdc9d79d3bcda0f0b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-expressroute.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001385</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Geo-Redundant Circuits">
  <data key="d0">Geo-Redundant Circuits</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Geo-redundant circuits provide multiple geographically diverse paths between on-premises networks and Azure for disaster recovery and high availability.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1aa82d5677c4e2d0032fac6a0dce0d32</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-expressroute.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001385</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Active-Active Mode">
  <data key="d0">Active-Active Mode</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Active-active mode operates both connections of an ExpressRoute circuit simultaneously for higher availability.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1aa82d5677c4e2d0032fac6a0dce0d32</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-expressroute.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001385</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Zone-Redundant Virtual Network Gateways">
  <data key="d0">Zone-Redundant Virtual Network Gateways</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Zone-redundant virtual network gateways ensure network remains operational even if one availability zone experiences an outage.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1aa82d5677c4e2d0032fac6a0dce0d32</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-expressroute.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001385</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Macsec">
  <data key="d0">Macsec</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Media Access Control Security (MACsec) provides link-layer encryption and integrity protection for ExpressRoute Direct resources, securing protocols like ARP, DHCP, and LACP.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-840c9812065b0dae17f67ebda4bb6ea9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-expressroute.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001386</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ipsec">
  <data key="d0">Ipsec</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Internet Protocol Security (IPsec) provides Layer 3 encryption for ExpressRoute private peering, ensuring secure communication between on-premises networks and Azure virtual networks.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-840c9812065b0dae17f67ebda4bb6ea9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-expressroute.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001386</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Azure Rbac">
  <data key="d0">Azure Rbac</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Azure Role-Based Access Control provides granular access management to control who can manage ExpressRoute resources such as circuits and gateways.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-840c9812065b0dae17f67ebda4bb6ea9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-expressroute.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001386</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Md5 Hash">
  <data key="d0">Md5 Hash</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">MD5 hash is used on ExpressRoute circuits to secure messages between on-premises routers and MSEE routers by verifying data integrity during transit.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-840c9812065b0dae17f67ebda4bb6ea9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-expressroute.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001386</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Azure Security Baseline For Expressroute">
  <data key="d0">Azure Security Baseline For Expressroute</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A security baseline applying guidance from the Microsoft Cloud Security Benchmark version 1.0 to ExpressRoute.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-840c9812065b0dae17f67ebda4bb6ea9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-expressroute.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001386</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Msee Routers">
  <data key="d0">Msee Routers</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">MSEE routers are Microsoft Enterprise Edge routers that communicate with on-premises routers over ExpressRoute circuits.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-840c9812065b0dae17f67ebda4bb6ea9</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-expressroute.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001386</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Bidirectional Forwarding Detection">
  <data key="d0">Bidirectional Forwarding Detection</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Bidirectional Forwarding Detection (BFD) is a protocol configured over ExpressRoute peering for faster failover during link failures.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0970265d9d614fdbdc9d79d3bcda0f0b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-expressroute.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001386</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ExpressRoute Metro">
  <data key="d0">ExpressRoute Metro</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">ExpressRoute Metro refers to circuits configured between multiple peering locations within the same metropolitan area for high resiliency.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0970265d9d614fdbdc9d79d3bcda0f0b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-expressroute.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001386</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ExpressRoute Resiliency Insights">
  <data key="d0">ExpressRoute Resiliency Insights</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">ExpressRoute Resiliency Insights provides a percentage-based resiliency index evaluating ExpressRoute control plane reliability through route resilience, zone-redundant gateways, and validation tests.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0970265d9d614fdbdc9d79d3bcda0f0b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-expressroute.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001387</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ExpressRoute FastPath">
  <data key="d0">ExpressRoute FastPath</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">ExpressRoute FastPath improves data path performance between on-premises networks and virtual network resources by bypassing the gateway.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e5abe8178a23baa37e13d7019242d00c</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-expressroute.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001387</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Scalable Gateways">
  <data key="d0">Scalable Gateways</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Scalable Gateways automatically scale gateway instances to accommodate performance needs, enabling up to 40-Gbps connectivity.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e5abe8178a23baa37e13d7019242d00c</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-expressroute.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001387</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ExpressRoute Circuit">
  <data key="d0">ExpressRoute Circuit</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">ExpressRoute circuits provide bandwidth shared between all connected virtual networks and can be upgraded to meet workload requirements.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e5abe8178a23baa37e13d7019242d00c</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-expressroute.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001387</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ErGwScale SKU">
  <data key="d0">ErGwScale SKU</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">ErGwScale SKU is a gateway SKU that enables up to 40-Gbps connectivity to virtual machines and Private Endpoints within a virtual network.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e5abe8178a23baa37e13d7019242d00c</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-expressroute.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001387</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Azure File Sync">
  <data key="d0">Azure File Sync</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Azure File Sync caches SMB file shares on local Windows servers and tiers infrequently used files to the cloud.&lt;SEP&gt;Azure File Sync backs up on-premises SMB file shares to an Azure file share.&lt;SEP&gt;A value-added service that integrates with Azure Files for file synchronization.&lt;SEP&gt;Azure File Sync takes share-level and file-level snapshots as part of regular usage and can increase Azure Files bills.&lt;SEP&gt;Azure File Sync can be configured with private endpoints to use private DNS zones.&lt;SEP&gt;Azure File Sync enables file synchronization across multicloud and edge environments with centralized Azure management.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5720bc2c35e1af5035855500579a20d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d6949b9609e5ef191a10ce0e8da0a14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba915883bba414ecf1ca82b7ab6c12cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eca94eb8d1a3fa16fe190b0f6eecd280&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8a88ba4349c5c5150df583b3f68ce96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0723b9dd0fc297957da0041030d0b242</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-files.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001472</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="SMB Multichannel">
  <data key="d0">SMB Multichannel</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">SMB Multichannel allows SMB 3.1.1 clients to establish multiple network connections to an SMB Azure file share, increasing throughput and IOPS.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5720bc2c35e1af5035855500579a20d1</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-files.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001472</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Metadata Caching">
  <data key="d0">Metadata Caching</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Metadata caching reduces metadata latency and raises metadata scale limits for SSD SMB file shares.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5720bc2c35e1af5035855500579a20d1</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-files.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001472</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Nconnect">
  <data key="d0">Nconnect</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Nconnect enables using more TCP connections between the client and Azure Files premium service for NFSv4.1 to increase performance at scale.&lt;SEP&gt;Nconnect is a client-side mount option enabling more TCP connections between Linux clients and Azure Files premium service for NFSv4.1.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5720bc2c35e1af5035855500579a20d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0723b9dd0fc297957da0041030d0b242</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-files.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001473</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="SMB">
  <data key="d0">SMB</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">SMB (Server Message Block) is a file sharing protocol supported by Azure Files with specific security requirements.&lt;SEP&gt;Server Message Block (SMB) is a file sharing protocol supported by Azure Files for mounting file shares.&lt;SEP&gt;SMB (Server Message Block) is a network protocol used for file sharing, with versions including SMB 1, SMB 2.1, and SMB 3.1.1.&lt;SEP&gt;Server Message Block is a network file sharing protocol supported by Azure NetApp Files.&lt;SEP&gt;Server Message Block protocol used for file sharing with features like access-based enumeration and nonbrowsable shares.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ba915883bba414ecf1ca82b7ab6c12cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3b291819acd1bfde1eed7819d1f9b371&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8a88ba4349c5c5150df583b3f68ce96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6cc4795580f532b2b4baa2c23d716389&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4057517613635840bc1340d6413748b3</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-files.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-netapp-files.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777002365</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="NFS">
  <data key="d0">NFS</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">NFS (Network File System) is a file sharing protocol supported by Azure Files with different security requirements than SMB.&lt;SEP&gt;Network File System (NFS) is a file sharing protocol supported by Azure Files for mounting file shares.&lt;SEP&gt;Network File System is a distributed file system protocol supported by Azure NetApp Files.&lt;SEP&gt;Network File System protocol that uses export policies to control volume access permissions via IP addresses.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ba915883bba414ecf1ca82b7ab6c12cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8a88ba4349c5c5150df583b3f68ce96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6cc4795580f532b2b4baa2c23d716389&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4057517613635840bc1340d6413748b3</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-files.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-netapp-files.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777002365</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Pay-As-You-Go Model">
  <data key="d0">Pay-As-You-Go Model</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A billing model where users pay only for what they use, with meters tracking capacity and transactions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eca94eb8d1a3fa16fe190b0f6eecd280</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-files.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001484</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Provisioned V1">
  <data key="d0">Provisioned V1</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A billing model where users specify and pay for a certain amount of capacity, IOPS, and throughput up front.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eca94eb8d1a3fa16fe190b0f6eecd280</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-files.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001484</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Transaction Optimized">
  <data key="d0">Transaction Optimized</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An access tier for standard SMB Azure file shares optimized for transaction-heavy workloads.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eca94eb8d1a3fa16fe190b0f6eecd280</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-files.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001485</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hot">
  <data key="d0">Hot</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An access tier for standard SMB Azure file shares with moderate storage and transaction pricing.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eca94eb8d1a3fa16fe190b0f6eecd280</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-files.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001485</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cool">
  <data key="d0">Cool</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An access tier for standard SMB Azure file shares with lower storage prices but higher transaction prices.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eca94eb8d1a3fa16fe190b0f6eecd280</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-files.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001485</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Defender For Storage">
  <data key="d0">Defender For Storage</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A value-added security service that integrates with Azure Files.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eca94eb8d1a3fa16fe190b0f6eecd280</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-files.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001485</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="SMB 3.1.1">
  <data key="d0">SMB 3.1.1</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">SMB 3.1.1 was released with Windows 10 and contains important security and performance updates including AES-256-GCM encryption.&lt;SEP&gt;SMB 3.1.1 is the most recent supported SMB protocol version, featuring important security and performance updates including AES-256-GCM channel encryption.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3b291819acd1bfde1eed7819d1f9b371&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e39ac746628d7273f799262e7cf7dc3</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-files.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001487</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="SMB 1">
  <data key="d0">SMB 1</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">SMB 1 is an outdated, inefficient, and insecure protocol not supported by Azure Files that should be disabled on clients.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3b291819acd1bfde1eed7819d1f9b371</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-files.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001488</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="AES-256-GCM">
  <data key="d0">AES-256-GCM</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">AES-256-GCM is a channel encryption method offering more secure encryption, included in SMB 3.1.1.&lt;SEP&gt;AES-256-GCM is a secure channel encryption algorithm recommended for SMB file share encryption in Azure Files.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3b291819acd1bfde1eed7819d1f9b371&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e39ac746628d7273f799262e7cf7dc3</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-files.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001488</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Recovery Services Vault">
  <data key="d0">Recovery Services Vault</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Recovery Services vault is where Azure Backup copies and stores offsite data for long-term retention.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d8a88ba4349c5c5150df583b3f68ce96</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-files.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001488</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Snapshots">
  <data key="d0">Snapshots</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Snapshots provide point-in-time restore capability to protect against accidental deletion or corruption.&lt;SEP&gt;Snapshots provide stability, scalability, and fast recoverability without affecting performance, serving as the foundation for backup and replication solutions in Azure NetApp Files.&lt;SEP&gt;Snapshots are space-efficient data protection features in Azure NetApp Files providing fast recoverability with short RTO and RPO.&lt;SEP&gt;Snapshots provide space-efficient and cost-efficient restore points in Azure NetApp Files.&lt;SEP&gt;Snapshots provide data-protection and data-management capabilities including single-file restores, volume restores, clones, cross-region replication, and long-term retention.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d8a88ba4349c5c5150df583b3f68ce96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-45ad5e579cc0c475ae93c1c16691c70e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4057517613635840bc1340d6413748b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-818813f6dc34b0174c539777eb18946d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-90d9c66189ea1f2f564261486eac199f</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-files.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-netapp-files.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777002365</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="RA-GRS">
  <data key="d0">RA-GRS</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">RA-GRS (Read-Access Geo-Redundant Storage) is a redundancy configuration that provides read access to data in the secondary region.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d8a88ba4349c5c5150df583b3f68ce96</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-files.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001489</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="RA-GZRS">
  <data key="d0">RA-GZRS</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">RA-GZRS (Read-Access Geo-Zone-Redundant Storage) is a redundancy configuration combining geo-zone redundancy with read access to the secondary region.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d8a88ba4349c5c5150df583b3f68ce96</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-files.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001489</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Windows ACLs">
  <data key="d0">Windows ACLs</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Windows ACLs (Access Control Lists) can be configured for SMB file shares without using storage account keys via the Windows permission model.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d8a88ba4349c5c5150df583b3f68ce96</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-files.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001489</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="NFS Azure File Shares">
  <data key="d0">NFS Azure File Shares</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">NFS Azure file shares support Linux clients using the NFSv4.1 protocol, are only accessible through restricted networks, and are available only for the SSD media tier.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7e39ac746628d7273f799262e7cf7dc3</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-files.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001489</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Kerberos Authentication">
  <data key="d0">Kerberos Authentication</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Kerberos authentication is a security feature not supported by NFS Azure file shares.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7e39ac746628d7273f799262e7cf7dc3</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-files.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001490</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="NFSv4.1">
  <data key="d0">NFSv4.1</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">NFSv4.1 is the protocol used by NFS Azure file shares with support for most features from the 4.1 specification.&lt;SEP&gt;NFSv4.1 is a version of the NFS protocol where the snapshot directory path is hidden by default and file access logs are supported.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7e39ac746628d7273f799262e7cf7dc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6cc4795580f532b2b4baa2c23d716389</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-files.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-netapp-files.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777002366</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Port 2049">
  <data key="d0">Port 2049</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Port 2049 must be opened on clients to communicate with NFS Azure file shares.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7e39ac746628d7273f799262e7cf7dc3</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-files.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001490</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Resource Manager Lock">
  <data key="d0">Resource Manager Lock</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A Resource Manager lock prevents accidental or malicious deletion of a storage account.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7e39ac746628d7273f799262e7cf7dc3</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-files.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001490</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Access Control Lists">
  <data key="d0">Access Control Lists</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Access control lists (ACLs) are a security feature not supported by NFS Azure file shares.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7e39ac746628d7273f799262e7cf7dc3</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-files.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001490</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="SSD File Shares">
  <data key="d0">SSD File Shares</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">SSD (premium) file shares provide high IOPS, fast data transfer speeds, and low latency, and are the only tier available for NFS Azure file shares.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7e39ac746628d7273f799262e7cf7dc3</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-files.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001490</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Azure Firewall Standard">
  <data key="d0">Azure Firewall Standard</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Azure Firewall Standard provides Layer 3 to Layer 7 firewall capabilities with autoscaling up to 30 Gbps.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4b8f25dd2dc2bf1ed1488dc9092be0f6</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-firewall.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Azure Firewall Basic">
  <data key="d0">Azure Firewall Basic</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Azure Firewall Basic is designed for SMB customers requiring up to 250 Mbps of throughput.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4b8f25dd2dc2bf1ed1488dc9092be0f6</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-firewall.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Security Copilot">
  <data key="d0">Security Copilot</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Security Copilot integrates with Azure Firewall to help analysts investigate malicious traffic intercepted by the IDPS feature.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4b8f25dd2dc2bf1ed1488dc9092be0f6</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-firewall.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001587</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="TLS Inspection">
  <data key="d0">TLS Inspection</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">TLS inspection allows Azure Firewall Premium to terminate and inspect TLS connections to detect and mitigate malicious activity in HTTPS.&lt;SEP&gt;TLS inspection terminates and inspects TLS connections to detect and mitigate malicious activity in HTTPS traffic.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4b8f25dd2dc2bf1ed1488dc9092be0f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca3e496d3c1901ebb6e0f0add1d59b5b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-firewall.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001588</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Explicit Proxy">
  <data key="d0">Explicit Proxy</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Explicit proxy enables outbound traffic routing through Azure Firewall's private IP address without requiring user-defined routes.&lt;SEP&gt;Explicit proxy mode allows Azure Firewall to handle outbound web traffic directly via its private IP address without requiring UDRs.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4b8f25dd2dc2bf1ed1488dc9092be0f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca3e496d3c1901ebb6e0f0add1d59b5b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-firewall.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001589</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="DNS Flow Trace Logs">
  <data key="d0">DNS Flow Trace Logs</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">DNS flow trace logs provide end-to-end visibility into DNS traffic and name resolution for troubleshooting and auditing.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4b8f25dd2dc2bf1ed1488dc9092be0f6</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-firewall.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001589</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Top Flows Log">
  <data key="d0">Top Flows Log</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">The top flows log (fat flows log) shows the top connections contributing to the highest throughput through the firewall.&lt;SEP&gt;The top flows log, also known as the fat flows log, shows the top connections contributing to the highest throughput through Azure Firewall.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4b8f25dd2dc2bf1ed1488dc9092be0f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea213cbc3ff82c4333ed2bb24ec62b5d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-firewall.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001591</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="IDPS">
  <data key="d0">IDPS</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The intrusion detection and prevention system (IDPS) is a detection mechanism enabled on Azure Firewall to monitor threats.&lt;SEP&gt;Intrusion Detection and Prevention System provides real-time monitoring and signature-based detection to identify and block malicious network activities.&lt;SEP&gt;IDPS (Intrusion Detection and Prevention System) is a feature of Azure Firewall that intercepts malicious traffic for investigation.&lt;SEP&gt;IDPS (Intrusion Detection and Prevention System) is a feature of Azure Firewall Premium that detects and prevents malicious network activity, with potential performance impact.&lt;SEP&gt;Intrusion detection and prevention system helps detect and prevent network-based attacks and malicious activity.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4b8f25dd2dc2bf1ed1488dc9092be0f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4fa337fdf4c15ddf378bf74ac66c3ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca3e496d3c1901ebb6e0f0add1d59b5b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4dd12866b1c0a9d8fe76b6447e59f118&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c88c271297891fe91c48171c10d75570</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-firewall.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/azure-vmware/security.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777005464</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Azure Security Center">
  <data key="d0">Azure Security Center</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Azure Security Center identifies subnets not protected with a next generation firewall.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-079bbb48956c67b51ccb9053f7fbe162</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-firewall.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001594</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Prescaling">
  <data key="d0">Prescaling</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Prescaling is a capability of Azure Firewall that allows proactive allocation of capacity units for anticipated traffic increases, enabling capacity planning based on metrics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-079bbb48956c67b51ccb9053f7fbe162</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-firewall.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001595</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Azure Firewall Workbook">
  <data key="d0">Azure Firewall Workbook</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Azure Firewall workbook extracts insights from Azure Firewall events, analyzing application and network rules and examining statistics about firewall activities.&lt;SEP&gt;Azure Firewall Workbook is a tool for monitoring common metrics and logs related to firewall devices.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eef9765311dfd798d022450d45e1c01d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-72ea85e904c2617bba6ba490ecb16f6e</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-firewall.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/azure-vmware/monitoring.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777005317</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="KQL Queries">
  <data key="d0">KQL Queries</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">KQL queries are used to quickly analyze and troubleshoot problems using Azure Firewall logs.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eef9765311dfd798d022450d45e1c01d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-firewall.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001596</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Policy Analytics Dashboard">
  <data key="d0">Policy Analytics Dashboard</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">The policy analytics dashboard analyzes the impact of Azure Firewall policies and identifies potential problems such as meeting policy limits and improper rules.&lt;SEP&gt;The policy analytics dashboard is used to identify potential problems in Azure Firewall monitoring data.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eef9765311dfd798d022450d45e1c01d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea213cbc3ff82c4333ed2bb24ec62b5d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-firewall.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001596</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Structured Firewall Logs">
  <data key="d0">Structured Firewall Logs</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Structured firewall logs format log data to be easy to search, filter, and analyze, and are a prerequisite for latest monitoring tools.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eef9765311dfd798d022450d45e1c01d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-firewall.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001597</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Packet Capture">
  <data key="d0">Packet Capture</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Packet capture capabilities capture specific traffic flows with filtering based on protocol, flags, and custom filters for troubleshooting.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eef9765311dfd798d022450d45e1c01d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-firewall.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001597</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="DNAT Rules">
  <data key="d0">DNAT Rules</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">DNAT rules route inbound traffic using FQDNs or private IP addresses, supporting complex hybrid networking scenarios.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eef9765311dfd798d022450d45e1c01d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-firewall.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001597</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Customer-Controlled Maintenance">
  <data key="d0">Customer-Controlled Maintenance</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Customer-controlled maintenance aligns system updates with business requirements by scheduling maintenance during planned downtime windows with minimum 5-hour windows and daily recurrence.&lt;SEP&gt;Customer-controlled maintenance allows defining maintenance windows for VPN and ExpressRoute gateways aligned with low-traffic periods.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eef9765311dfd798d022450d45e1c01d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c74dac0959c495ccca3e91ce252cb225</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-firewall.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-virtual-wan.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777002989</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Change Tracking">
  <data key="d0">Change Tracking</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Change tracking is a preview feature that monitors configuration changes made to Azure Firewall Rule Collection Groups, tracking who made what changes and when.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eef9765311dfd798d022450d45e1c01d</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-firewall.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001598</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Azure Cloud Adoption Framework">
  <data key="d0">Azure Cloud Adoption Framework</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">The Azure Cloud Adoption Framework model assigns Azure policies to management groups like CORP to restrict public IP usage.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e4fa337fdf4c15ddf378bf74ac66c3ea</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-firewall.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001600</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="AzureFirewallSubnet">
  <data key="d0">AzureFirewallSubnet</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">AzureFirewallSubnet is the subnet used by Azure Firewall, which may learn default routes via BGP.&lt;SEP&gt;AzureFirewallSubnet is the required dedicated subnet name for Azure Firewall, recommended with a /26 address space to accommodate scaling.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e4fa337fdf4c15ddf378bf74ac66c3ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4dd12866b1c0a9d8fe76b6447e59f118</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-firewall.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001600</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="AzureFirewallManagementSubnet">
  <data key="d0">AzureFirewallManagementSubnet</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">AzureFirewallManagementSubnet is a /26 address space subnet required for Azure Firewall forced tunneling mode.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e4fa337fdf4c15ddf378bf74ac66c3ea</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-firewall.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001600</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Forced Tunneling">
  <data key="d0">Forced Tunneling</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Forced tunneling is a configuration mode for Azure Firewall that routes all internet-bound traffic to a designated next hop instead of directly to the internet, reducing attack surface.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e4fa337fdf4c15ddf378bf74ac66c3ea</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-firewall.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001600</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="User-Defined Routes">
  <data key="d0">User-Defined Routes</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">User-defined routes (UDRs) are configured to force traffic through Azure Firewall.&lt;SEP&gt;Network routes configured to direct traffic only where needed and drop unnecessary traffic on the Azure NetApp Files delegated subnet.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e4fa337fdf4c15ddf378bf74ac66c3ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6cc4795580f532b2b4baa2c23d716389</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-firewall.md&lt;SEP&gt;/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-netapp-files.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777002366</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="SNAT">
  <data key="d0">SNAT</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Source Network Address Translation (SNAT) port usage should be evaluated before removing IP addresses from Azure Firewall configuration.&lt;SEP&gt;SNAT (Source Network Address Translation) is a mechanism in Azure Firewall that translates source IP addresses, with port exhaustion being a known concern for deployments requiring a minimum of five public IP addresses.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ea213cbc3ff82c4333ed2bb24ec62b5d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4dd12866b1c0a9d8fe76b6447e59f118</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-firewall.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001602</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="FQDN Filtering">
  <data key="d0">FQDN Filtering</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">FQDN filtering in network rules allows administrators to manage domain names instead of IP addresses, with dynamic resolution that auto-updates when IPs change.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ca3e496d3c1901ebb6e0f0add1d59b5b</data>
  <data key="d4">/Users/abossard/Desktop/cxe/well-architected/well-architected/service-guides/azure-firewall.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1777001603</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="FQDN Tags">
  <data key="d0">FQDN Tags</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">FQDN tags represent groups of FQDNs associated with well-known Microsoft services, simplifying network security rule management.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ca3e496d3c1901ebb6e0f0add1d59b5b</data>
